## 18. React Flow Node Types Complete Specification

> Added to provide the React Flow-integrated node type definitions for each of the six canvas element types. Each node registers with React Flow via `nodeTypes`, uses the `Handle` component for connection ports (where applicable), and stores its type-specific data in the node's `data` field.

### 18.1 TextNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/TextNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface TextNodeData {
  content: string;             // Text content, supports {{variable}} interpolation
  fontFamily: string;           // e.g. 'Inter', 'Cairo'
  fontSize: number;            // in px
  fontWeight: number;           // 100-900
  fontStyle: 'normal' | 'italic';
  color: string;                // hex or rgba
  textAlign: 'left' | 'center' | 'right';
  lineHeight: number;           // multiplier (1.2, 1.5, etc.)
  letterSpacing: number;        // in px
  textTransform: 'none' | 'uppercase' | 'lowercase' | 'capitalize';
  textDecoration: 'none' | 'underline' | 'line-through';
  textShadow?: string;          // CSS text-shadow value
  opacity: number;              // 0-1
  backgroundColor?: string;     // optional background
  padding?: number;             // internal padding
  borderRadius?: number;        // border radius
  borderWidth?: number;         // border width
  borderColor?: string;         // border color
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Renders content-editable `<div>` when double-clicked (enters edit mode).
- Shows `{{variable}}` placeholders with highlighted background in edit mode.
- Shows resolved value in preview mode (when canvas is in preview state).
- Resize handles at all 4 corners + 4 edges (via React Flow `NodeResizer`).
- Rotation handle at top (via custom `RotateHandle` component overlay).
- Exits edit mode on blur or Escape, committing content via `updateNode` action.
- Minimum size enforced: 20px width, 20px height.
- Content max length: 1000 characters (enforced in `maxLength` on the contenteditable).

### 18.2 ImageNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/ImageNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface ImageNodeData {
  src: string;                  // URL or base64
  alt: string;                  // accessibility text
  fit: 'contain' | 'cover' | 'fill' | 'none';
  opacity: number;              // 0-1
  borderRadius: number;         // px
  borderWidth: number;           // px
  borderColor: string;           // hex
  objectPosition: string;        // CSS object-position value
  dropShadow?: string;            // CSS box-shadow value
  width: number;                 // px
  height: number;                // px
  maxWidth?: number;
  maxHeight?: number;
  maintainAspectRatio: boolean;
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Drag handle at top for moving (React Flow `Handle` top port).
- Corner resize handles maintain aspect ratio when Shift held (`NodeResizer` `keepAspectRatio`).
- Double-click opens image replacement dialog (file upload or URL input).
- Broken/empty image shows placeholder with `ImageOutlined` icon and "Click to add image" text.
- Image loaded via `next/image` when src is a URL; base64 uses standard `<img>`.

### 18.3 ShapeNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/ShapeNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface ShapeNodeData {
  shapeType: 'rectangle' | 'circle' | 'ellipse' | 'line' | 'triangle' | 'star' | 'hexagon';
  fill: string;                  // fill color
  fillType: 'solid' | 'gradient' | 'none';
  gradient?: {
    type: 'linear' | 'radial';
    angle: number;
    stops: { offset: number; color: string }[];
  };
  strokeColor: string;
  strokeWidth: number;
  strokeStyle: 'solid' | 'dashed' | 'dotted';
  opacity: number;
  cornerRadius: number;         // for rectangle
  width: number;
  height: number;
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Shape is rendered as an inline SVG inside the node container.
- `rectangle`: `<rect>` with `rx`/`ry` = `cornerRadius`.
- `circle`: `<ellipse>` with equal rx/ry (maintains circle on resize unless Shift held).
- `ellipse`: `<ellipse>` with independent rx/ry.
- `line`: `<line>` from top-center to bottom-center (strokeWidth determines thickness).
- `triangle`: `<polygon>` three-point shape.
- `star`: `<polygon>` 5-point star.
- `hexagon`: `<polygon>` 6-point hexagon.
- Gradient fill rendered via SVG `<defs>` + `<linearGradient>` / `<radialGradient>`.
- Stroke dasharray computed from `strokeStyle` ('dashed' = `8 4`, 'dotted' = `2 2`).
- Resize maintains shape proportions for circles (rx === ry always).

### 18.4 QRCodeNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/QRCodeNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface QRCodeNodeData {
  dataBinding: 'static' | 'variable';
  staticValue: string;          // when dataBinding = 'static'
  variableRef: string;          // when dataBinding = 'variable' (ref to cert URL)
  size: number;                 // px (square — width === height)
  errorCorrectionLevel: 'L' | 'M' | 'Q' | 'H';
  fgColor: string;              // foreground color (modules)
  bgColor: string;              // background color
  margin: number;               // quiet zone size (in modules)
  logoImage?: string;           // optional logo in center
  logoSize?: number;            // logo size as % of QR code (0-100)
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Uses `qrcode.react` library (`QRCodeCanvas` or `QRCodeSVG`).
- In edit mode: shows QR code with a semi-transparent overlay and "Double-click to edit" hint.
- Double-click opens QR settings panel (in the property panel sidebar).
- QR code is always square (`width === height`); resize constrains to 1:1 aspect ratio.
- If `logoImage` is set, renders the logo centered with a white background patch.
- Preview mode renders the actual QR with live data from the bound variable.
- Error correction level affects QR density; 'H' is highest redundancy.

### 18.5 SignatureNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/SignatureNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface SignatureNodeData {
  mode: 'placeholder' | 'uploaded' | 'drawn';
  signerName: string;
  signerTitle: string;          // e.g. "Director", "Principal"
  imageSrc?: string;            // when mode = 'uploaded'
  drawnData?: string;           // base64 PNG when mode = 'drawn'
  color: string;                // text color for name/title
  fontScript: string;           // font family for signature text
  fontSize: number;
  showLabel: boolean;           // show "Authorized by" label
  width: number;
  height: number;
}
```

**Behavior:**
- `placeholder` mode: renders a styled text representation with signer name in a cursive/script font, title below in a smaller sans-serif, and a horizontal line above the name (signature line).
- `uploaded` mode: renders the uploaded signature image via `<img>` with the signer name and title overlaid or below.
- `drawn` mode: renders the base64 PNG from `drawnData` (captured from a signature pad).
- `showLabel = true`: shows an "Authorized by" label above the signature block.
- Double-click in `placeholder` mode opens a text editor for `signerName`/`signerTitle`.
- Double-click in `uploaded` mode opens the image replacement dialog.
- Double-click in `drawn` mode opens the signature pad dialog for re-drawing.
- Color applies to text elements only; image/drawn content is rendered as-is.

### 18.6 BackgroundNode

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/BackgroundNode.tsx`

```typescript
interface BackgroundNodeData {
  bgType: 'color' | 'gradient' | 'image' | 'pattern';
  color?: string;               // when bgType = 'color'
  gradient?: {
    direction: 'horizontal' | 'vertical' | 'diagonal' | 'radial';
    colors: string[];           // 2+ colors
  };
  imageSrc?: string;            // when bgType = 'image'
  imageFit: 'cover' | 'contain' | 'tile';
  pattern?: 'dots' | 'grid' | 'lines' | 'waves';
  patternColor?: string;
  patternScale?: number;
  opacity: number;
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Background node is always behind all other nodes (z-index 0 — React Flow `zIndex` prop set to 0).
- Not selectable in normal canvas mode (only via the layers panel — `selectable: false` in node config).
- Covers the entire canvas dimensions (width/height set to canvas width/height).
- `color`: solid background color fill.
- `gradient`: CSS `linear-gradient` / `radial-gradient` based on direction.
- `image`: `background-image` with `background-size` based on `imageFit` ('cover', 'contain', or 'tile' = `background-repeat: repeat`).
- `pattern`: CSS-generated patterns (dots via `radial-gradient`, grid via `linear-gradient` lines, lines via repeating gradients, waves via SVG background).
- Only one background node can exist at a time; adding a new background replaces the existing one (enforced in `addNode` action).

---

## 19. Element Library Palette (Left Panel)

### 19.1 Overview

The `ElementLibraryPalette` is the left-side panel in the canvas editor that provides a palette of draggable element cards. Users drag elements from the palette onto the canvas to create new nodes at the drop position.

### 19.2 Component Specification

**Component:** `ElementLibraryPalette`
**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/ElementLibraryPalette.tsx`

**Layout:**
- Grid layout (2 columns) of draggable element cards.
- Each card contains: an MUI Icon, element name, and a tooltip with description.
- Cards are visually styled as elevated tiles with hover effect.
- Panel is collapsible (toggle via toolbar button).

**Element Cards:**

| Element | Icon | Name | Description Tooltip |
|---------|------|------|---------------------|
| Add Text | `TextFieldsOutlined` | Text | "Add a text box with {{variable}} support" |
| Add Image | `ImageOutlined` | Image | "Add an image from URL or upload" |
| Add Rectangle | `SquareOutlined` | Rectangle | "Add a rectangular shape" |
| Add Circle | `CircleOutlined` | Circle | "Add a circular/elliptical shape" |
| Add Line | `HorizontalRuleOutlined` | Line | "Add a horizontal/vertical line" |
| Add QR Code | `QrCodeOutlined` | QR Code | "Add a QR code with variable data binding" |
| Add Signature | `DrawOutlined` | Signature | "Add a signature block (placeholder, uploaded, or drawn)" |
| Add Background | `WallpaperOutlined` | Background | "Set the certificate background (color, gradient, image, pattern)" |

**Drag Mechanism:**
- Uses HTML5 Drag API (`draggable={true}` on each card).
- `onDragStart`: sets `dataTransfer` with `application/x-canvas-element-type` carrying the element type string.
- `dataTransfer.effectAllowed = 'copy'`.
- Drop on canvas reads the type and calls `addNode(type, dropPosition)`.

**Drop Handling (on `ReactFlow` component):**
- `onDrop` event handler reads `dataTransfer.getData('application/x-canvas-element-type')`.
- Converts screen coordinates to canvas coordinates using `screenToFlowPosition` from React Flow.
- Calls `addNode` with the element type and canvas position.
- Shows a drop indicator (highlighted border) on the canvas during dragover.

**Accessibility:**
- Each card is keyboard-focusable and has `aria-label`.
- Pressing Enter on a focused card adds the element at canvas center.
- `aria-describedby` links to the description tooltip.

---

## 20. Canvas Context Menu

### 20.1 Overview

The `CanvasContextMenu` appears on right-click (`contextMenu` event) on a node or on the canvas background. It provides quick access to common node operations.

### 20.2 Component Specification

**Component:** `CanvasContextMenu`
**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/CanvasContextMenu.tsx`

**Trigger:**
- `onContextMenu` event on a node: opens menu at cursor position, scoped to that node.
- `onContextMenu` event on canvas background: opens menu at cursor position, scoped to canvas (no node).
- Menu position: `{ x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY }` (viewport coordinates).
- Menu state stored in Zustand: `contextMenu: { open: boolean; x: number; y: number; nodeId: string | null }`.

**Implementation:**
- Uses MUI `Menu` component with `anchorReference="anchorPosition"` and `anchorPosition={{ left: x, top: y }}`.
- `open` is controlled by Zustand store `contextMenu.open`.
- `onClose` dispatches `closeContextMenu`.

**Menu Items (General — shown for all nodes):**

| Item | Shortcut | Action | Condition |
|------|----------|--------|-----------|
| Copy | Ctrl+C | `copyNodes([nodeId])` | Always |
| Paste | Ctrl+V | `pasteNodes()` | Clipboard non-empty |
| Duplicate | Ctrl+D | `duplicateNode(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| Delete | Del | `deleteNode(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| — Divider — | | | |
| Bring to Front | Ctrl+Shift+] | `bringToFront(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| Bring Forward | Ctrl+] | `bringForward(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| Send Backward | Ctrl+[ | `sendBackward(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| Send to Back | Ctrl+Shift+[ | `sendToBack(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| — Divider — | | | |
| Lock/Unlock | Ctrl+L | `toggleNodeLock(nodeId)` | Node selected |
| Properties | — | Focus property panel on node | Node selected |

**Menu Items (Element-Type-Specific):**

| Element Type | Additional Items |
|--------------|-----------------|
| QR Code | "Edit QR Settings" — opens QR property panel |
| Signature | "Change Signature Mode" — opens signature mode selector |
| Image | "Replace Image" — opens image replacement dialog |
| Text | "Edit Text" — enters content-edit mode |
| Background | "Edit Background" — opens background property panel |

**Canvas Background Context Menu (no node):**

| Item | Shortcut | Action |
|------|----------|--------|
| Paste | Ctrl+V | `pasteNodes()` |
| Select All | Ctrl+A | `selectAll()` |
| — Divider — | | |
| Toggle Grid | Ctrl+' | `toggleGrid()` |
| Toggle Snap | Ctrl+Shift+' | `toggleSnap()` |
| Fit to Screen | Ctrl+0 | `fitView()` |
| — Divider — | | |
| Zoom In | Ctrl+= | `zoomIn()` |
| Zoom Out | Ctrl+- | `zoomOut()` |

**Styling:**
- Uses `theme.palette.background.paper` for menu background.
- Shortcut text shown as muted `Typography` to the right of each item.
- Icons from MUI for each action (e.g., `ContentCopyOutlined`, `ContentPasteOutlined`, `CopyAllOutlined`, `DeleteOutlined`, etc.).
- Menu auto-closes after item click.

**Accessibility:**
- Keyboard navigable (MUI Menu default behavior).
- `aria-label` on menu container.

---

## 21. Zustand Canvas Store (React Flow Integration)

### 21.1 Overview

The `useCanvasStore` is a Zustand store that manages the React Flow graph state (nodes, edges), clipboard, viewport, grid/snap settings, context menu state, and all canvas actions. It replaces the bespoke interaction-state machine from earlier design sections with React Flow's built-in node/edge change handling.

### 21.2 Store Interface

```typescript
interface CanvasState {
  // === React Flow nodes/edges ===
  nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[];
  edges: Edge[];
  onNodesChange: OnNodesChange;
  onEdgesChange: OnEdgesChange;
  onConnect: OnConnect;

  // === Selection ===
  selectedNodeIds: string[];

  // === Clipboard ===
  clipboard: SerializedNode[];

  // === Viewport ===
  viewport: { x: number; y: number; zoom: number };

  // === Grid ===
  gridSize: number;
  gridVisible: boolean;
  snapEnabled: boolean;

  // === Context menu ===
  contextMenu: { open: boolean; x: number; y: number; nodeId: string | null };

  // === Actions: Node CRUD ===
  addNode: (type: CertElementType, position: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
  updateNode: (id: string, data: Partial<CertNodeData>) => void;
  deleteNode: (id: string) => void;
  duplicateNode: (id: string) => void;

  // === Actions: Selection ===
  selectNodes: (ids: string[]) => void;
  selectAll: () => void;
  clearSelection: () => void;

  // === Actions: Clipboard ===
  copyNodes: (ids: string[]) => void;
  pasteNodes: () => void;

  // === Actions: Viewport ===
  setViewport: (viewport: { x: number; y: number; zoom: number }) => void;
  zoomIn: () => void;
  zoomOut: () => void;
  fitView: () => void;

  // === Actions: Grid & Snap ===
  toggleGrid: () => void;
  toggleSnap: () => void;
  setGridSize: (size: number) => void;

  // === Actions: Context menu ===
  openContextMenu: (x: number, y: number, nodeId: string | null) => void;
  closeContextMenu: () => void;

  // === Actions: Z-index ordering ===
  bringToFront: (id: string) => void;
  bringForward: (id: string) => void;
  sendBackward: (id: string) => void;
  sendToBack: (id: string) => void;

  // === Actions: Lock ===
  toggleNodeLock: (id: string) => void;
}
```

### 21.3 Key Behaviors

**Node Management:**
- `addNode`: Creates a new React Flow node with a UUID, default data for the given type, initial dimensions, and `zIndex` = max existing + 1.
- `updateNode`: Patches the node's `data` field with partial updates (merges, does not replace).
- `deleteNode`: Removes the node from `nodes[]` and clears it from `selectedNodeIds`.
- `duplicateNode`: Creates a deep copy with a new UUID, offset position (+20px x/y), and new zIndex.

**Selection:**
- `selectedNodeIds: string[]` (NOT a Set — per Zustand conventions; see §5 store design).
- `selectNodes` replaces the entire selection array.
- `selectAll` sets `selectedNodeIds` to all node IDs.
- `clearSelection` empties the array.

**Clipboard:**
- `copyNodes` serializes the selected nodes (strips connection edges, keeps data).
- `pasteNodes` deserializes from clipboard, creates new UUIDs, offsets +20px, selecting the pasted nodes.
- Clipboard is session-only (not persisted).

**Viewport:**
- `setViewport` syncs with React Flow's `onMove` / `setViewport` API.
- `zoomIn` / `zoomOut` use React Flow's `zoomIn` / `zoomOut` methods (via `useReactFlow` hook in the component layer).
- `fitView` calls React Flow's `fitView` method.

**Grid & Snap:**
- `gridSize` default: 20px.
- `gridVisible` default: true.
- `snapEnabled` default: true.
- When `snapEnabled`, React Flow's `snapToGrid` prop is set to `(snapEnabled ? gridSize : undefined)`.

**Context Menu:**
- `openContextMenu` sets `{ open: true, x, y, nodeId }`.
- `closeContextMenu` sets `{ open: false, ..., nodeId: null }`.

**Z-index Ordering (Renormalized):**
- `bringToFront`: sets target node zIndex = max + 1, then renormalizes all zIndexes to dense 0..N sequence.
- `sendToBack`: sets target node zIndex = min - 1, then renormalizes.
- `bringForward` / `sendBackward`: swaps zIndex with adjacent node in the z-order.
- Renormalization prevents integer growth/negatives (per §3 design decision).

**Lock:**
- `toggleNodeLock`: toggles `node.locked` (React Flow's built-in lock property).
- Locked nodes are not draggable, not selectable for resize, and not deletable.


---

## Round 4 Enhancement — Production Hardening

### R4.1 React Flow Edge Cases — Z-Index, Zoom Boundaries, Collision Detection, Off-Canvas Indicators

This section addresses production-grade edge case handling for the React Flow canvas that was not covered in earlier design rounds. These edge cases arise from real-world usage patterns where many nodes overlap, users zoom to extremes, or nodes drift off the visible canvas area.

#### R4.1.1 Overlapping Nodes Z-Index Management

React Flow assigns `zIndex` per node and renders them in DOM order. When the user drags a node over another, the dragged node must float to the top via a transient zIndex bump, and settle back to its actual z-index on drag end.

**Implementation approach:**
- The `onNodeDragStart` handler elevates the dragged node's `zIndex` to `max(all zIndexes) + 1000` and sets a `dragging: true` flag.
- The `onNodeDragStop` handler restores the original `zIndex` from the `nodeZOrder` array (see §21.2 renormalization logic).
- A transient visual outline appears on overlapped nodes beneath the dragged node — a `0.5` opacity overlay rectangle matching the underlying node's bounding box, rendered via a React Flow `Panel` component overlay.
- When two nodes fully overlap (overlap area > 90% of either node), a "swap" context menu item appears: "Swap Positions" exchanges `{x, y}` coordinates between both nodes.

**Store additions (`useCanvasStore`):**
```typescript
draggedNodeId: string | null;
overlappedNodeIds: string[]; // node IDs that intersect the dragged node's bounds
setDraggedNode: (id: string | null) => void;
getOverlappingNodes: (id: string) => string[];
```

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/useDragOverlap.ts` — a custom hook subscribed to `useCanvasStore` that uses `useStore` from `@xyflow/react` to read node positions during drag. It computes AABB intersection between the dragged node's `{x, y, width, height}` and all other nodes. Runs via `requestAnimationFrame` for perf (not on every pointer move event).

**Collision detection algorithm:**
```
for each node N in nodes (excluding dragged):
  if AABB_intersect(dragged.bounds, N.bounds):
    overlapArea = computeOverlap(dragged.bounds, N.bounds)
    if overlapArea / min(dragged.area, N.area) > 0.05:
      overlappedNodeIds.push(N.id)
```

The `overlappedNodeIds` array triggers the visual overlay UI in a `CanvasOverlapOverlay` component.

#### R4.1.2 Zoom Boundaries & Navigation

**Zoom limits:**
- **Max zoom**: 4.0 (400%) — set via React Flow's `maxZoom={4}` prop on `<ReactFlow>`.
- **Min zoom**: 0.1 (10%) — set via React Flow's `minZoom={0.1}` prop.
- Default zoom: 1.0 (100%).
- Step: `zoomIn()` increments by 0.2, `zoomOut()` decrements by 0.2.

**Custom zoom logic:**
- Ctrl+Scroll zooms toward the cursor position using React Flow's `zoomTo(zoom, { x, y })` API.
- The `onWheel` handler intercepts `event.ctrlKey` and calls `zoomTo(factor, { x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY })`.
- When `event.deltaY < 0`: `zoom = clamp(currentZoom * 1.2, 0.1, 4.0)`.
- When `event.deltaY > 0`: `zoom = clamp(currentZoom / 1.2, 0.1, 4.0)`.

**Zoom to fit:**
- `fitView()` calls React Flow's built-in `fitView({ padding: 0.2, includeHiddenNodes: false })`.
- Padding of 0.2 ensures 20% margin around the bounding box of all nodes.

**Zoom to selection:**
- `zoomToSelection()` — new store action. Uses `useReactFlow().fitBounds(bounds)` where `bounds` is computed from the union AABB of all `selectedNodeIds`.
- If selection is empty, falls back to `fitView()`.
- Keyboard shortcut: Cmd+Shift+F (Cmd+F is browser find).

**Pan limits / bounds:**
- Optional `translateExtent` prop on `<ReactFlow>`: `{ x: [-2000, 2000], y: [-2000, 2000] }` when "pan bounds" toggle is on.
- The "Pan Bounds" toggle is a toolbar button that stores a `panBoundsEnabled: boolean` in `useCanvasStore`.
- When enabled, the user cannot scroll beyond ±2000px from origin.
- Default: disabled (free pan).

**Store additions:**
```typescript
panBoundsEnabled: boolean;
togglePanBounds: () => void;
zoomToSelection: () => void;
```

#### R4.1.3 Node Collision Detection & Nudge

When drag ends and the dragged node overlaps one or more other nodes by more than 30% area:
- A "Nudge" action appears in the context menu: `nudgeNode(id, direction)`.
- `nudgeNode` moves the node by `gridSize` pixels in the least-overlapping direction.
- The "least-overlapping direction" is computed by checking which of `up`/`down`/`left`/`right` results in the smallest total overlap area after displacement.
- If all directions have the same overlap, default to "right" (+gridSize on x).
- If the nudge would push the node outside canvas bounds, the next-best direction is chosen.
- If no direction clears the overlap, the node remains with a yellow warning outline (CSS opacity 0.5) and a toast: "Node overlaps — adjust manually or use alignment guides."

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/collisionUtils.ts`

```typescript
type Direction = "up" | "down" | "left" | "right";

function computeNudgeDirection(
  draggedNode: Node<CertNodeData>,
  otherNodes: Node<CertNodeData>[],
  gridSize: number,
  canvasBounds: { width: number; height: number },
): Direction | null {
  const directions: Direction[] = ["up", "down", "left", "right"];
  let best: Direction | null = null;
  let bestOverlap = Infinity;

  for (const dir of directions) {
    const candidate = applyDirection(draggedNode, dir, gridSize);
    if (outOfBounds(candidate, canvasBounds)) continue;
    const totalOverlap = sumOverlaps(candidate, otherNodes);
    if (totalOverlap < bestOverlap && totalOverlap < draggedNode.width * draggedNode.height * 0.3) {
      best = dir;
      bestOverlap = totalOverlap;
    }
  }
  return best;
}
```

#### R4.1.4 Off-Canvas Node Indicators (Minimap)

When nodes are off the visible viewport rectangle (due to zoom or pan), their positions are shown in the minimap (see §R4.8 Minimap & Controls). Additionally:
- The edges of the canvas display "off-canvas indicators" — small arrow-shaped badges on the nearest edge of the viewport pointing toward the invisible node.
- Each badge shows the node type icon and is clickable, which triggers `setViewport` to center on that node.
- Badge color uses `theme.palette.primary.main` with opacity 0.7.
- Only rendered for nodes that are currently outside the viewport rectangle (computed via `useStore`, React Flow's store selector).

**Component:** `CanvasOffscreenIndicators`
**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/CanvasOffscreenIndicators.tsx`

```tsx
import { useStore, type ReactFlowState } from "@xyflow/react";
import { Box, IconButton, Tooltip } from "@mui/material";
import { useTheme } from "@mui/material/styles";

const selector = (state: ReactFlowState) => {
  const viewportRect = getViewportRect(state.transform, state.width, state.height);
  return state.nodes.filter((n) => !rectContains(viewportRect, n.positionAbsolute ?? n.position));
};
```

---

### R4.2 Node Resize Handles — 8-Way, Aspect Lock, Snap, Min/Max, Panel Sync

#### R4.2.1 Eight Resize Handles Per Node

Each node receives 8 resize handles rendered as small 8×8px squares at the corners and edge midpoints:
- Corners: `nw`, `ne`, `sw`, `se`
- Edges: `n`, `s`, `e`, `w`

Each handle has a unique CSS cursor:
| Handle | Cursor |
|--------|--------|
| `nw` / `se` | `nwse-resize` |
| `ne` / `sw` | `nesw-resize` |
| `n` / `s` | `ns-resize` |
| `e` / `w` | `ew-resize` |

**Implementation:**
- Uses React Flow's `NodeResizer` component from `@xyflow/react` with `handleStyle` customization.
- `NodeResizer` accepts `minHeight`, `minWidth`, `maxHeight`, `maxWidth` per node.
- The 8 handles are rendered via `NodeResizer`'s default behavior; handle positions are React Flow defaults.
- Each handle gets a `data-handle` attribute with the direction for custom CSS targeting.

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/ResizableNodeWrapper.tsx`

```tsx
import { NodeResizer, NodeProps } from "@xyflow/react";
import { useCanvasStore } from "@/frontend/common/stores/canvasStore";

export function ResizableNodeWrapper({ id, selected, data, children }: ResizableNodeProps) {
  const snapEnabled = useCanvasStore((s) => s.snapEnabled);
  const gridSize = useCanvasStore((s) => s.gridSize);
  const dimensions = nodeDimensions[data.type];

  return (
    <>
      <NodeResizer
        minWidth={dimensions.minWidth}
        minHeight={dimensions.minHeight}
        maxWidth={dimensions.maxWidth}
        maxHeight={dimensions.maxHeight}
        isVisible={selected && !data.locked}
        shouldResize={(event) => {
          if (snapEnabled) {
            // snap to gridSize
            event.dimensions.width = Math.round(event.dimensions.width / gridSize) * gridSize;
            event.dimensions.height = Math.round(event.dimensions.height / gridSize) * gridSize;
          }
        }}
      />
      {children}
    </>
  );
}
```

#### R4.2.2 Aspect Ratio Lock (Shift Key)

When the user holds `Shift` during resize:
- Corner handles (nw/ne/sw/se) lock the aspect ratio to `width / height` at the moment of drag start.
- Edge handles (n/s/e/w) are disabled while Shift is held (they cannot preserve aspect).
- The lock is implemented via `NodeResizer`'s `keepAspectRatio` prop which accepts a boolean.
- `shouldResize` is attached via React Flow's `onResize` callback to check `event.shiftKey` and enforce `keepAspectRatio`.

**Implementation detail:**
- On `onResizeStart`, the store records `resizeStartRatio = node.width / node.height`.
- On `onResize` (while `shiftKey` is true), the handler forces `height = width / resizeStartRatio` (or vice versa depending on which dimension changed more).
- The ratio lock is released when `shiftKey` goes up (pointer-level, not just initial key).

#### R4.2.3 Min/Max Dimensions Per Node Type

Each node type has its own min/max dimension constraints enforced by `NodeResizer`:

| Node Type | Min Width | Min Height | Max Width | Max Height |
|-----------|----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| Text | 30 | 20 | 2000 | 1000 |
| Image | 20 | 20 | 2000 | 2000 |
| Shape | 10 | 10 | 2000 | 2000 |
| QR Code | 40 | 40 | 600 | 600 (square enforced) |
| Signature | 80 | 30 | 600 | 200 |
| Background | page width | page height | page width | page height (matches A4 / custom) |

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodeDimensions.ts`

```typescript
export const nodeDimensions: Record<CertElementType, { minWidth: number; minHeight: number; maxWidth: number; maxHeight: number }> = {
  text: { minWidth: 30, minHeight: 20, maxWidth: 2000, maxHeight: 1000 },
  image: { minWidth: 20, minHeight: 20, maxWidth: 2000, maxHeight: 2000 },
  shape: { minWidth: 10, minHeight: 10, maxWidth: 2000, maxHeight: 2000 },
  qr_code: { minWidth: 40, minHeight: 40, maxWidth: 600, maxHeight: 600 },
  signature: { minWidth: 80, minHeight: 30, maxWidth: 600, maxHeight: 200 },
  background: { minWidth: 595, minHeight: 842, maxWidth: 595, maxHeight: 842 },
};
```

QR Code is special: it enforces a square aspect ratio always (not just on Shift). This is done by setting `maxWidth === maxHeight` and using `keepAspectRatio` permanently.

#### R4.2.4 Resize Handle Visual Feedback

- Default: `8px` square, `theme.palette.primary.main` border, `theme.palette.background.paper` fill, 1px border.
- Hover: handle expands to `10px`, border becomes `2px`, fill becomes `theme.palette.primary.main` with 0.2 opacity.
- Active (during drag): handle stays at `10px`, border becomes `theme.palette.secondary.main`, a tooltip appears showing current dimensions: `W: 120px × H: 80px`.

The visual feedback is implemented via MUI `styled` wrapper around the handle CSS:

```tsx
import { styled } from "@mui/material/styles";

const ResizeHandle = styled("div", { shouldForwardProp: (p) => p !== "$active" && p !== "$snap" })<{
  $active: boolean;
  $snap: boolean;
}>(({ theme, $active, $snap }) => ({
  width: 8,
  height: 8,
  border: `1px solid ${theme.palette.primary.main}`,
  background: theme.palette.background.paper,
  borderRadius: 2,
  transition: "width 0.1s, height 0.1s",
  "&:hover": {
    width: 10,
    height: 10,
    borderWidth: 2,
  },
  ...($active && {
    width: 10,
    height: 10,
    borderColor: theme.palette.secondary.main,
    background: theme.palette.action.hover,
  }),
  ...($snap && {
    borderColor: theme.palette.success.main,
  }),
}));
```

When resize snapping is active (node snaps to grid), the handle ring turns `theme.palette.success.main`.

#### R4.2.5 Resize via Property Panel Sync

When the user manually edits width/height in the property panel (right sidebar), the node resizes in real time:
- The property panel has `Width` and `Height` `TextField`s with `InputAdornment="px"`.
- On `onChange` (debounced 200ms), the store's `updateNode(id, { style: { width, height } })` is called.
- React Flow performs `node.width = newWidth` mutation and the node re-renders.
- If the input violates min/max, the field shows red border with helper text "Min: 30px, Max: 2000px" (for text).
- Changing width/height in property panel bypasses aspect ratio lock (unless "Lock aspect" checkbox is checked in the panel).
- The "Lock aspect" checkbox stores `data.locked = true` temporarily (not the same as node lock) and forces ratio maintenance on subsequent panel edits.

---

### R4.3 Snap-to-Grid System — Configurable, Toggleable, Visual Overlay

#### R4.3.1 Configurable Grid Size

The grid system supports four preset sizes: 4px, 8px, 16px, and 32px. The default is 16px.

**Grid size selector in toolbar:**
- A `ToggleButtonGroup` with four `ToggleButton`s, each labeled with the pixel value.
- `value={gridSize}`, `onChange={(e, newSize) => setGridSize(newSize)}`.
- Only visible when `gridVisible === true` (see toggle below).
- The selector is disabled (greyed out) when `gridVisible === false`.

**Store additions:**
```typescript
gridSize: number; // already exists (§21.2)
setGridSize: (size: number) => void; // already exists
```

Grid sizes are validated to be one of `[4, 8, 16, 32]`. Any other value is rejected with a console warn (using `logger`, not `console.warn`).

#### R4.3.2 Visual Grid Overlay

When `gridVisible === true`, the canvas background shows a grid pattern via CSS background-image:

- **Dots mode** (default): Small 1×1px dots at each grid intersection. CSS: `radial-gradient(circle, rgba(0,0,0,0.2) 1px, transparent 1px)` with `backgroundSize: ${gridSize}px ${gridSize}px`.
- **Lines mode** (toggle): Horizontal + vertical lines at grid intervals. CSS: `linear-gradient(...)` two gradients.

Grid overlay toggle is in the toolbar as a "Background" popover menu:
- `IconButton` with `GridOnOutlined` icon.
- Popover with `RadioGroup` options: "Dots", "Lines", "Cross", "None".
- "None" = `gridVisible = false` (same as toggling off).
- Selection persists in store as `backgroundPattern: "dots" | "lines" | "cross" | "none"`.

#### R4.3.3 Snap to Other Nodes' Edges

Beyond grid snapping, when dragging, the node snaps to the edges (left, right, top, bottom, center x, center y) of other nodes:
- The snap threshold is `gridSize / 2` (e.g., 8px at 16px grid).
- Each snap is announced via a visual guide line (see §R4.10 Alignment Guides).
- Snapping targets include:
  1. Other nodes' edges.
  2. Canvas center (horizontal and vertical centerlines).
  3. Canvas edges (since background node IS the canvas bounds).
- When multiple snap targets are within threshold, the closest wins.

**Algorithm:**
```typescript
function findSnapTargets(
  draggedNode: Node<CertNodeData>,
  allNodes: Node<CertNodeData>[],
  threshold: number,
  canvasBounds: { width: number; height: number },
): { xAxis: number | null; yAxis: number | null } {
  const targets = { xAxis: null as number | null, yAxis: null as number | null };
  let minDistX = threshold;
  let minDistY = threshold;

  // Check other nodes' edges
  for (const other of allNodes) {
    if (other.id === draggedNode.id) continue;
    const left = other.position.x;
    const centerX = other.position.x + (other.measured?.width ?? 0) / 2;
    const right = other.position.x + (other.measured?.width ?? 0);

    for (const candidate of [left, centerX, right]) {
      const dist = Math.abs(draggedCandidateX(draggedNode) - candidate);
      if (dist < minDistX) { minDistX = dist; targets.xAxis = candidate; }
    }
  }

  // Canvas center
  const canvasCenterX = canvasBounds.width / 2;
  const distToCenterX = Math.abs(draggedNode.position.x + (draggedNode.measured?.width ?? 0) / 2 - canvasCenterX);
  if (distToCenterX < minDistX) { minDistX = distToCenterX; targets.xAxis = canvasCenterX; }

  // Repeat for Y
  // ...

  return targets;
}
```

Snap targets are visualized with guide lines from the dragged node to the snap target. On drag end, the node's position is set to the snapped coordinates (x or y as appropriate).

---

### R4.4 Multi-Select & Box Selection — Rubber Band, Shift+Click, Select All, Multi-Drag

#### R4.4.1 Rubber Band Selection

When the user starts a pointer-down on the canvas background (not on a node) and drags:
- A rubber band rectangle appears with semi-transparent fill: `theme.palette.primary.main` with 0.1 opacity, 1px border `theme.palette.primary.main`.
- All nodes whose AABB intersects the rubber band rectangle become selected.
- Intersection is computed on pointermove (throttled to 16ms).
- The selection is finalized on pointer-up. The `selectedNodeIds` array is updated with the union of existing selection (if shiftKey) or replace (if no shift).

**React Flow integration:**
- React Flow's built-in `selectionOnDrag` prop handles this natively.
- Set `<ReactFlow selectionOnDrag>` to enable rubber band selection by drag (rather than needing keyboard hold).
- The `panOnDrag` prop is set to `[1, 2]` (middle/right mouse for pan, left mouse for selection).

#### R4.4.2 Shift+Click Add/Remove from Selection

When the user shift+clicks a node:
- If the node is already in `selectedNodeIds`, it is removed.
- If the node is not in `selectedNodeIds`, it is added (union).
- This matches the behavior from §21.2 but is implemented via React Flow's `onNodeClick` handler.

```tsx
const onNodeClick = useCallback((event: React.MouseEvent, node: Node) => {
  if (event.shiftKey) {
    const current = useCanvasStore.getState().selectedNodeIds;
    const isSelected = current.includes(node.id);
    useCanvasStore.getState().selectNodes(
      isSelected ? current.filter((id) => id !== node.id) : [...current, node.id]
    );
  } else {
    useCanvasStore.getState().selectNodes([node.id]);
  }
}, []);
```

#### R4.4.3 Select All (Cmd+A / Ctrl+A)

When the user presses Cmd/Ctrl+A:
- All node IDs become selected via `selectAll()` (already defined in §21.2).
- If the focus is in an input (property panel or text editing), Cmd+A does NOT select all nodes — it performs the default browser select-all in the input.
- Focus detection: `document.activeElement` is checked; if it's `input`/`textarea`/`[contenteditable]`, the event is not prevented.
- The keyboard handler is `useKeyboardShortcuts` hook (see §R4.10.3).

#### R4.4.4 Selection Bounds Display

When 2 or more nodes are selected, a blue bounding rectangle appears around the union of their AABBs:
- Border: 1px dashed `theme.palette.primary.main`.
- Fill: `none` (transparent).
- Label in top-left corner: `N selected` where N is the count, rendered as a small `Chip` with `size="small"`.
- The bounds rectangle also shows the total width and height in the label: `3 selected · 480 × 320px`.

#### R4.4.5 Multi-Drag Move

When the user drags any selected node:
- All selected nodes move by the same delta `{dx, dy}`.
- React Flow handles this natively when multiple nodes are in its `selected` state.
- Snapping still applies but only to the dragged node's anchor (top-left of the bounding rect of selection).
- The selection bounds rectangle follows.

#### R4.4.6 Multi-Delete

Pressing `Delete`/`Backspace` when multiple nodes are selected deletes all of them:
- One batch undo entry (see §R4.9.5).
- Store iterates `selectedNodeIds` and calls `deleteNode` for each, but wraps them in a history batch.

#### R4.4.7 Multi-Align

An alignment toolbar appears when 2+ nodes are selected (positioned in the RightSidebar above the property panel or as a floating toolbar above the canvas):
- Align Left: set all selected nodes' `x` to `min(selectedNodes x)`.
- Align Right: set all selected nodes' `x + width` to `max(selectedNodes right)`.
- Align Center Horizontally: set all selected nodes' `x` so their horizontal center matches the average center.
- Align Top: set all selected nodes' `y` to `min(selectedNodes y)`.
- Align Bottom: set all selected nodes' `y + height` to `max(selectedNodes bottom)`.
- Align Center Vertically: set all selected nodes' `y` so their vertical center matches the average center.
- Distribute Horizontally: equal space between adjacent nodes horizontally.
- Distribute Vertically: equal space between adjacent nodes vertically.

**Component:** `AlignmentToolbar`
**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/AlignmentToolbar.tsx`

Each alignment button is a `Tooltip`-"label"` + `IconButton`. Tooltip text is localized via the i18n system (not hardcoded).

#### R4.4.8 Alignment Algorithms

```typescript
function alignLeft(nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[]): Node<CertNodeData>[] {
  const minX = Math.min(...nodes.map((n) => n.position.x));
  return nodes.map((n) => ({ ...n, position: { ...n.position, x: minX } }));
}

function alignCenterH(nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[]): Node<CertNodeData>[] {
  const avgCenter = avg(nodes.map((n) => n.position.x + (n.measured?.width ?? 0) / 2));
  return nodes.map((n) => ({ ...n, position: { ...n.position, x: avgCenter - (n.measured?.width ?? 0) / 2 } }));
}

function distributeH(nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[]): Node<CertNodeData>[] {
  const sorted = [...nodes].sort((a, b) => a.position.x - b.position.x);
  const totalWidth = sum(sorted.map((n) => n.measured?.width ?? 0));
  const totalRange = right(sorted[sorted.length - 1]) - sorted[0].position.x;
  const gap = (totalRange - totalWidth) / (sorted.length - 1);
  let cursor = sorted[0].position.x;
  return sorted.map((n, i) => {
    const node = { ...n, position: { ...n.position, x: cursor } };
    cursor += (n.measured?.width ?? 0) + gap;
    return node;
  });
}
```

Align operations are batched as a single history entry.

---

### R4.5 Copy/Paste Between Canvases — Clipboard Store, Center, Offset, Cross-Tab

#### R4.5.1 Clipboard Store with Serialized Node Data

The clipboard store is a new Zustand store separate from `useCanvasStore`. This allows copying from one canvas instance to another (e.g., from a "Templates" tab to "Edit Certificate" tab).

**Store:** `useClipboardStore`
**File:** `frontend/common/stores/clipboardStore.ts`

```typescript
interface ClipboardEntry {
  nodes: SerializedNode[];
  relativeOffset: { x: number; y: number };
  timestamp: number; // epoch ms
}

interface ClipboardState {
  entry: ClipboardEntry | null;
  // optional source identifier for cross-tab tracking
  sourceCanvasId: string | null;
  saveToClipboard: (nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[]) => void;
  readFromClipboard: () => ClipboardEntry | null;
  clearClipboard: () => void;
}
```

`SerializedNode` strips React Flow internals (`coordinates`, `target`, `source`, etc.):
```typescript
type SerializedNode = {
  type: CertElementType;
  position: { x: number; y: number };
  width: number;
  height: number;
  rotation: number;
  data: CertNodeData;
};
```

On `copyNodes(ids)`:
1. Fetches the nodes by ID from `useCanvasStore`.
2. Computes the bounding box of the nodes.
3. Stores `relativeOffset` = top-left of the bounding box (so粘贴的位置 can be center of viewport, not absolute).
4. Stores each node's position relative to the bounding box (so群 maintains spatial layout).
5. Saves to `useClipboardStore` and navigator.clipboard (for cross-tab).

**Serialization format (in localStorage for cross-tab):**

```typescript
const clipboardPayloadSchema = z.object({
  version: z.literal(1),
  nodes: z.array(serializedNodeSchema),
  relativeOffset: z.object({ x: z.number(), y: z.number() }),
  timestamp: z.number(),
});

function persistToLocalStorage(entry: ClipboardEntry): void {
  localStorage.setItem("cert-clipboard", JSON.stringify({ version: 1, ...entry }));
}

function readFromLocalStorage(): ClipboardEntry | null {
  const raw = localStorage.getItem("cert-clipboard");
  if (!raw) return null;
  const parsed = clipboardPayloadSchema.safeParse(JSON.parse(raw));
  return parsed.success ? parsed.data : null;
}
```

A `storage` event listener detects writes from other tabs:
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  const handler = (e: StorageEvent) => {
    if (e.key === "cert-clipboard" && e.newValue) {
      useClipboardStore.getState().loadFromLocalStorage();
    }
  };
  window.addEventListener("storage", handler);
  return () => window.removeEventListener("storage", handler);
}, []);
```

#### R4.5.2 Paste to Center of Viewport

On `pasteNodes()`:
1. Reads `ClipboardEntry` from `useClipboardStore`.
2. Computes the current viewport center: `viewportCenter = { x: -viewport.x / zoom + windowWidth / 2 / zoom, y: -viewport.y / zoom + windowHeight / 2 / zoom }`.
3. For each serialized node in the entry:
   - Creates a new node with new UUID (`crypto.randomUUID()`).
   - Position = `viewportCenter + relativeOffset + node.position` (top-left of bounding box aligns to center).
   - Re-selects the pasted nodes only.
4. Records the paste as a single undo entry.

#### R4.5.3 Multi-Paste with Offset

Subsequent pastes within 2 seconds of the previous paste offset each pasted batch by `{ x: +20px, y: +20px }`:
- A `pasteCounter` in `useClipboardStore` tracks how many times the same entry has been pasted in current session.
- Every paste multiplies offset: `offset = pasteCounter * 20`.
- Reset `pasteCounter` to 0 when a new entry is copied.
- If the user pastes, then copy不同 nodes, the counter resets and the new entry starts at center with 0 offset.

#### R4.5.4 Preserves Relative Positions

The bounding-box-relative positions are what makes the pasted cluster maintain its layout. Example:
- Node A at (100, 100), width 50.
- Node B at (200, 200), width 50.
- Bounding box top-left: (100, 100).
- Relative offsets: A=(0,0), B=(100, 100).
- On paste at viewport center (400, 300):
  - A lands at (400, 300).
  - B lands at (500, 400).
- A's pasteCounter offset of 20: A at (420, 320), B at (520, 420).
- This pattern preserves visual arrangement across paste destinations.

#### R4.5.5 Cross-Tab Paste via localStorage

The `useClipboardStore` listens to the `storage` event. When a write is detected from another tab:
1. The store reloads from localStorage (via `readFromLocalStorage`).
2. The canvas context menu's "Paste" item becomes enabled even though no copy was done in this tab.
3. The paste uses the most recent updated entry (highest `timestamp`).

A toast notification appears: "Clipboard updated from another tab" via `useToast` hook (MUI snackbar).

---

### R4.6 Touch Support — Pinch, Pan, Long-Press, Larger Handles

#### R4.6.1 Pinch to Zoom

Implemented via React Flow's `panActivationKeyCode` prop and `zoomActivationKeyCode`:
- Two-finger pinch calls React Flow's internal zoom handler.
- React Flow v12 supports `panOnDrag={[1]}` and touch gestures out of the box for two-finger pinch/zoom.
- The `zoomOnPinch` prop is enabled by default in React Flow; we set it explicitly for clarity: `zoomOnPinch={true}`.

#### R4.6.2 Two-Finger Pan

- `panOnDrag` is set to `[1, 2]` (middle/right mouse for desktop, two-finger drag for touch).
- `On touch devices`, a single finger is interpreted as "select / edit" by default; two fingers pan.

#### R4.6.3 Long-Press to Select

A `useLongPress` hook detects 500ms of touch without movement:
- On long-press start, add the touched node to `selectedNodeIds`.
- On long-press move (after threshold), enter drag mode for the selected node.
- On long-press end (release), keep node selected (don't deselect as the default tap behavior does).

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/hooks/useLongPress.ts`

```typescript
export function useLongPress(callback: () => void, ms = 500) {
  const timerRef = useRef<number | null>(null);

  const start = useCallback(
    (event: React.TouchEvent) => {
      const startX = event.touches[0].clientX;
      const startY = event.touches[0].clientY;

      timerRef.current = window.setTimeout(() => {
        callback();
      }, ms);

      // cancel if finger moves more than 10px
      const moveHandler = (e: TouchEvent) => {
        if (Math.abs(e.touches[0].clientX - startX) > 10 || Math.abs(e.touches[0].clientY - startY) > 10) {
          if (timerRef.current) clearTimeout(timerRef.current);
          window.removeEventListener("touchmove", moveHandler);
        }
      };
      window.addEventListener("touchmove", moveHandler);
    },
    [callback, ms]
  );

  const cancel = useCallback(() => {
    if (timerRef.current) clearTimeout(timerRef.current);
  }, []);

  return { start, cancel };
}
```

#### R4.6.4 Drag to Move (Touch)

Touch drag uses the same `onNodeDragStart/Move/Stop` but with touch events:
- React Flow handles this automatically when `panOnDrag` is set for touch.
- The single-finger drag without long-press is interpreted as pan (or no-op, depending on context).
- Single-finger drag WITH long-press held = drag node.

#### R4.6.5 Tap to Edit

- A single tap on a node selects it.
- A double-tap enters edit mode for text nodes (contenteditable) or opens the image/shape/QR/signature settings dialog.
- Tap detection: `onTouchEnd` with `event.touches.length === 0` within 300ms of `onTouchStart`.

#### R4.6.6 Touch-Friendly Handles

- Resize handles are 18×18px on touch devices (10px larger than desktop 8×8px).
- Detection: `useMediaQuery("(pointer: coarse)")` for touch devices (MUI utility).
- Rotation handle on touch is 24×24px with a larger grab target radius.
- Selection border on touch is 2px solid (vs 1px desktop) for visibility.

```tsx
const isTouch = useMediaQuery("(pointer: coarse)");
const handleSize = isTouch ? 18 : 8;
```

#### R4.6.7 Gesture Recognition Pipeline

A consolidated gesture handler component listens to all touch events and routes them to the appropriate action:

| Gesture | Event Sequence | Action |
|---------|---------------|--------|
| Tap | Touchstart → Touchend (< 300ms, < 10px move) | Select node |
| Double tap | Two taps within 350ms | Enter edit mode |
| Long press | Touchstart → 500ms hold | Select node + drag mode |
| Drag | Long press → Move (with finger) | Move selected nodes |
| Pinch | Two fingers, distance change | Zoom (React Flow built-in) |
| Two-finger pan | Two fingers move together | Pan (React Flow built-in) |

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/TouchGestureHandler.tsx`

The handler wraps the `<ReactFlow>` instance and intercepts `onTouchStart`, `onTouchMove`, `onTouchEnd`, and forwards to handlers based on gesture classification (state machine).

---

### R4.7 Performance Optimization — Virtualization, Memoization, Debounce

#### R4.7.1 Node Virtualization for > 100 Nodes

React Flow provides `onlyRenderVisibleElements` and `nodeOrigin` props:
- `<ReactFlow onlyRenderVisibleElements>`: React Flow only renders nodes whose AABB intersects the current viewport rectangle (plus a margin).
- Recommended threshold: enable this prop when node count > 100.
- Detection: `useCanvasStore((s) => s.nodes.length) > 100` selector in the canvas component.

```tsx
const nodeCount = useCanvasStore((s) => s.nodes.length);
<ReactFlow
  onlyRenderVisibleElements={nodeCount > 100}
  // ...
/>
```

#### R4.7.2 Debounced Resize

When the canvas viewport (zoom/pan) changes rapidly (during pan/zoom), node dimensions update many times per second. To prevent layout thrash:
- `useCanvasStore` updates are already throttled by React Flow's internal batching.
- Custom resize logic for the canvas container uses a 200ms debounce:
  ```typescript
  import { useDebounce } from "@uidotdev/usehooks";

  const [canvasSize, setCanvasSize] = useState({ width: 0, height: 0 });
  const debouncedSize = useDebounce(canvasSize, 200);
  useEffect(() => {
    useCanvasStore.getState().setCanvasSize(debouncedSize);
  }, [debouncedSize]);
  ```

#### R4.7.3 useReactFlow Imperative API

For all imperative operations (programmatic moves, zoom changes, history events), we use `useReactFlow()` to get access to React Flow's internal API:
- `getNodes()`, `getNode(id)`, `setNodes()`, `addNodes()`.
- `fitView(options)`, `zoomIn()`, `zoomOut()`, `zoomTo(level)`.
- `setViewport(viewport)`, `getViewport()`.
- `project({x, y})` (convert screen coords to flow coords).

The store actions (e.g., `zoomIn`) call React Flow's methods indirectly through the canvas component's `useReactFlow` instance:

```tsx
// Inside Canvas component:
const reactFlow = useReactFlow();

const handleZoomIn = useCallback(() => reactFlow.zoomIn(), [reactFlow]);
const handleFitView = useCallback(() => reactFlow.fitView({ padding: 0.2 }), [reactFlow]);

useEffect(() => {
  useCanvasStore.getState().setReactFlowApi(reactFlow);
}, [reactFlow]);
```

The store keeps a (non-reactive) reference to the `reactFlow` instance:

```typescript
// In canvasStore
reactFlowApi: ReactFlowInstance | null;
setReactFlowApi: (api: ReactFlowInstance) => void;
```

Store actions calling `reactFlow.zoomIn()` etc. use this stored instance.

#### R4.7.4 nodesDraggable Perf Flag

- All nodes have `draggable: true` by default.
- A `nodesDraggable` flag in the store can disable all dragging for performance scenarios (e.g., during read-only preview mode or animations):
- Setting it to `false` removes the resize and drag overlays and any drag event handlers.

```typescript
nodesDraggable: boolean; // default: true
setNodesDraggable: (val: boolean) => void;
```

#### R4.7.5 Memoized Node Components

All 6 node components (`TextNode`, `ImageNode`, etc.) are wrapped in `React.memo`:
- With a custom comparator that compares only `data.*`, `selected`, `dragging` (NOT `position` which changes on every drag frame).
- This prevents re-render of node content during other nodes' drags.

```tsx
import { memo } from "react";

export const TextNode = memo(({ id, data, selected, dragging }: NodeProps<CertNodeData>) => {
  // ... render
}, (prev, next) => {
  return prev.data === next.data && prev.selected === next.selected && prev.dragging === next.dragging;
});

TextNode.displayName = "TextNode";
```

The `data` ref is stable unless the node's data actually changes (useCanvasStore returns the same data ref for unchanged nodes thanks to Zustand's shallow equality).

#### R4.7.6 Viewport Memoization

A separate `useViewportMemo` hook depends only on `viewport.x`, `viewport.y`, `viewport.zoom`:
- Components that show "current zoom %" label only re-render when zoom changes.
- The minimap re-renders on any viewport change (it must).

#### R4.7.7 Edge Computing Performance

For tools we add to canvas: avoid `JSON.stringify(nodes)` on every render. Snapshot only on save events.

