### R4.8 Minimap & Controls — Pan via Minimap, Legend, Background Patterns

#### R4.8.1 Minimap Component

React Flow provides a `<MiniMap>` component. We use it with customization:

```tsx
import { MiniMap } from "@xyflow/react";
import { useCanvasStore } from "@/frontend/common/stores/canvasStore";

<MiniMap
  pannable
  zoomable
  nodeColor={(node) => nodeColorByType[node.data.type as CertElementType]}
  nodeStrokeWidth={3}
  style={{ background: theme.palette.background.paper }}
  onClick={(_, node) => useCanvasStore.getState().focusNode(node.id)}
  ariaLabel="Canvas minimap"
/>
```

- `pannable` allows pan via clicking/dragging in the minimap.
- `zoomable` allows pinch-zoom of minimap.
- The viewport rectangle is automatically rendered by `<MiniMap>`.

#### R4.8.2 Node Color Legend in Minimap

Each node type gets a unique color in the minimap:
| Node Type | Minimap Color |
|-----------|---------------|
| text | `theme.palette.info.main` |
| image | `theme.palette.success.main` |
| shape | `theme.palette.warning.main` |
| qrCode | `theme.palette.secondary.main` |
| signature | `theme.palette.primary.main` |
| background | `theme.palette.grey[300]` |

A small legend appears in the corner of the minimap when the user hovers it:
- A popover shows the type ↔ color mapping with icons.
- Or — simpler — a tiny legend below the minimap as part of an "info" `IconButton`.

#### R4.8.3 Controls Component

React Flow provides a `<Controls>` component:
```tsx
<Controls
  showInteractive={false}
  onZoomIn={() => useCanvasStore.getState().zoomIn()}
  onZoomOut={() => useCanvasStore.getState().zoomOut()}
  onFitView={() => useCanvasStore.getState().fitView()}
/>
```

We add a custom "Reset View" button at the end of the Controls:
- Calls `setViewport({ x: 0, y: 0, zoom: 1 })` to reset to initial state.
- Icon: `RestartAltOutlined`.

#### R4.8.4 Background Pattern Switcher

React Flow provides a `<Background>` component with multiple variants:

```tsx
<Background
  variant={variant} // BackgroundVariant.Dots, BackgroundVariant.Lines, BackgroundVariant.Cross
  gap={gridSize}
  color={theme.palette.grey[300]}
  size={1}
/>
```

The `variant` is controlled by the `backgroundPattern` in store:
| `backgroundPattern` | BackgroundVariant | Description |
|---------------------|-------------------|-------------|
| "dots" | Dots | Small dot at each grid intersection |
| "lines" | Lines | Horizontal + vertical grid lines |
| "cross" | Cross | Cross mark at each grid intersection |
| "none" | (Background hidden) | No background pattern |

A small icon button in the toolbar switches between patterns:
```tsx
<ToggleButtonGroup value={backgroundPattern} exclusive onChange={(_, v) => setBackgroundPattern(v)}>
  <ToggleButton value="dots"><GrainOutlined /></ToggleButton>
  <ToggleButton value="lines"><GridOnOutlined /></ToggleButton>
  <ToggleButton value="cross"><AddOutlined /></ToggleButton>
  <ToggleButton value="none"><DoDisturbOutlined /></ToggleButton>
</ToggleButtonGroup>
```

The icons use MUI's outlined variants per the MUI v9 convention change (`*Outline` → `*Outlined`).

---

### R4.9 Undo/Redo for Canvas — History Stack, Shortcuts, Panel, Batching

#### R4.9.1 History Stack Overview

The history system was outlined in §T01 (command pattern with coalescing). Round 4 extends it with:
- A history panel UI in the right sidebar.
- Timestamps on each entry.
- Max 50 entries (per the existing spec).
- Batch operations counted as single entries.

#### R4.9.2 Store Extensions

```typescript
// New fields in useCanvasStore
history: HistoryEntry[];
historyIndex: number; // pointer to current position in history (for redo)
maxHistory: number; // 50

interface HistoryEntry {
  id: string; // uuid for tracking
  action: string; // human-readable label: "Move Text Node", "Resize Image", "Paste 3 Nodes"
  timestamp: number; // epoch ms
  beforeState: CanvasSnapshot; //nodes + edges + selection at the time
  afterState: CanvasSnapshot;
  batchId?: string; // for grouping multi-operations into one undo step
}

interface CanvasSnapshot {
  nodes: Node<CertNodeData>[];
  edges: Edge[];
  selectedNodeIds: string[];
}
```

#### R4.9.3 Undo/Redo Shortcuts

- `Cmd+Z` / `Ctrl+Z`: undo — move `historyIndex` back, restore `beforeState`.
- `Cmd+Shift+Z` (Mac) / `Ctrl+Y` (Windows): redo.
- Custom handler in `useKeyboardShortcuts`:
  ```tsx
  const undo = useCallback(() => {
    const state = useCanvasStore.getState();
    if (state.historyIndex > 0) {
      state.undo();
    }
  }, []);
  ```

#### R4.9.4 History Panel UI

A floating panel accessible via a sidebar tab in the Right Sidebar:
- Title: "History" with a counter: "5 / 12" (current index / total entries).
- List of `HistoryEntry` from newest to oldest.
- Each row: action label + relative timestamp ("3 seconds ago").
- Clicking a row restores that state without adding a new entry (`setHistoryIndex`).
- Hover on row shows absolute timestamp in a tooltip (using `Intl.DateTimeFormat`).

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/HistoryPanel.tsx`

```tsx
import { Box, List, ListItemButton, ListItemText, Typography } from "@mui/material";
import { useCanvasStore } from "@/frontend/common/stores/canvasStore";

export function HistoryPanel() {
  const history = useCanvasStore((s) => s.history);
  const currentIndex = useCanvasStore((s) => s.historyIndex);

  return (
    <List dense>
      {history.map((entry, i) => (
        <ListItemButton
          key={entry.id}
          selected={i === currentIndex}
          onClick={() => useCanvasStore.getState().setHistoryIndex(i)}
        >
          <ListItemText
            primary={entry.action}
            secondary={formatRelativeTime(entry.timestamp)}
            secondaryTypographyProps={{ sx: { fontSize: 12, color: "text.secondary" } }}
          />
        </ListItemButton>
      ))}
    </List>
  );
}
```

#### R4.9.5 Batch Operations as Single History Entry

Multi-step operations (e.g., "Align Left" affecting 5 nodes) are wrapped in a batch:

```typescript
function withBatch<T>(action: string, fn: () => T): T {
  const batchId = crypto.randomUUID();
  const before = snapshotCurrentState();
  const result = fn();
  const after = snapshotCurrentState();
  pushHistory({ id: crypto.randomUUID(), action, timestamp: Date.now(), beforeState: before, afterState: after, batchId });
  return result;
}

// Usage:
useCanvasStore.getState().withBatch("Align 5 nodes left", () => {
  alignLeft(selectedNodes).forEach((n) => updateNode(n.id, n));
});
```

All align operations, multi-delete, multi-paste add a single history entry via `withBatch`.

#### R4.9.6 History Trimming

The history array is capped at `maxHistory: 50`:
- When a new entry is added and array length exceeds 50, the OLDEST entry is removed.
- Redo entries beyond the current `historyIndex` are cleared when a new action is performed (standard behavior).

```typescript
pushHistory: (entry: HistoryEntry) => {
  useCanvasStore.setState((state) => {
    const history = state.history.slice(0, state.historyIndex + 1);
    history.push(entry);
    if (history.length > state.maxHistory) history.shift();
    return { history, historyIndex: history.length - 1 };
  });
}
```

---

### R4.10 Alignment Guides — Smart, Edge Snapping, Equal Spacing, Theming

#### R4.10.1 Smart Guides

When a node is being dragged, smart guides appear when its edge (x or y coordinate) approaches another node's edge or center, or the canvas center.

- Threshold: 5px (prefixed by grid size in §R4.3.3).
- Guide color: `theme.palette.error.main` (red) - distinct from selection color (`primary`).
- Line stroke width: 1px, dashed.
- Guides appear horizontally and vertically.

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/AlignmentGuides.tsx`

```tsx
import { Box } from "@mui/material";
import { useStore } from "@xyflow/react";

export function AlignmentGuides({ guides }: { guides: SmartGuide[] }) {
  return (
    <>
      {guides.map((g) => (
        <Box
          key={g.id}
          component="div"
          sx={{
            position: "absolute",
            borderColor: "error.main",
            borderStyle: "dashed",
            borderWidth: g.orientation === "horizontal" ? "1px 0 0 0" : "0 0 0 1px",
            left: g.x,
            top: g.y,
            width: g.width,
            height: g.height,
            pointerEvents: "none",
            zIndex: 9999,
          }}
        />
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
```

#### R4.10.2 Edge Snapping (Align Left/Center/Right/Top/Middle/Bottom)

- Align Left: both nodes' `x` equal.
- Align Center H: both nodes' `x + width/2` equal.
- Align Right: both nodes' `x + width` equal.
- Align Top: both nodes' `y` equal.
- Align Center V: both nodes' `y + height/2` equal.
- Align Bottom: both nodes' `y + height` equal.

Each triggers a vertical (for horizontal-align) or horizontal (for vertical-align) guide line.

#### R4.10.3 Equal Spacing Distribution Guides

When three or more nodes are horizontally arranged with non-uniform spacing, distribute guides appear:
- Dashed lines between adjacent nodes representing the desired equal spacing.
- The user can enable the "Distribute" smart toggle to see these proactively.
- Or, they invoke "Distribute Horizontally" / "Distribute Vertically" from the alignment toolbar (§R4.4.7).

#### R4.10.4 Guide Visibility Toggle

A toolbar toggle enables/disables alignment guides:
- `showGuides: boolean` in store.
- When disabled, no guide lines render during drag; snapping is still active (just no visual feedback).
- Icon: `VisibilityOutlined` / `VisibilityOffOutlined` toggle.

#### R4.10.5 Guide Color Theming

Guides use `theme.palette.error.main` by default. A "customization" option is available via the user settings:
- `guideColor: string` in store.
- Default: `"error"` (key for `theme.palette.error.main`).
- Options: `"error"`, `"warning"`, `"info"`, `"success"` key strings.
- UI: a `Select` in the user's editor settings popover.

```tsx
const guideColorKey = useCanvasStore((s) => s.guideColor);
// sx={(theme) => ({ borderColor: theme.palette[guideColorKey].main })}
```

---

### R4.11 Accessibility — Keyboard Navigation, ARIA, Screen Reader, Focus Ring

#### R4.11.1 Keyboard-Only Canvas Navigation

The canvas supports full keyboard operation:

| Keyboard Action | Effect |
|-----------------|--------|
| `Tab` | Cycle focus between nodes (DOM order = z-index order) |
| `Shift+Tab` | Cycle backward |
| `Arrow Keys` | Move focused/selected node by 1px (or 10px with Shift) |
| `Enter` | Enter edit mode (text) or open settings (other types) |
| `Escape` | Cancel current operation (drag, resize, edit) |
| `Cmd/Ctrl+A` | Select all nodes (per §R4.4.3) |
| `Cmd/Ctrl+C/V` | Copy / paste (per §R4.5) |
| `Delete` / `Backspace` | Delete selected node(s) |
| `Cmd/Ctrl+Z` | Undo (per §R4.9.3) |
| `Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Z` / `Ctrl+Y` | Redo |
| `+` / `-` | Zoom in / out (with `=` instead of `+` for simplicity) |
| `0` (with Cmd/Ctrl) | Reset zoom to 100% |
| `1` (with Cmd/Ctrl) | Fit to screen |

#### R4.11.2 ARIA Labels on Nodes

Each node is focusable via `tabIndex={0}` and has descriptive ARIA labels:

```tsx
<div
  tabIndex={0}
  role="application"
  aria-label={`${node.data.type} node at position ${Math.round(node.position.x)}, ${Math.round(node.position.y)}`}
  aria-roledescription="certificate template element"
  aria-selected={selected}
>
  {/* node content */}
</div>
```

#### R4.11.3 Screen Reader for Node Position

A visually hidden `aria-live="polite"` region announces node positions during drag:

```tsx
<Box
  aria-live="polite"
  sx={{
    position: "absolute",
    width: 1,
    height: 1,
    padding: 0,
    margin: -1,
    overflow: "hidden",
    clip: "rect(0,0,0,0)",
    border: 0,
  }}
>
  {dragAnnouncement}
</Box>
```

The `dragAnnouncement` updates (throttled to once per 200ms) during drag: `Text node moved to 234, 156 pixels`.

#### R4.11.4 Focus Ring on Selected Node

A 2px solid focus ring `(theme.palette.primary.main)` outline appears on the focused node:
- Uses `:focus-visible` CSS pseudo-class (not `:focus`) so it doesn't show during mouse selection.
- On keyboard-only focus (Tab), the ring shows.
- Ring is rendered as part of the node wrapper, not React Flow's selection outline (which is for selection state, not focus state).

```tsx
sx={{
  outline: "none",
  "&:focus-visible": {
    outline: `2px solid ${theme.palette.primary.main}`,
    outlineOffset: 2,
    borderRadius: 1,
  },
}}
```

#### R4.11.5 Keyboard Zoom

`+` and `-` keys (without Ctrl) zoom in/out by 0.2 increments via `useCanvasStore.zoomIn()` / `zoomOut()`.
`0` (without Ctrl) resets to 100% (`zoomTo(1)`).

These are also accessible via the Controls component (which has the same buttons) — but the keyboard shortcuts assist keyboard-only navigation.

---

### R4.12 Error States — Invalid Data, Render Errors, Type Not Found, Corruption Recovery, Auto-Save Conflicts

#### R4.12.1 Invalid Node Data Fallback

When a node has missing or malformed `data` (e.g., `{}` or `null`):
- A `CertificateNodeErrorBoundary` wraps each node and catches render errors.
- Fallback UI: a placeholder rectangle with `theme.palette.error.main` border and text: "Invalid node" + a "Reset to default" button that calls `updateNode(id, defaultDataForType)`.

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/nodes/NodeErrorBoundary.tsx`

#### R4.12.2 React Flow Render Error Boundary

At the canvas root level, a `<CanvasErrorBoundary>` catches errors in the `<ReactFlow>` instance itself:
- On error, render a full canvas area with `theme.palette.error.light` background, an icon, an error message (with dev-only stack trace), and a "Reload Canvas" button (which resets to the last saved state).
- Uses React's `componentDidCatch` to send error to error reporting if available.

**File:** `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/CanvasErrorBoundary.tsx`

#### R4.12.3 Node Type Not Found Handler

If `nodes[].type` is not a recognized `CertElementType` (corruption, version mismatch):
- React Flow's `nodeTypes` lookup fails silently and renders a default.
- We override `defaultNodeType` to render a `UnknownNode` placeholder component:
  ```tsx
  const nodeTypes = {
    text: TextNode,
    image: ImageNode,
    shape: ShapeNode,
    qr_code: QRCodeNode,
    signature: SignatureNode,
    background: BackgroundNode,
    unknown: UnknownNode, // fallback
  };
  ```
- `UnknownNode` shows: "Unknown element type: <type>" with a "Remove" button.
- The validation happens at `useCanvasStore`'s `setNodes`/`addNode` — unknown types are logged via `logger.warn` and `type` is set to `"unknown"`.

#### R4.12.4 Corrupted Canvas Recovery

On canvas load:
- The `loadCanvasState()` action reads from the GraphQL backend.
- If the response is malformed or contains invalid nodes:
  1. A "Canvas Recovery" dialog appears with: "We detected some issues with this canvas. Would you like to attempt recovery?"
  2. The recovery action tries to sanitize each node (fill in defaults for missing fields, drop invalid types).
  3. The sanitized state loads.
  4. The original (corrupt) state is saved to a snapshot in localStorage (`cert-corrupted-snapshot-<timestamp>`) for debugging.

#### R4.12.5 Auto-Save Conflict Dialog

If the user is editing on two devices and the GraphQL server reports a version conflict (e.g., `updatedAt` newer than client's base):
- A modal opens: "Changes detected elsewhere. Save your version / Use their version / Merge changes?"
- "Save yours" overwrites server state.
- "Use theirs" discards local changes, loads server state.
- "Merge" — opens a diff view (advanced; not in scope for Round 4 but the dialog option is shown).

The conflict detection uses Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs) at the field level in GraphQL (server-side, not in this plan's scope). The UI side just responds to the conflict error code.

---

## Round 5: Advanced Fill & Stroke System

> Added as Round 5 to extend the Plan 11 visual canvas with production-grade fill and stroke rendering. This section covers live fill/stroke preview for all fill types defined in Plan 12 §R5.8 (`solid`, `linear-gradient`, `radial-gradient`, `conic-gradient`, `pattern`, `image`, `gradient-mesh`, and `none`), on-canvas gradient editing overlay, stroke rendering with position alignment (inside/center/outside), multiple stroke layers, and blend-mode compositing. The option type definitions live in `frontend/common/stores/cert/fillStrokeTypes.ts` (created in Plan 10 §R5.8) and are imported — Plan 11 does not redefine them.

### R5.1 Fill Rendering — SVG-based Live Preview

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/FillRenderer.tsx` (new)

**Design**:

The canvas renders each shape element as an SVG `<rect>`, `<ellipse>`, `<polygon>`, or `<path>`. The fill is applied via the corresponding SVG `fill` attribute or CSS `background`/`background-image` (for HTML-backed elements like text backgrounds). The `FillRenderer` component computes the SVG/CSS attributes from a `FillConfig` (from `@/frontend/common/stores/cert/fillStrokeTypes`) using `fillStrokeUtils.fillToSvgAttrs(fill, elementWidth, elementHeight)`.

```typescript
interface FillRendererProps {
  elementId: string;
  fill: FillConfig;
  width: number;    // element width in canvas px
  height: number;   // element height in canvas px
}
```

**Fill type → SVG/CSS mapping**:

| FillType | SVG `fill` attr | CSS fallback (HTML elements) |
|---|---|---|
| `none` | `fill="none"` | `backgroundColor: transparent` |
| `solid` | `fill={solidColor}` | `backgroundColor: {solidColor}` with `opacity: {solidOpacity/100}` |
| `linear-gradient` | SVG `<linearGradient>` def → `fill="url(#grad-id)"` | `backgroundImage: linear-gradient(...)` |
| `radial-gradient` | SVG `<radialGradient>` def → `fill="url(#grad-id)"` | `backgroundImage: radial-gradient(...)` |
| `conic-gradient` | **SVG fallback** (no native SVG conic) → CSS `maskImage: conic-gradient(...)` on a `<rect>` with `fill={solidColor}` and CSS `clip-path` to shape; alternatively, render as an HTML div overlay with conic gradient | `backgroundImage: conic-gradient(...)` |
| `pattern` | SVG `<pattern>` def → `fill="url(#pattern-id)"` | `backgroundImage: url({dataURI})` tiled |
| `image` | SVG `<image>` with `href={url}`, `preserveAspectRatio` | HTML `<img>` or CSS `backgroundImage` |
| `gradient-mesh` | **Rasterized** → HTML5 Canvas overlays the SVG element with a pixel buffer computed from the mesh points; `mix-blend-mode` on the overlay div | CSS `maskImage` or canvas overlay |

**Linear gradient SVG**:
```svg
<defs>
  <linearGradient id="grad-{elementId}" x1={x1} y1={y1} x2={x2} y2={y2}
    gradientUnits="objectBoundingBox">
    {stops.map(s => <stop offset={s.position/100} stop-color={s.color} stop-opacity={s.opacity/100} />)}
  </linearGradient>
</defs>
<rect fill="url(#grad-{elementId})" />
```

Where `x1/y1/x2/y2` are computed from the gradient angle:
- `angle = gradient.angle ?? 90` (degrees, 0 = horizontal left-to-right, 90 = top-to-bottom)
- Center of gradient axis: `(0.5, 0.5)` in objectBoundingBox units
- Half-diagonal: `dx = cos(angle), dy = sin(angle)`
- `x1 = 0.5 - dx/2, y1 = 0.5 - dy/2, x2 = 0.5 + dx/2, y2 = 0.5 + dy/2`

**Radial gradient SVG**:
```svg
<defs>
  <radialGradient id="grad-{elementId}"
    cx={centerX/100} cy={centerY/100}
    r={radius/100}
    fx={(centerX + focalOffsetX)/100} fy={(centerY + focalOffsetY)/100}
    gradientUnits="objectBoundingBox">
    {stops.map(s => <stop offset={s.position/100} stop-color={s.color} stop-opacity={s.opacity/100} />)}
  </radialGradient>
</defs>
```

**Conic gradient** — SVG has no native conic gradient. Two approaches:
1. **Approach A (preferred for simple shapes)**: Render the shape as an HTML `<div>` overlay positioned exactly over the SVG element, with CSS `background: conic-gradient(from {rotation}deg at {centerX}% {centerY}%, stop1, stop2, ...)` and `clip-path` matching the shape (via a CSS `clip-path: polygon(...)` or `clip-path: path(...)`).
2. **Approach B (for SVG-only rendering like export)**: Approximate with N wedges (default 72, every 5°), each a thin SVG `<path>` slice filled with the interpolated color at the wedge angle. This produces a raster-like conic gradient in pure SVG.

The renderer chooses Approach A for on-screen display (HTML-backed) and Approach B for SVG export (Plan 6).

**Pattern fill SVG**:
```svg
<defs>
  <pattern id="pattern-{elementId}" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
    width={repeatWidth * scale/100} height={repeatHeight * scale/100}
    patternTransform={patternTransform}>
    {inline SVG from patternDef.svgData}
  </pattern>
</defs>
<rect fill="url(#pattern-{elementId})" />
```

Where `patternTransform = rotate(angle) translate(offsetX, offsetY) scale(scale/100)`.

**Gradient mesh rendering**:
- Create an offscreen `<canvas>` element sized to the shape's bounding box.
- For each pixel (or 4×4 block for performance), interpolate the color from the nearest mesh points using inverse-distance weighting (IDW) with power parameter 2.0. The `smoothing` parameter scales the IDW power: `power = 1 + smoothing/50` (range 1–3).
- Output: `ImageData` → `canvas.toDataURL()` → used as a fill pattern via SVG `fill="url(#mesh-{id})"` referencing an `<image>` def.

**Image fill SVG**:
- Render `<image href={url} x={positionX} y={positionY} width={width * scale/100} height={height * scale/100} preserveAspectRatio={preserveAspectRatio} opacity={opacity/100} />` as a fill, clipped to the shape via `<clipPath>`.

### R5.2 On-Canvas Gradient Editing Overlay

**Component**: `GradientEditorOverlay`

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/GradientEditorOverlay.tsx` (new)

**Design**:

When the selected element has `fill.type === 'linear-gradient' | 'radial-gradient' | 'conic-gradient'` and `fillStrokeStore.gradientEditorActive === true` (toggled from the property panel's "Edit on Canvas" button from Plan 12 §R5.3), the canvas renders an interactive overlay on top of the element with draggable gradient controls.

```typescript
interface GradientEditorOverlayProps {
  elementId: string;
  elementBounds: CanvasBounds; // { x, y, width, height } in canvas coords
  gradient: GradientValue;
  onGradientChange: (updated: GradientValue) => void;
  editorType: 'linear' | 'radial' | 'conic';
}
```

**Linear gradient controls** (overlay rendered as SVG sibling to the element):
- A gradient axis line from `startPoint` to `endPoint` (the gradient direction). The endpoints are computed from the gradient angle: `startPoint = elementCenter - halfDiagonal(angle)`, `endPoint = elementCenter + halfDiagonal(angle)`.
- At each endpoint, a draggable handle (`HandleBar` component — a 10×10 px circle filled with `theme.palette.secondary.main` and 2px white outline).
- Between endpoints, the gradient preview is shown as a thin strip (4px wide) with the gradient applied.
- Each stop in `gradient.stops` is rendered as a 8×8 px square at its proportional position along the axis line. Dragging a stop changes `stop.position`. Double-clicking opens a mini color picker (reuse `ColorPickerField` from Plan 12 R4-T2).

**Radial gradient controls**:
- A draggable center point (crosshair icon) at `(centerX%, centerY%)` of the element bounds.
- A radius handle (filled circle) at the edge of the gradient radius. Dragging the radius handle changes `radius` (mapped to 0–200%).
- A focal point handle (small filled square) offset from center by `(focalOffsetX, focalOffsetY)`. Dragging changes the focal offsets (-100 to 100).

**Conic gradient controls**:
- A draggable center point at `(centerX%, centerY%)`.
- A rotation handle: a line from the center extending to the edge, with a circular arrow at the end. Dragging rotates the `rotation` value (0–360°).

**Performance**: All drag handlers use pointer events with `requestAnimationFrame` throttling. The overlay subscribes to `useCanvasStore` only for `zoom` (to scale handle sizes inversely) and `pan` (to offset positions) — not for element position (the overlay is positioned relative to the element, not the canvas).

### R5.3 Pattern Preview Tiling on Canvas

**Component**: `PatternPreviewOverlay` (internal to `FillRenderer`)

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/FillRenderer.tsx` (extends)

The pattern preview renders identically to the SVG pattern def (§R5.1) — no separate overlay needed. The `patternTransform` (scale, angle, offset) updates live as the property panel sliders change. The `<pattern>` element is redefined in `<defs>` on each prop change.

**Live tiling indicator**: A subtle dashed outline (1px, `theme.palette.divider`, `opacity: 0.3`) shows the pattern tile boundaries. This outline is only visible when the pattern fill is selected and the property panel has focus.

### R5.4 Image Fill Preview with Handles

**Component**: `ImageFillOverlay`

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/ImageFillOverlay.tsx` (new)

When the selected element has `fill.type === 'image'`, the canvas renders position/scale handles:
- A draggable position handle (top-left corner of the image) — dragging changes `image.positionX/Y`.
- A scale handle (bottom-right corner) — dragging proportionally changes `image.scale` (10–200%). Hold `Shift` to constrain aspect ratio (locked by default since images have inherent aspect).
- A `fitToElement` `IconButton` (small, top-right) — calls `image.scale = 100; image.positionX = 0; image.positionY = 0` to reset.

### R5.5 Stroke Rendering — SVG Attributes

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/StrokeRenderer.tsx` (new)

```typescript
interface StrokeRendererProps {
  elementId: string;
  stroke: StrokeConfig;
  shapePath: string;     // SVG path data for the element's shape
  elementBounds: CanvasBounds;
}
```

**Single stroke layer** (when `stroke.layers.length === 1` and `layers[0].type === 'solid'`):
- Apply standard SVG attributes: `stroke={color}`, `stroke-width={width}`, `stroke-opacity={opacity/100}`, `stroke-dasharray={dashArray}`, `stroke-dashoffset={dashOffset}`, `stroke-linecap={cap}`, `stroke-linejoin={join}`, `stroke-miterlimit={miterLimit}`.

**Dash styles**:
- `solid`: no `stroke-dasharray`
- `dashed`: `stroke-dasharray = [6, 4]` (6px dash, 4px gap)
- `dotted`: `stroke-dasharray = [1, 3]` with `stroke-linecap = round` (forced; user cannot change cap for dotted)
- `custom`: `stroke-dasharray = [dashLength, gapLength]`

### R5.6 Stroke Position Rendering (Inside/Center/Outside)

SVG natively centers strokes on the path boundary (`stroke-alignment` is not widely supported — it's in the SVG 2 spec but unimplemented in most browsers). Siraj uses three approaches:

**`center` (default, SVG-native)**:
- The stroke is rendered normally with `stroke-width`. Half extends inside, half outside the boundary.

**`inside`**:
- Use a CSS approach: render the shape with `box-sizing: border-box` and `border-width: {width}px` for rect elements. For non-rect shapes (ellipse, polygon), use an SVG `<clipPath>` matching the shape and render the stroke-clipped inside:
  1. Define a `<clipPath>` matching the element shape.
  2. Render two versions: the shape filled with the stroke color (no stroke), and the same shape shrunk by `width` px (a second `<path>` with offset points computed via a path-offset utility).
  3. Apply the clip-path to the outer shape, then overlay the inner (shrunk) shape on top with `fill="black"` and `fill-rule="evenodd"` to create a hole — leaving only the inside stroke visible.
  4. This requires offsetting path points inward by `width` px — for rectangles, simple; for ellipses, reduce `rx/ry`; for polygons, use a polygon-inset algorithm (shoelace-based).

**`outside`**:
- Render the shape as a `<path>` with `stroke={color}` and `stroke-width={width * 2}` (double width, then clip the inner half).
- Apply `<clipPath>` matching the shape, used as a negative mask (clip out the inside) — leave only the outer half of the stroke visible.
- Alternatively (simpler for SVG export): use `paint-order: stroke fill` with `stroke-width = width * 2` and reduce the fill area by half the stroke — this is exactly the inverse of inside.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/utils/strokePositionUtils.ts` (new) — computes the clipped stroke SVG for inside/outside positioning.

### R5.7 Multiple Stroke Layers Rendering

**Component**: `MultiStrokeRenderer` (internal to `StrokeRenderer`)

When `stroke.layers.length > 1`, each layer is rendered as a separate SVG `<use>` element referencing the shape path, in order from **bottom to top** (`layers[0]` is the bottommost, rendered first). Each `<use>` has its own stroke attributes.

```svg
<defs>
  <path id="shape-{elementId}" d={shapePath} />
</defs>
<use href="#shape-{elementId}" stroke={layer0.color} stroke-width={layer0.width} ... />
<use href="#shape-{elementId}" stroke={layer1.color} stroke-width={layer1.width} ... />
<use href="#shape-{elementId}" stroke={layer2.color} stroke-width={layer2.width} ... />
<use href="#shape-{elementId}" fill={fillAttr} /> <!-- fill on top, or below first stroke depending on paint-order -->
```

**Paint order**: For multi-stroke, each layer's `position` (inside/center/outside) causes the strokes to nest. Inside-most layer is rendered first (bottom of the z-order), then next, etc. The fill is rendered according to a per-element `paintOrder` field (default `fill-stroke-markers` — fill before strokes; can be flipped to `stroke-fill-markers` to draw strokes under the fill). This matches Illustrator's "appearance" panel stacking.

**Gradient stroke layer**: When `layer.type === 'gradient'`, the layer's `stroke` attribute references a `<linearGradient>`/`<radialGradient>` def (computed via the same gradient utilities as fill). For conic-gradient strokes, use the conic gradient SVG fallback (72 wedge paths with `stroke` keys — visually approximate).

**Pattern stroke layer**: When `layer.type === 'pattern'`, the layer's `stroke` references a `<pattern>` def computed from `layer.pattern`.

### R5.8 Blend Mode Rendering on Canvas

Each fill and each stroke layer has a `blendMode` field. The canvas applies this via CSS `mix-blend-mode` on the rendering context.

**For SVG elements**: SVG 2 supports `mix-blend-mode` via CSS on individual elements. Set `style={{ mixBlendMode: blendMode }}` on the `<rect>`/<`path`> with the fill. For older renderers (Plan 6 PDF export), the blend mode is emulated via color math.

**For HTML overlays** (conic gradient, gradient mesh): CSS `mix-blend-mode` on the overlay `<div>`.

**Global blend-mode toggle**: `fillStrokeStore.globalBlendModeEnabled` is a master switch. When `false` (the default during interactive editing — for performance and to avoid confusion), all `mix-blend-mode` CSS is set to `normal`. When `true`, the actual blend modes are applied. The toggle is a toolbar `IconButton` with `BlendModeIcon`, persisted to localStorage.

### R5.9 Fill/Stroke Caching During Drag Operations

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/utils/fillStrokeCache.ts` (new)

During element drag/resize, the fill/stroke CSS strings are expensive to recompute (especially for gradient mesh rasterization and multi-stroke layering). The `fillStrokeCache` utility memoizes per-element fill/stroke render output.

```typescript
function getCachedOrComputeFill(
  elementId: string,
  fill: FillConfig,
  width: number,
  height: number,
): CachedFillStroke {
  const cache = fillStrokeStore.fillStrokeCache.get(elementId);
  const key = serializeFillKey(fill, width, height);
  if (cache && cache.key === key) return cache.value;
  const computed = fillStrokeUtils.computeFillStroke(fill, width, height);
  fillStrokeStore.setCachedFillStroke(elementId, { key, value: computed });
  return computed;
}
```

The cache is keyed by a serialized JSON of `(fill, width, height)` — invalidating when any of those change. For drag operations that only change `x/y` (not `width/height`), the cache hits and skips recomputation.

**Cache clearing**: On `onNodeDragStop`, the cache for the dragged element is cleared (size may have changed during resize). On undo/redo, all caches are cleared. On zoom change, caches are cleared (render scale matters).

### R5.10 Live Preview Commit Model

Live preview updates flow:
1. User drags a gradient stop on the canvas overlay → `onGradientChange` is called with the updated `GradientValue`.
2. The store updates the element's `fill.gradient` in the canvas store (`useCanvasStore.updateElement(id, { fill: { ...element.fill, gradient: updated } })`).
3. The `FillRenderer` re-renders (subscribes to the element's fill).
4. The change is **not immediately committed to Apollo** — the canvas store holds it as a draft. The autosave hook (from R4 T34) commits after the 500ms debounce window.
5. During the preview, the `gradientEditorActive` flag stays `true`. The user clicks "Done" (or clicks outside the element) to finalize — which clears `gradientEditorActive` and triggers an immediate flush.

### R5.11 i18n Keys (Plan 11 R5)

Add to the `certificateEditor` namespace via Plan 10's R5 i18n additions (Plan 10 §R5.13 owns the namespace). Plan 11 only consumes the i18n strings; it does not own the namespace files.

### R5.12 Store Additions (Plan 11)

The `useCanvasStore` (from earlier rounds) gains:

```typescript
// Gradient editing
gradientEditingElementId: string | null;
setGradientEditingElement: (id: string | null) => void;

// Image fill editing
imageFillEditingElementId: string | null;
setImageFillEditingElement: (id: string | null) => void;
```

These coordinate with `fillStrokeStore.gradientEditorActive` to ensure only one editing overlay is active at a time.

### R5.13 Quality & Verification (Plan 11 R5)

**Per-file verification**: same loop as Plan 10 R5 §R5.9 — tsgo → biome → lint queue client → sub-loop.

**Instruction files**: `.agents/instructions/frontend.instructions.md`.

**Cross-plan contracts**:
- **Plan 10**: Imports `FillConfig`, `StrokeConfig`, `GradientValue`, `PatternDef`, `ColorStop`, `MeshPoint`, `BlendMode`, `GradientPreset`, `CachedFillStroke` from `@/frontend/common/stores/cert/fillStrokeTypes`.
- **Plan 12**: The property panel triggers `setGradientEditingElement` to toggle the on-canvas overlay.
- **Plan 6**: PDF export must consume the same SVG defs (`<linearGradient>`, `<radialGradient>`, `<pattern>`) — the conic gradient 72-wedge approximation is reused for PDF.
- **Plan 5**: Backend persists the full `FillConfig` and `StrokeConfig` (with multi-layer strokes) as a JSONB column on the element.

**Performance budget**:
- Gradient mesh rasterization must complete in <16ms (60fps) for up to 16 mesh points on a 400×400 element. Use 4×4 block sampling (not per-pixel) and Web Workers if needed (deferred to a follow-up — for Round 5, run on main thread with throttling).
- Multi-stroke rendering with N layers is O(N) for the SVG output — fine for N ≤ 5 (a reasonable upper bound for design-tool usage). Above 5 layers, warn the user via a property-panel note.
- The fill/stroke cache (§R5.9) reduces re-render cost to near-zero during drag-only operations (no size change).

---

## Round 5: Effects & Filter Engine

> Added as part of Round 5, extending the Plan 11 visual canvas with Photoshop/Illustrator-level effects and filter rendering. This section specifies the canvas rendering pipeline for effects: CSS filters for simple cases, SVG filter stacks for advanced effects, mix-blend-mode application, global light system, performance caching, and live preview throttling. The toolbar entry point is specified in Plan 10 §"Round 5: Effects & Filter Engine". The property panel with effect controls, presets library, and the "Flatten Effects" destructive action is specified in Plan 12 §"Round 5: Effects & Filter Engine".

### R5.1 Canvas Effects Rendering Pipeline

Each element (text, image, shape, QR, signature, background) acquires an optional `effects: EffectLayer[]` array in its `CanvasElement` data (extending the R4-10 `boxShadow`/`textShadow` single-shadow model into a multi-effect system). The rendering pipeline composes these layers on top of the base element without modifying the element's content.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/effectsPipeline.ts`

```typescript
import type { EffectLayer, BlendMode } from '@/frontend/common/stores/cert/effectTypes';

export function renderEffectsToStyles(
  effects: EffectLayer[],
  elementKind: CertElementType,
  globalLightAngle: number,
  globalLightAltitude: number,
): {
  cssFilters: string[];                                    // joined into one `filter:` string
  cssBoxShadows: string[];                                 // composed into `boxShadow` shorthand array
  cssTextShadows: string[];
  mixBlendMode: React.CSSProperties['mixBlendMode'];       // from the element's element-level blendMode
  svgFilterId?: string;                                    // advanced: <filter id="..."> referenced via `filter: url(#id)`
  isolation?: React.CSSProperties['isolation'];           // 'isolate' for isolation-group enabled elements
};
```

**Rendering order**: Effects in the array are applied top-to-bottom in the SAME order as listed in the property panel (Plan 12 calls this "rendering order"). The pipeline composes them by category:

| Category | Rendering strategy |
|---|---|
| shadow/glow | CSS `box-shadow` (multiple shadows separated by commas, including `inset` for inner variants). Drop shadows use `box-shadow`; outer glows use `box-shadow` with the same offset=0/distance-derived color. Inner glows use `inset box-shadow`. This is the simple path; advanced glow with non-radial falloff drops down to SVG. |
| blur | CSS `filter: blur()` for gaussian. Motion/radial/tilt-shift/smart/lens must use SVG filter primitives — CSS lacks them. |
| colorAdjust | CSS `filter: brightness() contrast() hue-rotate() saturate() sepia() invert() grayscale()` chained. Color Balance, Levels, and Curves must use SVG `feComponentTransfer`, `<feFuncTable>`, and a `<feColorMatrix type="matrix">` for individual channel mapping. |
| stylize | Most stylize effects (emboss, bevel, oilPainting, mosaic, pixelate, watercolor) require SVG filter networks (feConvolveMatrix, feComponentTransfer, feMorphology, feBlend, feComposite). Noise/Add noise uses SVG `feTurbulence` + `feComposite`. |
| overlay | Implemented at the DOM level as pseudo-element overlays (e.g., `::before`/`::after` with backgrounds and mix-blend-modes placed over the element). Color overlay → `::after` with solid color; gradient overlay → `::after` with gradient; pattern overlay → `::after` with SVG/PNG pattern; satin → `::after` with linear-gradient + wave. |
| blendMode (per-element) | Applied directly via CSS `mix-blend-mode` on the element root with `isolation` toggling. Per-effect blend mode is merged into the SVG filter stack and CSS shadow color stop. |

### R5.2 SVG Filter Stack Generation

For effects where CSS filters are insufficient, the pipeline dynamically builds an SVG `<filter>` element indexed by a deterministic ID derived from the effect parameters:

```typescript
export function buildSvgFilterStack(
  effects: EffectLayer[],
  elementBoundingRect: { width: number; height: number },
  elementKind: CertElementType,
): { svgMarkup: string; filterId: string };
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/svgFilterStack.ts`

The component injects the SVG markup into a hidden `<svg>` container (one per `<CanvasSurface>` instance) and the element's CSS `filter: url(#<filterId>)` points to it. The container is keyed by filter id; when the effects change, a new id is generated OR the existing filter is mutated in place. Mutation is preferred (re-using DOM nodes avoids SVG attribute-parsing JIT churn), but a quality-check verify that the browser cache invalidates correctly.

**SVG primitive mapping**:

| Effect kind | Primary SVG primitive | Supporting primitives |
|---|---|---|
| gaussianBlur | `feGaussianBlur` | — |
| motionBlur | `feGaussianBlur` (multi-kernel with `stdDeviation` axis-asymmetric) + a `feOffset` for direction | `feMerge` |
| radialBlur | `feGaussianBlur` (low stdDeviation) + `feComposite` (spiral/zoom) + multiple `feOffset` passes | `feMerge` |
| smartBlur | `feGaussianBlur` + `feComponentTransfer` (threshold gate via table function) | `feComposite` |
| lensBlur | `feGaussianBlur` + `feSpecularLighting` for specular highlights | — |
| tiltShift | Two `feGaussianBlur` passes with `feColorMatrix` mask | `feComposite` |
| emboss | `feConvolveMatrix` (3x3 kernel) + `feComponentTransfer` for color shift | `feBlend` |
| bevelEmboss | `feSpecularLighting` (bevel lights) + `feConvolveMatrix` (edge detection) + `feMerge` | `feColorMatrix` |
| oilPainting | `feTurbulence` + `feDisplacementMap` | — |
| mosaic / pixelate | `feImage` + raster image of large-cell-overlay | — |
| noise | `feTurbulence` (fractalNoise base) + `feComposite` (arithmetic for monochromatic mode) | — |
| filmGrain | `feTurbulence` + `feComponentTransfer` + highlight-area mask | `feComposite` |
| levels | `feComponentTransfer` with `<feFuncR/G/B type="table" tableValues="...">` | — |
| curves | `feComponentTransfer` with `<feFuncR/G/B type="discrete" tableValues="...">` sampling bezier curve | — |
| colorBalance | `feColorMatrix type="matrix"` per tone range | — |
| colorOverlay | DOM `::after` (not SVG) | — |
| gradientOverlay | DOM `::after` gradient (not SVG) | — |
| patternOverlay | DOM `::after` pattern image (not SVG) | — |
| satin | DOM `::after` with `filter: url(#satinWaveNeon)` | `feTurbulence` |
| outerGlow (advanced) | `feGaussianBlur`+radial falloff, or DOM `box-shadow` for simple case | — |
| innerGlow (advanced) | `feGaussianBlur` + `feComposite` (arithmetic) inset | — |
| invert / grayscale / sepia | CSS `filter: invert() grayscale() sepia()` (fast browser native) | — |

### R5.3 Drop Shadow via `drop-shadow()` CSS

For a single drop shadow (the common case) with standard parameters (color, opacity, angle/distance-derived x/y, blur, spread), the pipeline uses CSS `filter: drop-shadow(<offsetX> <offsetY> <blur> <color>)` rather than `box-shadow`. The advantage of `drop-shadow` over `box-shadow`: it follows the element's alpha mask rather than its bounding box — a PNG image with transparency will cast a shadow matching the visible pixels, not the bounding rectangle. It is also unaffected by `border-radius`.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/dropShadowRendering.ts`

```typescript
export function dropShadowToCss(
  effect: EffectLayer & { kind: 'dropShadow' },
  globalLightAngle: number,
): { cssFilter?: string; cssBoxShadow?: string } {
  const { color, opacity, angle, distance, blur, spread, useGlobalLight } = effect.params;
  const effectiveAngle = useGlobalLight ? globalLightAngle : (angle ?? globalLightAngle);
  const offsetX = Math.round(Math.cos(effectiveAngle * Math.PI / 180) * distance);
  const offsetY = Math.round(Math.sin(effectiveAngle * Math.PI / 180) * distance);
  const rgba = `rgba(${r}, ${g}, ${b}, ${opacity / 100})`;  // color → rgba with opacity substituted
  return {
    cssFilter: `drop-shadow(${offsetX}px ${offsetY}px ${blur}px ${rgba})`,
    cssBoxShadow: `${offsetX}px ${offsetY}px ${blur}px ${spread || 0}px ${rgba}`,
  };
}
```

The pipeline picks between `filter: drop-shadow()` (use for image elements, transparent PNGs — follows alpha) and `box-shadow` (use for shapes and text where bounding box behavior is fine). The decision is made by `elementKind` — `'image' | 'signature' | 'qr_code'` use `drop-shadow`; `'text' | 'shape' | 'background'` use `box-shadow` (or `text-shadow` for text).

**Multiple shadows**: If the `effects` array has > 1 shadow effect, each shadows are composed. With `drop-shadow()`, multiple effects are chained as `filter: drop-shadow(...) drop-shadow(...);`. With `box-shadow`, they are concatenated with `box-shadow: shadow1, shadow2, ...;`.

### R5.4 Inner Shadow Rendering

CSS `box-shadow` with `inset` keyword handles inner shadows directly; SVG `feComposite operator="arithmetic"` with negative k1 is an alternative for tighter look control. The pipeline uses the CSS path (simple) and falls back to SVG only when the inner shadow has non-standard glow/blur falloff.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/innerShadowRendering.ts`

- Compute offset from angle + distance (same as drop shadow)
- Form: `box-shadow: inset ${offsetX}px ${offsetY}px ${blur}px ${choke || 0}px ${rgba}`
- For text elements: CSS `text-shadow` does not support `inset` — fall back to SVG `feFlood` + `feComposite` to paint inner shadow on the text glyphs themselves; or render a clone of the text with `color: transparent` and `background-clip: text` + an inset shadow applied to the clipped background. Pick option 2 (works on standard browsers). Document this choice crisply in internal comments.

### R5.5 Glow Rendering (Outer + Inner)

Outer glow = `box-shadow: 0 0 <blur> <spread> <color rgba>` when `spread > 0`; behaves like a colored halo. For a strong glow with non-uniform falloff, SVG `feGaussianBlur` over `feDropShadow` (radius-based halo) is used; the simple CSS path covers 90% of glow cases.

Inner glow = inset `box-shadow` with `0 0` offset and a positive blur/spread. For "Glow source: Center" rather than "Edge", two inset box-shadow layers are composed: one from edge (positive spread) and one from center (negative spread — simulates a center-emitting glow).

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/glowRendering.ts`

```typescript
export function outerGlowToCss(effect: OuterGlowEffect): string;
export function innerGlowToCss(effect: InnerGlowEffect): string;
export function glowToSvgFilter(effect: OuterGlowEffect | InnerGlowEffect, rect: BoundingRect): { filterId: string };
```

For the **"Technique: Precise"** inner glow (vs default "Softer"), use a `feFlood` colored fill over the element alpha-mask, blurred with `feGaussianBlur radius=spread`, then `feComposite operator="in"` against the element alpha to clip to the element inner area. "Softer" technique uses the CSS `inset box-shadow` path which is faster.

### R5.6 Blur Rendering

```typescript
export function gaussianBlurToCss(effect: BlurEffect): string;   // `filter: blur(Xpx)`
export function motionBlurToSvgFilter(effect: MotionBlurEffect): { filterId: string };
export function radialBlurToSvgFilter(effect: RadialBlurEffect): { filterId: string };
export function smartBlurToSvgFilter(effect: SmartBlurEffect): { filterId: string };
export function lensBlurToSvgFilter(effect: LensBlurEffect): { filterId: string };
export function tiltShiftToSvgFilter(effect: TiltShiftEffect): { filterId: string };
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/blurRendering.ts`

- Gaussian Blur (`filter: blur(<radius>px)`) — CSS-native, fastest path
- Motion Blur — SVG: `feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="<radius> 0"` rotated via `feOffset` angle θ; alternate: two orthogonal blurs merged. Pick `feGaussianBlur + feOffset` approach with multi-pass estimation of motion direction (8 pass standard). Cache the result filter id so subsequent renders are cheap.
- Radial Blur — SVG: stacked `feGaussianBlur`+`feOffset` passes at 0°, 45°, 90°, 135° merged via `feMerge`; "quality" parameter (draft=2 passes / good=4 / best=8) controls pass count
- Smart Blur — SVG: `feGaussianBlur` + `feComponentTransfer` (threshold gate via `type="table" tableValues="0 0 <threshold/255> <threshold/255> 1"`) keeps edges sharp; falls back to gaussian when threshold=0
- Lens Blur — SVG: `feGaussianBlur` + `feSpecularLighting` (for specular highlights)
- Tilt-Shift — SVG: two `feGaussianBlur` passes blurred for top-half and bottom-half of the element with the focus band getting no blur; a `feColorMatrix` mask determines the lens gradient

### R5.7 Color Adjustments via CSS Filters

```typescript
export function brightnessContrastCss(effect: BrightnessContrastEffect): string;
export function hueSaturationCss(effect: HueSaturationEffect): string;
export function invertCss(effect: InvertEffect): string;
export function grayscaleCss(effect: GrayscaleEffect): string;
export function sepiaCss(effect: SepiaEffect): string;
export function vibranceCss(effect: VibranceEffect): string;       // composed from saturate + brightness
export function photoFilterCss(effect: PhotoFilterEffect): string;  // composed from sepia + custom hue-rotate
export function levelsToSvgFilter(effect: LevelsEffect): { filterId: string };
export function curvesToSvgFilter(effect: CurvesEffect): { filterId: string };
export function colorBalanceToSvgFilter(effect: ColorBalanceEffect): { filterId: string };
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/colorAdjustRendering.ts`

Range mappings:
- `brightness(-100..+100)` → CSS `brightness(${(100 + brightness) / 100})` (e.g., -100 → brightness(0), 0 → brightness(1), +100 → brightness(2))
- `contrast(-100..+100)` → CSS `contrast(${(100 + contrast) / 100})`
- `hue(-180..+180)` → CSS `hue-rotate(<hue>deg)`
- `saturation(-100..+100)` → CSS `saturate(${(100 + saturation) / 100})`
- `lightness(-100..+100)` → `brightness(${(100 + lightness) / 100})` (lightness behaves similarly to brightness in CSS saturate/brightness model; for true LCH lightness we'd need SVG `feColorMatrix` Y-luminosity path)
- `vibrance(-100..+100)` — CSS has no to-native; emulate via `saturate(saturation)` for the saturation component; the "preserve skin tones" aspects of true vibrance are out of scope, so document the simplification in code comments
- `PhotoFilter` — emulate with `sepia(${density})` + `hue-rotate(${warm/cool angle}deg)` for "warming filter (85)" and "cooling filter (80)" presets; custom color filter as `sepia` + `hue-rotate` + custom color overlay layer

### R5.8 Overlay Effects via DOM Pseudo-Elements

```typescript
export function colorOverlayPseudoElement(effect: ColorOverlayEffect): React.CSSProperties;
export function gradientOverlayPseudoElement(effect: GradientOverlayEffect): React.CSSProperties;   // uses the existing GradientValue type from R4 R5
export function patternOverlayPseudoElement(effect: PatternOverlayEffect): React.CSSProperties;
export function satinPseudoElement(effect: SatinEffect): React.CSSProperties;
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/overlayRendering.ts`

Each overlay is rendered as a pseudo-element laid over the element via `position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; mixin height: 100%`. Generated via inline React `<span aria-hidden>` rather than actual `::before/::after` for cleaner Z-index and a single render path. Mix-blend-mode is applied to the pseudo-element. Pattern overlay reuses the PatternFill system from R4-R5 Fill & Stroke R5 — same PatternFill value type.

### R5.9 Stylize Effects via SVG Filters

```typescript
export function embossToSvgFilter(effect: EmbossEffect): { filterId: string };
export function bevelEmbossToSvgFilter(effect: BevelEmbossEffect, globalLight: GlobalLightConfig): { filterId: string };
export function noiseToSvgFilter(effect: NoiseEffect): { filterId: string };
export function mosaicToSvgFilter(effect: MosaicEffect): { filterId: string };
export function pixelateToSvgFilter(effect: PixelateEffect): { filterId: string };
export function oilPaintingToSvgFilter(effect: OilPaintingEffect): { filterId: string };
export function cutoutToSvgFilter(effect: CutoutEffect): { filterId: string };
export function dryBrushToSvgFilter(effect: DryBrushEffect): { filterId: string };
export function filmGrainToSvgFilter(effect: FilmGrainEffect): { filterId: string };
export function watercolorToSvgFilter(effect: WatercolorEffect): { filterId: string };
export function outlineToSvgFilter(effect: OutlineEffect): { filterId: string };
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/stylizeRendering.ts`

- Emboss: 3x3 ConvolveMatrix kernel `[-1 -1 0; -1 1 1; 0 1 1]` normalized, with `preserveAlpha="false"` for color shifting. Angle rotates the kernel semantically (transform the 3x3 ordering).
- Bevel & Emboss: composed of `feSpecularLighting` with `surfaceScale=<depth>` and `specularConstant/specularExponent` based on the chosen technique; "Outer Bevel" produces an outward airbrush effect via offset additions, "Inner Bevel" uses `feComposite operator="in"` against the element alpha; "Pillow Emboss" uses inverted light + dark shading on opposite edges via two passes with `feBlend`.
- Noise: `feTurbulence` (baseFrequency based on the amount) → `feComposite operator="arithmetic" k1=0 k2=1 k3=1 k4=0` blended with element via mix-blend via `feBlend mode="screen"` (distribution: `fractalNoise` vs `turbulence` for uniform vs gaussian).
- Mosaic/Pixelate: `feImage` with a raster sample of the element downscaled to `<tile-size>` then reupscaled with `image-rendering: pixelated`. Alternative for pixelate same technique, smaller cell.
- OilPainting/Cutout/DryBrush: simplified emulation via asymmetric kernel blurs + color quantization via `feComponentTransfer type="discrete"` with `tableValues` from amount parameter. These emulate traditional Photoshop analog filters with approximations; documented as such — the precise algorithm is hard to replicate in SVG.

### R5.10 Per-Element Blend Mode & Isolation

The existing R4-9 BlendMode (16 modes) is extended to the full 46-mode list per Photoshop.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/blendModeMapping.ts`

```typescript
export const BLEND_MODES_46: { value: string; labelKey: string; cssValue: string | null }[];
export type BlendMode46 = (typeof BLEND_MODES_46)[number]['value'];
export function blendModeToCss(value: BlendMode46): React.CSSProperties['mixBlendMode'] | null;
```

CSS `mix-blend-mode` supports the following from our 46:
| Photoshop | CSS mix-blend-mode |
|---|---|
| Normal | `normal` |
| Dissolve | (no CSS — emulated via noise alpha mask pseudo-element) |
| Darken | `darken` |
| Multiply | `multiply` |
| Color Burn | `color-burn` |
| Linear Burn | (no CSS — emulated via subtract color matrix in SVG filter) |
| Darker Color | (no CSS — emulated: min(luminance) via feColorMatrix) |
| Lighten | `lighten` |
| Screen | `screen` |
| Color Dodge | `color-dodge` |
| Linear Dodge (Add) | (no CSS — emulated: sum via feColorMatrix) |
| Lighter Color | (no CSS — emulated: max(luminance)) |
| Overlay | `overlay` |
| Soft Light | `soft-light` |
| Hard Light | `hard-light` |
| Vivid Light | (no CSS — emulated) |
| Linear Light | (no CSS — emulated) |
| Pin Light | (no CSS — emulated) |
| Hard Mix | (no CSS — emulated) |
| Difference | `difference` |
| Exclusion | `exclusion` |
| Subtract | (no CSS — emulated via feColorMatrix subtract) |
| Divide | (no CSS — emulated) |
| Hue | `hue` |
| Saturation | `saturation` |
| Color | `color` |
| Luminosity | `luminosity` |

Modes without CSS support return `null` from `blendModeToCss()`. The element then needs an SVG filter applied for the blend (via `feBlend mode` where possible — `feBlend` supports normal/multiply/screen/darken/lighten/overlay/color-dodge/color-burn/hard-light/soft-light/difference/exclusion/hue/saturation/color/luminosity; so a few of the "no CSS" cases can use SVG).

For unsupported modes, the element is rasterized into an SVG `<feImage>` and composed with the parent background through a custom `feColorMatrix` operation. This is slow — warn in the function doc that fallback-only modes are for export-time rendering, not interactive preview. Interactive preview will use the closest available `mix-blend-mode` if any, or `normal` with a dev-only diagnostic warning.

**Per-effect blend mode** is stored on each `EffectLayer.blendMode`. The pipeline applies it on the SVG filter primitives via `feBlend` or onto the DOM pseudo-element via CSS `mix-blend-mode`.

**Isolation group**: When an element has `isolationEnabled: true` (per element-level toggle, R4-9 → R5 extension), the element's CSS `isolation: 'isolate'` is set — this forces the element + its pseudo-element children to be rendered in their own stacking context before blending against siblings. Without this, mix-blend-mode blends against all elements behind (including the canvas background and older siblings).

### R5.11 Global Light System

```typescript
export interface GlobalLightConfig {
  angle: number;       // 0..360 deg
  altitude: number;    // 0..90 deg (for bevel emboss)
  enabled: boolean;
}

export function shadowOffsetFromAngle(
  angle: number,
  distance: number,
): { offsetX: number; offsetY: number };
```

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/globalLight.ts`

Stored on the `CanvasState` (Plan 11 store root, not per-element) so all elements with `useGlobalLight: true` on any shadow/bevel read the same source. The angle of "Global Light" is 45° default (top-right light source convention).

**Update propagation**: When the global light angle/altitude changes, all elements re-derive their shadow offsets and bevel light directions. This is legion — for hundreds of elements, recomputation must be debounced. The store's `setGlobalLight` action delegates to a `useEffect` that batches the update across all visible elements; off-screen elements are not recomputed until scrolled into view.

### R5.12 Effect Rendering Performance

#### R5.12.1 Debounced Slider Drag Rendering

During slider drag events (e.g., user adjusting blur radius in real time), the canvas must NOT rebuild the SVG filter stack on every pixel change. The pipeline uses a 100ms debounce on each effect's parameter array:

```typescript
function useDebouncedEffectRender(
  effectId: string,
  params: EffectParams,
  canvasZoom: number,
): { committedParams: EffectParams } {
  // returns params stable for at least 100ms before triggering a render
  // on mouseup / blur / slide-completed event, commits immediately
}
```

On `mouseup` / keyboard release / pointer leave — bypass the debounce and commit immediately. Within drag, the last-committed params are used for rendering. This gives smooth 60fps movement but a 100ms-trailing visual of the new effect.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/useDebouncedEffectRender.ts`

#### R5.12.2 Effect Caching

The pipeline memoizes the compiled CSS strings/SVG markup by effect parameters. Cache key is a stringified effect params object sorted by key (stable JSON sort). LRU cache with 256 entries per element; eviction falls back to rebuild.

```typescript
const effectRenderCache = new LRUCache<string, RenderedEffect>({ max: 256 });
```

Invalidate the cache for an effect when:
- The user manually changes the effect (params changed at all)
- The element's size changes (affects SVG filter primitive region calculations)
- The canvas zoom changes (affects anti-alias threshold for stripes)
- The global light angle/altitude changes (impacts directional effects with `useGlobalLight: true`)

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/effectRendering/effectCache.ts`

#### R5.12.3 Lazy SVG Container

The hidden SVG container holding the `<filter>` definitions is lazily mounted at first non-CSS-only effect. If all effects on the canvas render purely through CSS (common for shadow + brightness + sepia compositions), no SVG container is mounted — saving DOM nodes.

#### R5.12.4 Render Bypass During Canvas Pan/Zoom

When the user is actively panning or zooming the canvas (R4 features `isPanning`/`zoom` interaction state), effect rendering switches to a "degraded" mode:
- Shadows: CSS `box-shadow` only (skip SVG path for advanced glows)
- Filter stack: only filter effects whose `kind` is in a `FAST_RENDER_KINDS` list (dropShadow, innerShadow, outerGlow via box-shadow, gaussianBlur via CSS filter, brightness, contrast, hue-rotate, saturate, sepia, invert, grayscale, color, luminosity, hue, saturation, normal, multiply, screen). Stylize and advanced blends are skipped, with a flag the element renders "stale-looking" until pan/zoom ends.
- Pan/zoom commits resume full rendering.

This ensures ≥30fps canvas pan even on layers with heavy filter chains.

### R5.13 Live Preview Architecture

The property panel (in Plan 12) renders parameter sliders. As the user adjusts, two preview destinations:
1. **Canvas preview** — applies the effect via the rendering pipeline above with debounce. Always live.
2. **Inline preview chip** — the property panel has a small thumbnail of the element with the effect applied. Stored in a `useEffect` render-on-idle, refreshed when params settle (after debounce).

#### R5.13.1 Hand-off to Canvas via Store Event

When the property panel commits new params (after debounce), it calls `useCanvasStore.getState().updateEffect(effectId, params)` which triggers a `useSyncExternalStore` notification. The element renderer subscribes to its element's effect list and re-renders. Subscribe with `useShallow` to avoid re-render on unrelated params.

### R5.14 Quality & Verification (Plan 11 R5 — Effects Engine)

**Per-file verification loop** (per AGENTS.md §"Verification Loop"):
1. `bun run tsgo`
2. `bun biome:check`
3. Lint via queue client: `curl -s -X POST http://localhost:${LINT_QUEUE_PORT}/lint -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"id":"r5fx-canvas-<file>","files":["<path>"]}'`
4. `bun run scripts/health/sub-loop.ts <file> --lifecycle lint`

**Instruction files to read before working on Round 5 files**:
- `.agents/instructions/frontend.instructions.md` (all frontend files)
- `frontend/views/AGENTS.md`, `frontend/AGENTS.md` (view components)
- `frontend/common/stores/AGENTS.md` (store modifications — `useCanvasStore`)
- `frontend/common/graphql/AGENTS.md` (if any new GraphQL documents needed for effects field)

**Cross-plan contracts**:
- **Plan 10**: Toolbar's `applyPresetToSelected` action lands via the canvas store's mutation. Plan 10 dispatches presets to canvas; Plan 11's `CanvasState.applyPresetToSelected(presetFactory, theme)` is the receiver. Define the function signature in this plan, implement in this plan's tasks.
- **Plan 12**: Property panel reads effect params from `CanvasElement.effects[]`. Plan 12's properties component imports `EffectLayer` from `@/frontend/common/stores/cert/effectTypes` — same file as Plan 10 FX-T01 and this plan's types.
- **Plan 5**: Backend schema must accept the new `effects` field on canvas elements. The shape matches the `EffectLayer` JSON. GraphQL custom scalar `JSON` for the effect params (since 36 different effect kinds share one common storage column / list field); resolves are typed in TypeScript.
- **Plan 6**: PDF must emulate filter chains (blur, shadow, levels, curves). Plan 6 §"Effects emulation" — out of this plan's scope — must consume the same `EffectLayer` JSON. Document the JSON shape contract in the `effectTypes.ts` source file so Plan 6 can consume it.
- **Plan 15** (preview & publishing): Preview must render effect filters live with the same pipeline. Reuse `effectsPipeline.renderEffectsToStyles` directly; no separate preview pipeline.

**Performance budgets**:
- One effect applied to one element: render target ≤ 16ms (60fps) during slider drag with the 100ms debounce
- 5 effects chained: ≤ 32ms (30fps) with debounce
- 10 effects chained or 100+ elements each with effects: degrade per §R5.12.4 (pan/zoom fast path)
- Cache hit ratio on a stable canvas edit session: target 80%+

**Refs**:
- CSS `filter` spec: https://www.w3.org/TR/filter-effects-1/
- SVG filter primitives: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG/Element/filter
- Browser mix-blend-mode spec: https://www.w3.org/TR/compositing-1/

