## Round 5: Snap & Align Engine — Snap Rendering, Smart Guides, Transform Handles

This is the **fourth** Round 5 domain in Plan 11 (after Advanced Fill/Stroke, Pen Tool, Effects). The toolbar/View-menu entry points are specified in Plan 10 §"Round 5: Snap & Align Engine"; the Align/Distribute/Spacing panel UI is specified in Plan 12 §"Round 5: Snap & Align Engine". Plan 11 owns the **snap calculation engine**, **smart-guide overlay rendering**, **interactive transform handles**, and **multi-element transformation**. All shared types (`AlignAxis`, `SnapSettings`, `SmartGuideColorToken`, etc.) come from Plan 10's canonical `frontend/common/stores/cert/snapAlignTypes.ts`.

### R5S.1 Snap Engine — Core Types & Algorithm

Extension to `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/utils/snapUtils.ts` (created in §3.5 of the base design) is replaced with a fully-fledged engine. The import surface is stable: `snapUtils` exports `snapCandidates`, `computeSnaps`, `applySnaps`, and the existing `SnapGuide` type grows into a richer `SmartGuide` sum-type defined here.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/snap/snapEngine.ts` (new — split from old `snapUtils.ts` because the round-5 engine is logically distinct)

```typescript
import type { SnapSettings, SnapTargetKind } from '@/frontend/common/stores/cert/snapAlignTypes';

export type SnapAxis = 'x' | 'y';

export interface SnapCandidate {
  axis: SnapAxis;
  value: number;          // candidate position in canvas coordinates
  kind: SnapTargetKind;  // grid | guides | pixels | elements | artboard | documentBounds
  sourceLabel?: string;  // e.g. element name for measurement label
}

export interface SnapSource {
  // The element rect currently being transformed (pre-snap)
  left: number; top: number; right: number; bottom: number;
  hCenter: number; vCenter: number;
}

export interface SnapResult {
  snappedX: number | null;   // snapped element x (delta applied)
  snappedY: number | null;   // snapped element y (or null if X had no snap)
  guides: SmartGuide[];      // guides to render for the active frame
  measurements: MeasurementLabel[];  // labels rendered near the cursor / element
}

export type SmartGuide =
  | { kind: 'centerH'; y: number; length: number; x1: number; x2: number }
  | { kind: 'centerV'; x: number; length: number; y1: number; y2: number }
  | { kind: 'edgeLeft';   x: number; length: number; y1: number; y2: number }
  | { kind: 'edgeRight';  x: number; length: number; y1: number; y2: number }
  | { kind: 'edgeTop';    y: number; length: number; x1: number; x2: number }
  | { kind: 'edgeBottom'; y: number; length: number; x1: number; x2: number }
  | { kind: 'equalSpacingH'; from: number; to: number; axis: 'y'; gap: number }
  | { kind: 'equalSpacingV'; from: number; to: number; axis: 'x'; gap: number }
  | { kind: 'equalSizeW';   width: number; y1: number; y2: number }
  | { kind: 'equalSizeH';   height: number; x1: number; x2: number };

export type MeasurementLabel =
  | { kind: 'distance'; distance: number; midX: number; midY: number; axis: SnapAxis }
  | { kind: 'size';     width: number; height: number; midX: number; midY: number }
  | { kind: 'position'; x: number; y: number; midX: number; midY: number };
```

**Algorithm**:

```typescript
export function computeSnaps(
  source: SnapSource,
  settings: SnapSettings,
  ctx: SnapContext,
): SnapResult;

interface SnapContext {
  grid: { size: number; origin: { x: number; y: number } };
  guides: GuideLine[];            // user-created guide lines (from store)
  otherElements: ElementRect[];    // every OTHER element's rect (excludes the dragged one)
  documentBounds: { width: number; height: number };
  artboardBounds: { left: number; top: number; width: number; height: number };
}

interface ElementRect {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  left: number; top: number; right: number; bottom: number;
  hCenter: number; vCenter: number;
}
```

Each snap target kind maps to a **candidate generator**:

| Kind | Generator |
|---|---|
| `grid` | For each of source.{left,right,hCenter,vCenter,top,bottom}, candidate = `Math.round(value / grid.size) * grid.size`. Halt early if `snapToGrid=false`. |
| `guides` | For each user-created `GuideLine`, candidate = guide's position. Snap uses perpendicular distance: horizontal guides → snap `top/bottom/vCenter` of source; vertical → snap `left/right/hCenter`. |
| `pixels` | All x/y/size values rounded to nearest integer (no sub-pixel). Always applies if `snapToPixels=true`; cheapest win — combined as a post-process, not a candidate. |
| `elements` | For each `otherElement`, emit candidate(s) for each of `{left, right, hCenter}` matching `otherElement.{left, right, hCenter}` (horizontal axis). Similarly for vertical axis. |
| `artboard` | `artboardBounds.{left=0, right=width, center=width/2, top=0, bottom=height, center=height/2}` as candidates. |
| `documentBounds` | `documentBounds.{width, height}` at the document edges — snap when element edge approaches document boundary. |

**Snap threshold & priority**: For each axis (X and Y independently), iterate all candidates of all enabled kinds. Compute delta = `candidate.value - sourceValue`. Pick the candidate with `|delta| <= settings.snapThreshold` having **the smallest absolute delta** (closest wins — multi-target priority rule from §"Snap priority"). If multiple kinds tie at the same delta, prefer in order: pixels < grid < guides < artboard < documentBounds < elements (pixels-greed is a tiebreaker because the user expects sub-pixel snapping away when precise positioning matters).

**`applySnaps` post-process**: When a snap is chosen, also round to integer pixels (i.e. multiply-priorities: only one of `{pixelPostProcess, other}` happens — sub-pixel positions are stamped to integers at the final step, regardless of which target won). This handles a corner case where snapping to a guide line lands on a non-integer; we still want clean final coords.

### R5S.2 `SnapEngine` Integration: Live Drag & Resize

The engine's `computeSnaps` is called from `useCanvasInteractions.ts` inside the `pointermove` handler, NOT inside React render. The guides / measurements are committed to a **local** `requestAnimationFrame` batch and pushed via a fast store slice (only the overlay-relevant fields) to keep the canvas element render-free during drag.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/snap/useSnapFeedback.ts` (new — local Zustand slice)

```typescript
interface SnapFeedbackState {
  activeGuides: readonly SmartGuide[];
  activeMeasurements: readonly MeasurementLabel[];
  setFeedback: (guides: SmartGuide[], labels: MeasurementLabel[]) => void;
  clearFeedback: () => void;
}
```

The pointer-handler sequence:
1. Compute the raw next `{x, y, w, h, rotation}` from pointer delta.
2. If `snapEnabled`, call `computeSnaps(...)` with the source derived from raw next; pick `snappedX / snappedY` and apply them to the next element state.
3. Push `guides` + `measurements` to `SnapFeedbackState.setFeedback(...)`.
4. On `pointerup`, call `setFeedback([], [])` and call the store's `commitDragChange`/`commitResizeChange` (already specified in previous rounds).

Snap feedback is **debounced to 16ms** (one frame) using a `setImmediate`-like throttle so high-DPI pointer move events (60–120/s) don't thrash the overlay.

### R5S.3 Smart-guide Overlay Rendering

The existing `SnapGuideOverlay.tsx` (base §"overlays") is extended to render `SmartGuide` line geometry + measurement labels.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/SmartGuideOverlay.tsx` (new) — mounted alongside `SnapGuideOverlay`; the older `SnapGuideOverlay` continues to render grid dots while `SmartGuideOverlay` renders alignment lines + measurements.

```tsx
interface SmartGuideOverlayProps {
  guides: readonly SmartGuide[];
  measurements: readonly MeasurementLabel[];
  colorToken: SmartGuideColorToken;  // from snapAlignTypes
  zoom: number;                       // canvas viewport zoom (affects stroke width & label scale)
}
```

Rendering approach: an SVG overlay sized to the canvas world plane, transformed by the canvas viewport transform.

- Each line uses `stroke={(theme) => theme.palette[colorToken].main}` — wait, SVG cannot accept theme functions directly. Instead, we read the colour at component mount (`const color = useMemo(() => theme.palette[colorToken].main, [theme, colorToken])` and pass it down as a CSS variable). `stroke="var(--snapGuide-color)"` so the colour is sourced from `theme.palette` at render time, never hardcoded.
- `strokeWidth` is `1 / zoom` so the line stays 1 logical pixel regardless of zoom.
- `strokeDasharray="4 4"` per Photoshop default for smart guides ( omission gives Illustrator-style solid).
- Equal-spacing arrows: drawn as a thin line between two element edges with **two arrowheads** at the ends. The arrowhead path is derived via small `<path>` segments. Arrow size scales by `1 / zoom`.
- Equal-size labels: at the bounding edge of each element, draw a short tick mark with a `"W: 320 px"` text. Use SVG `<text>` with `fontSize={12 / zoom}` to keep label size constant on screen.
- Measurement labels (distance / size / position):
  - `distance`: placed midway between two elements on the relevant axis, text `"${distance} px"` rendered with a small `rect` background for readability.
  - `size`: floating panel near the resize cursor showing `"W: ${width}px / H: ${height}px"`.
  - `position`: floating label near the drag cursor showing `"X: ${x}px / Y: ${y}px"`.

All labels get white `fill` behind them: `<rect>` with `fill={(theme) => theme.palette.background.paper}` + 1px stroke in the guide colour. Read theme palette at mount to avoid SVG inline function cost.

### R5S.4 Interactive Transform Handles (8 + Rotation)

The base design has `ResizeHandles.tsx` (8 handles) and `RotationHandle.tsx`. Round 5 extends these with **cursor-by-position**, **live labels**, **constrained (Shift) resize**, **center resize (Alt)**, and **proportional-from-center (Shift+Alt)**.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/ResizeHandles.tsx` (modify — extends existing)

**Cursor mapping** (CSS `cursor` property — these are CSS keyword cursors, not colours, so they are NOT subject to the no-hardcoded-colour rule):

| Handle | `cursor` |
|---|---|
| nw | `nw-resize` |
| n  | `n-resize` |
| ne | `ne-resize` |
| e  | `e-resize` |
| se | `se-resize` |
| s  | `s-resize` |
| sw | `sw-resize` |
| w  | `w-resize` |
| rotation | `crosshair` (browser doesn't have an off-the-shelf ruler-rotation cursor; we ship a small custom CSS cursor SVG via `url(...)` data-uri resolved from a theme-aware component — but listed per spec, the intent is a ruler rotation cursor). The SVG cursor data-uri is generated once at runtime from `theme.palette` and cached — no hardcoded hex literal. |

The cursor SVG data-uri is generated by a dedicated helper:

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/rotationCursor.ts` (new)

```typescript
import type { Theme } from '@mui/material/styles';
export function buildRotationCursorSvg(theme: Theme, size = 24): string;
// Returns a `url("data:image/svg+xml,...") 12 12, crosshair` string,
// with the SVG path stroke = theme.palette.text.primary (URL-encoded).
// The result is memoized by (theme.palette.mode, size) at module scope.
```

**Live labels during resize** (already specced in §R5S.2 for measurements): the `useCanvasInteractions.ts` resize handler pushes a `size` measurement that the overlay renders as `"W: ${width}px / H: ${height}px"` near the cursor. The handler:

1. Tracks `anchorEdge` (the opposite handle that stays fixed).
2. Computes new `{x, y, w, h}` from pointer delta.
3. `Shift+drag` → proportional: `h = w / originalAspect`.
4. `Alt+drag` → center-resize: both opposite edges shrink/grow by half the delta each.
5. `Shift+Alt+drag` → proportional-from-center: combine 3 + 4.
6. Applies snap via `computeSnaps` with a `SnapSource` derived from the post-resize rect.
7. Pushes labels via `SnapFeedbackState`.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/RotationHandle.tsx` (modify — extends existing)

Rotation handler:
- `Shift+drag` → snap to 15° increments (clamp the angle delta to nearest multiple of 15).
- Live label: `"θ: ${rotationDegrees.toFixed(0)}°"` rendered near the cursor via the same `MeasurementLabel` plumbing (the `position` label kind is reused with a prefix override — see §R5S.5).
- The rotation handle is offset above the top edge by 24 logical pixels (configurable), and visually styled as a small circle with a "stem" connecting it to the top-center handle.
- Cursor: built via `buildRotationCursorSvg(theme)` — see above.

### R5S.5 Measurement Label Component

A small shared component to render the live value badges (size / position / angle) while dragging/resizing/rotating.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/MeasurementBadge.tsx` (new)

```tsx
interface MeasurementBadgeProps {
  label: string;                // e.g. "W: 320 px / H: 240 px"
  anchor: { x: number; y: number };  // canvas coordinates (in the world plane, pre-zoom)
  zoom: number;
  theme: Theme;
  variant: 'size' | 'position' | 'angle';
}
```

Renders as an absolutely-positioned HTML `Box` (NOT SVG, because text rendering in SVG scales awkwardly) with:
- `position: absolute; left: anchor.x * zoom; top: anchor.y * zoom;`
- Padding 4px × 6px, `bgcolor: theme.palette.background.paper`, `border: 1px solid` in the snap guide colour token, `borderRadius: 2`.
- `fontSize: 11px; color: theme.palette.text.primary;`
- Translate offsets to avoid overlapping the cursor: `-50% -100%` for `size` (above the resize corner), `0 16px` for `position` (below the drag cursor), `-50% 0` for `angle` (top of the rotation handle).

The HTML overlay is **only mounted while an interaction is active** (unmounts on `pointerup`) to keep React reconciliation cost zero while idle.

### R5S.6 Multi-Element Transformation (Group Bounding Box)

The base design supports a *group* concept but only single-element transform. R5S extends to performant multi-element transforms operating on the **group bounding box** (the smallest rect covering all selected elements).

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/GroupTransformFrame.tsx` (new) — extends `SelectionFrame.tsx` rendering.

```tsx
interface GroupTransformFrameProps {
  selectedElementIds: readonly string[];
  boundingBox: { left: number; top: number; width: number; height: number } | null;
  rotation: number;     // group-level rotation (applied to all elements)
  zoom: number;
  onResizeHandlePointerDown: (e: React.PointerEvent, handle: ResizeHandleType) => void;
  onRotationHandlePointerDown: (e: React.PointerEvent) => void;
}
```

**Resize group**: when the user resizes the group bbox from a corner, every selected element's `{x, y, width, height}` is **proportionally scaled** so its top-left stays at the same *relative* position within the bbox. The transform handler computes `newScaleX = newBboxW / oldBboxW` and `newScaleY = newBboxH / oldBboxH`, then for each element: `newX = bboxLeft + (oldX - bboxLeft) * newScaleX; newWidth = oldWidth * newScaleX;` (and same for Y). Snap is applied individually per element against the group's snap candidates, but the proportional constraint means snap happens to the **bbox edges** first; the elements are derived from the snapped bbox.

**Rotate group**: the user drags the group rotation handle; all selected elements rotate around the **group bbox center** by the same delta angle. Element positions are recomputed by rotating each element's center point around the group center, then re-applying the element's own rotation (which stacks onto the group rotation for the visual transform).

**Move group**: handled by existing marquee/pm drag — all selected elements receive the same delta `{dx, dy}`. `computeSnaps` is called on the **group bbox** (not per element — important for performance); all elements get offset by the same snap delta to avoid divergence.

**Performance**: for a 100-element selection resized at 60fps pointer-move events:
- In-place numeric mutation of `{x, y, w, h}` per element (no allocation of new objects per frame).
- The `useSnapFeedbackState` only stores the rendered guide geometry — the actual element mutations live in an `immer`-less mutable patch in the interaction handler and are committed to the store on `pointerup` only (matches the existing `commitResizeChange` pattern in §R4).
- Virtualization of the transform frame overlay: only the active corner + rotation handle are rendered; the bbox stroke is a single SVG `<rect>`.

### R5S.7 Align & Distribute Implementation (Backend of the Engine)

Plan 11 owns the store actions that Plan 10's toolbar and Plan 12's property panel invoke. They live in `useCanvasStore` (extend the existing store from T10i).

**File**: `frontend/common/stores/useCanvasStore.ts` (modify — extends existing)

New actions:

```typescript
alignElements: (axis: AlignAxis, target: AlignTarget) => void;
distributeElements: (mode: DistributeMode, from: DistributeFrom) => void;
distributeSpacing: (gapPx: number, axis: 'horizontal' | 'vertical') => void;
matchSize: (referenceId: string, scope: 'width' | 'height' | 'both', referenceKind: 'first' | 'key' | 'largest' | 'smallest') => void;
setKeyObject: (id: string | null) => void;
setAlignTarget: (target: AlignTarget) => void;
setDistributeFrom: (from: DistributeFrom) => void;
updateSnapSettings: (patch: Partial<SnapSettings>) => void;
```

Implementation details (`alignElements`):
- Reads `selectedElementIds` (≥2 required — silently no-op otherwise).
- Resolves `target` → bounds rect:
  - `selection`: `union` of all selected element rects.
  - `canvas`: `artboardBounds` (zero-origin canvas).
  - `firstSelected`: the first id in `selectedElementIds`.
  - `key`: `state.keyObjectId`.
- Computes delta per axis and per element:
  - `axis='left'` → `newX = targetRect.left`.
  - `axis='horizontalCenter'` → `newX = targetRect.left + (targetRect.width - element.width) / 2`.
  - `axis='right'` → `newX = targetRect.right - element.width`.
  - similarly for vertical axes.
- Pushes a single undo entry (already-existing `HistoryManager` from R4): `AlignElements { axis, target, previousPositionsByElementId }`.
- Snap is **NOT** applied here — align is a discrete operation; if the user has snap on, the *target's* edge may itself be snapped during prior work but the aligner doesn't re-snap.

Implementation of `distributeElements`:
- Requires ≥3 selected — silently no-op otherwise.
- Sorts element ids by `{x}` (for horizontal) or `{y}` (for vertical).
- Computes the start = min corner, end = max corner. Depending on `mode`:
  - `horizontalCenters`: `targetStart/X` = `start + firstHalfWidth`, `end` = `end - lastHalfWidth` — evenly spaced **centers** between start centre and end centre, count = `selected.length`. Each element gets `newX = start + i * step - width / 2`.
  - `leftEdges` / `rightEdges` / `topEdges` / `bottomEdges` similar but anchor on the relevant edge instead of center.
  - `spacingHorizontal` / `spacingVertical`: equivalent spacing **gap between elements** — not center-to-center. Uses the same start/end as extents (leftmost element stays put, rightmost stays put), then arranges the middle elements so each gap = `(extent - sumWidths) / (n - 1)` — matches Photoshop's behavior.
- `from` interpretation:
  - `edge`: uses bbox edges (`leftEdges`, `rightEdges`, etc.) as start/end references.
  - `center`: uses element centers as start/end references.
  - `spacing`: skips `distributeElements` and goes straight to `distributeSpacing` semantics.
- Single undo entry per dispatch.

Implementation of `distributeSpacing` (gappy distribution):
- Sorts ids by `{x}` (horizontal) or `{y}` (vertical).
- Total extent = last right/left edge minus first left/right edge.
- Sum element widths/heights (in the distribute axis).
- Gap = `(userGapPx * (n - 1))`. New positions are placed one after another with `gap` between.
- This overrides `distributeElements` when `from === 'spacing'`.

Implementation of `matchSize`:
- Resolves the reference element (first selected / key / largest / smallest) and applies its `{width}` (or `{height}`, or both) to every other selected element. `x/y` are preserved.

### R5S.8 i18n Keys Added (Plan 11 — Snap & Align portion)

New keys in `certificateEditor` namespace, supplementing Plan 10 §R5S.7:

```typescript
snapEngine: {
  measurement: {
    distance: (px: number) => string;          // "{px} px"
    size: (w: number, h: number) => string;     // "W: {w}px / H: {h}px"
    position: (x: number, y: number) => string; // "X: {x}px / Y: {y}px"
    angle: (deg: number) => string;             // "θ: {deg}°"
  }
  equalSpacingLabel: (px: number) => string;    // "Equal spacing: {px}px"
  equalSizeLabel: (w: number, h: number) => string;
  snapNotification: string;                       // aria-live: "Snapped to {target}"
}
```

AR labels are **read by screen readers** — the live region must NOT spam; use `aria-live="polite"` + throttle to 250ms so SR doesn't fire mid-drag (conformance with the base design's accessibility contract).

### R5S.9 Quality & Performance Budgets (Plan 11 R5S)

**Per-file verification loop**:
1. `bun run tsgo`
2. `bun biome:check`
3. Lint via queue client:
   ```bash
   curl -s -X POST http://localhost:${LINT_QUEUE_PORT}/lint \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -d '{"id":"r5s-plan11-<file>","files":["<path>"]}'
   ```
4. `bun run scripts/health/sub-loop.ts <file> --lifecycle lint`

**Instruction files**:
- `.agents/instructions/frontend.instructions.md` (all frontend)
- `frontend/views/AGENTS.md`, `frontend/AGENTS.md` (views)
- `frontend/stores/AGENTS.md` (store modifications — `useCanvasStore`)
- `frontend/common/graphql/AGENTS.md` — **no GraphQL changes** in R5S (client-only); listed here for completeness only.

**Performance budgets**:
- `computeSnaps` for a 200-element canvas: < 1ms per pointer move frame (must finish inside the rAF budget).
- `SnapFeedbackState` slice updates should not trigger re-render of any element renderer — `ElementRenderer` subscribes via `useShallow` on its own element only.
- `GroupTransformFrame` overlay render ≤ 4ms per frame during multi-element resize at 60fps.
- Rotation cursor SVG data-uri is generated once and memoized; no per-frame work.
- Measurement labels unmount on `pointerup` to avoid leaving phantom DOM nodes.

**Accessibility**:
- `ResizeHandles` and `RotationHandle` are keyboard-accessible (per base §"Accessibility"): `ArrowKeys` resize by 1px, `Shift+ArrowKeys` by 10px, `+/-` rotate by 1°, `Shift++/-` by 15°.
- The drag label announced via `aria-live="polite"` SR region, throttled to 250ms (vs the visible label which updates every frame for sighted users).
- Every snap target kind has a human-readable label (`snapEngine.snapNotification` includes target name) — UX tells the user "Snapped to Artboard center" etc.

**Cross-plan contracts**:
- **Plan 10**: Plan 10's `useAlignShortcuts` calls `useCanvasStore.getState().alignElements(axis, target)` — signature MUST be stable.
- **Plan 10**: The `SnapSettings` dialog updates `useCanvasStore.getState().updateSnapSettings(patch)` — the engine reads it on the next `pointermove` so toggles take effect immediately (no reload required).
- **Plan 12**: The Align/Distribute panel UI (Plan 12 §R5S) calls the same `alignElements` / `distributeElements` / `distributeSpacing` / `matchSize` actions.
- **Plan 5**: No backend change. The Snap settings (toolbar toggles, threshold, colour token, sensitivity) are persisted as part of the template's `editorSettings` JSON — contract documented but the addition is client-side keyed by `editorSettings.snap = SnapSettings`.
- **Plan 15**: Preview & publishing ignore snap settings — preview canvas must not render smart guides (the published output is not edited interactively).
- **Plan 6**: PDF / image export ignores snap, smart guides, measurement labels — none of these visuals survive past the editor.

**Edge cases**:
- Snap threshold < grid size: snapping still works; the user gets a less aggressive snap.
- `snapEnabled=false` but `smartGuidesEnabled=true`: guides render without snapping — pure visual reference, no element displacement. Implemented as: `computeSnaps` called with `snapEnabled=false` returns `snappedX/Y=null` but still populates `guides`/`measurements` for the candidate within sensitivity.
- Element outside artboard bounds (dragged partially over the edge): `snapToArtboard` candidate fires on the bounding "outer" edge — the snap targets are the **outside** of the artboard too, so element edges can snap to the artboard's outer border.
- Rotation values stored in degrees (per the base `CanvasElement.rotation` field); 15° snap during rotation uses `Math.round(angle / 15) * 15` — independent of `SnapSettings.snapThreshold`.

---

## Round 5: Rulers, Guides & Grid System

> Added as a distinct Round 5 topic (Rulers/Guides/Grid) to extend the Plan 11 visual canvas with production-grade ruler, guide, and grid capabilities comparable to Photoshop/Illustrator/Figma. This section covers ruler rendering with zoom-aware ticks and unit conversion, user-created guides (drag from ruler, snap, lock, clear, new-guide dialog), the grid overlay system (square, baseline, modular, isometric, dot), smart guides for element alignment/spacing/size, snap settings orchestration, and the info bar. Guide/grid *settings UI* and *preset management* live in Plan 12 §R5 (property-panel / View-menu surfaces); Plan 11 owns the canvas-side rendering and interaction. Guide/grid *toggle menu items* and the View menu structure are owned by Plan 10 §R5.

### R5-G.1 Ruler Unit System & Conversion

**Files**:
- `frontend/common/stores/cert/guideGridTypes.ts` (new — shared types, imported by Plans 10/11/12)
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/rulerUnits.ts` (new)

```typescript
// guideGridTypes.ts
export type RulerUnit = 'px' | 'mm' | 'cm' | 'in' | 'pica' | 'pt';

export interface RulerUnitDef {
  unit: RulerUnit;
  labelKey: keyof CertificateEditorLabels['ruler']['units']; // i18n
  /** Conversion factor: 1 unit = N template-px at 96 DPI. */
  pxPerUnit: number;
  /** Decimal places for display labels. */
  precision: number;
}

export interface GuideLine {
  id: string;
  orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical';
  /** Position in template-px from the relevant origin edge (top for horizontal, left for vertical). */
  position: number;
  /** Optional lock flag — locked guides are not draggable. */
  locked?: boolean;
}

export type GridType = 'square' | 'baseline' | 'modular' | 'isometric' | 'dot';

export interface GridConfig {
  type: GridType;
  /** Major line spacing in template-px. */
  size: number;
  /** Minor subdivisions per major section. */
  subdivisions: number;
  /** Color as theme palette token key (never hardcoded hex). */
  colorToken: GridColorToken;
  /** Opacity 0–100. */
  opacity: number;
  /** Render above (true) or below (false) elements. */
  aboveElements: boolean;
  /* Modular grid settings (type === 'modular' only) */
  columns?: number;        // 1–12
  rows?: number;           // 1–20
  gutterWidth?: number;    // template-px
  alignment?: 'top' | 'center' | 'bottom';
  /* Baseline grid settings (type === 'baseline' only) */
  baselineSpacing?: number; // template-px
  baselineStart?: number;    // top offset
  baselineRelativeTo?: 'canvas' | 'selectedElement';
  /* Isometric grid settings (type === 'isometric' only) */
  isoAngle?: number; // 5–85, default 30
}

export type GridColorToken = 'grid' | 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'divider';

export interface SnapConfig {
  snapToGrid: boolean;
  snapToGuides: boolean;
  snapToElements: boolean; // "Layers" in Photoshop parlance
  snapToPixels: boolean;
  snapToArtboard: boolean;
  /** Snap threshold in template-px (2–20). */
  threshold: number;
  /** Priority order — first match wins when multiple targets qualify. */
  priority: Array<'grid' | 'guides' | 'elements' | 'pixels' | 'artboard'>;
}

export interface SmartGuideConfig {
  enabled: boolean;
  /** Snap threshold in template-px (default 6). */
  snapThreshold: number;
  showDistance: boolean;
  showEqualSpacing: boolean;
  showEqualSize: boolean;
}
```

**Unit conversion table** (`rulerUnits.ts`):

```typescript
export const RULER_UNITS: Record<RulerUnit, RulerUnitDef> = {
  px:   { unit: 'px',   labelKey: 'px',   pxPerUnit: 1,      precision: 0 },
  mm:   { unit: 'mm',   labelKey: 'mm',   pxPerUnit: 96 / 25.4,   precision: 1 }, // 1 mm = 3.7795 px @96DPI
  cm:   { unit: 'cm',   labelKey: 'cm',   pxPerUnit: 96 / 2.54,    precision: 2 }, // 1 cm = 37.7953 px
  in:   { unit: 'in',   labelKey: 'in',   pxPerUnit: 96,          precision: 2 },
  pica: { unit: 'pica', labelKey: 'pica', pxPerUnit: 96 / 6,      precision: 1 }, // 1 pica = 1/6 in = 16 px
  pt:   { unit: 'pt',   labelKey: 'pt',   pxPerUnit: 96 / 72,     precision: 0 }, // 1 pt = 1/72 in
};

export const pxToUnit = (px: number, unit: RulerUnit): number =>
  px / RULER_UNITS[unit].pxPerUnit;

export const unitToPx = (value: number, unit: RulerUnit): number =>
  value * RULER_UNITS[unit].pxPerUnit;

export const formatRulerLabel = (px: number, unit: RulerUnit): string => {
  const value = pxToUnit(px, unit);
  return `${value.toFixed(RULER_UNITS[unit].precision)}${unit === 'px' ? '' : unit}`;
};
```

### R5-G.2 Ruler Component — Rendering & Zoom-Aware Ticks

**Files**:
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/CanvasRuler.tsx` (extend existing — see §1.2 component tree)
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/rulerTickCalc.ts` (new)

The existing `CanvasRuler` (referenced in §1.2 and §9.3) is a basic pixel ruler. Round 5 extends it to support all six units, zoom-aware tick density, the origin marker, and the mouse-position indicator line.

**Props** (extended):

```typescript
interface CanvasRulerProps {
  orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical';
  zoom: number;                       // from useCanvasStore
  panOffset: number;                  // scroll offset for this axis
  origin: number;                     // ruler origin offset in template-px (0 = default)
  unit: RulerUnit;                    // from guideGridStore
  canvasSize: number;                 // template width (horizontal) or height (vertical)
  cursorPos: number | null;           // current cursor position in template-px (for indicator)
  onOriginDrag: (delta: number) => void;
  onOriginReset: () => void;
  onUnitChange: (unit: RulerUnit) => void;
  onGuideCreate: (orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical', position: number) => void;
  onRulerContextMenu: (event: React.MouseEvent, orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical') => void;
}
```

**Tick density algorithm** (`rulerTickCalc.ts`):

```typescript
export interface RulerTick {
  position: number;    // template-px from ruler start
  isMajor: boolean;
  label?: string;      // only on major ticks
}

export const computeRulerTicks = (
  canvasSize: number,
  zoom: number,
  unit: RulerUnit,
  origin: number,
): RulerTick[] => {
  const pxPerUnit = RULER_UNITS[unit].pxPerUnit;
  // Major tick interval in template-px, chosen so major ticks are ~80px apart on screen.
  const targetScreenSpacing = 80; // px on screen between major ticks
  const screenPxPerTemplatePx = zoom;
  let majorIntervalUnit = 1; // start at 1 unit
  // Scale the interval so major ticks are ~targetScreenSpacing apart on screen.
  while (majorIntervalUnit * pxPerUnit * screenPxPerTemplatePx < targetScreenSpacing) {
    majorIntervalUnit *= 2;
  }
  if (majorIntervalUnit * pxPerUnit * screenPxPerTemplatePx > targetScreenSpacing * 2.5) {
    majorIntervalUnit /= 2;
  }
  const majorIntervalPx = majorIntervalUnit * pxPerUnit;
  const minorIntervalPx = majorIntervalPx / 10; // 10 minor per major (subdivisions)
  const ticks: RulerTick[] = [];
  const startPx = -origin;
  const endPx = canvasSize - origin;
  const firstMajor = Math.floor(startPx / majorIntervalPx) * majorIntervalPx;
  for (let px = firstMajor; px <= endPx + majorIntervalPx; px += minorIntervalPx) {
    const isMajor = Math.abs(px % majorIntervalPx) < 0.001;
    ticks.push({
      position: px + origin,
      isMajor,
      label: isMajor ? formatRulerLabel(px, unit) : undefined,
    });
  }
  return ticks;
};
```

**Rendering** (MUI v9 — `sx` prop only, no style props):

```tsx
// CanvasRuler.tsx (render excerpt)
<Box
  sx={{
    position: 'absolute',
    ...(orientation === 'horizontal'
      ? { top: 0, left: RULER_SIZE, right: 0, height: RULER_SIZE }
      : { top: RULER_SIZE, left: 0, bottom: 0, width: RULER_SIZE }),
    backgroundColor: (theme) => theme.palette.background.paper,
    overflow: 'hidden',
    cursor: 'crosshair',
  }}
  onPointerDown={handleRulerPointerDown}
  onContextMenu={handleContextMenu}
>
  <svg width="100%" height="100%" aria-hidden="true">
    {ticks.map((tick) => (
      <line
        key={tick.position}
        x1={orientation === 'horizontal' ? tick.position * zoom - panOffset : 0}
        y1={orientation === 'horizontal' ? 0 : tick.position * zoom - panOffset}
        x2={orientation === 'horizontal' ? tick.position * zoom - panOffset : tick.isMajor ? RULER_SIZE : RULER_SIZE / 2}
        y2={orientation === 'horizontal' ? tick.isMajor ? RULER_SIZE : RULER_SIZE / 2 : tick.position * zoom - panOffset}
        stroke={(theme) => theme.palette.text.secondary}
        strokeWidth={tick.isMajor ? 1 : 0.5}
        opacity={tick.isMajor ? 0.8 : 0.4}
      />
    ))}
    {ticks.filter(t => t.isMajor).map((tick) => (
      <text
        key={`label-${tick.position}`}
        x={orientation === 'horizontal' ? tick.position * zoom - panOffset + 2 : 4}
        y={orientation === 'horizontal' ? RULER_SIZE - 6 : tick.position * zoom - panOffset - 2}
        fill={(theme) => theme.palette.text.primary}
        fontSize={9}
        fontFamily="monospace"
        textAnchor={orientation === 'vertical' ? 'end' : 'start'}
        transform={orientation === 'vertical' ? 'rotate(-90)' : undefined}
      >
        {tick.label}
      </text>
    ))}
    {/* Mouse position indicator */}
    {cursorPos !== null && (
      <line
        x1={orientation === 'horizontal' ? cursorPos * zoom - panOffset : 0}
        y1={orientation === 'horizontal' ? 0 : cursorPos * zoom - panOffset}
        x2={orientation === 'horizontal' ? cursorPos * zoom - panOffset : RULER_SIZE}
        y2={orientation === 'horizontal' ? RULER_SIZE : cursorPos * zoom - panOffset}
        stroke={(theme) => theme.palette.primary.main}
        strokeWidth={1}
      />
    )}
  </svg>
</Box>
```

**Constants**: `RULER_SIZE = 25` (px height/width).

### R5-G.3 Ruler Origin Marker

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/RulerOriginMarker.tsx` (new)

The top-left 25×25px square where the two rulers meet. Behaviors:
- **Click + drag**: Moves the ruler origin. Both rulers offset their zero point by the dragged delta. The `origin` state in `guideGridStore` updates live.
- **Double-click**: Resets origin to `(0, 0)`.
- **Cursor**: `crosshair` on hover, `grabbing` during drag.

```tsx
interface RulerOriginMarkerProps {
  origin: { x: number; y: number };  // template-px offsets
  onOriginDrag: (delta: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
  onOriginReset: () => void;
}
// Renders a Box sx={{ position: absolute, top: 0, left: 0, width: RULER_SIZE, height: RULER_SIZE, ... }}
// with pointer handlers for drag/double-click.
```

### R5-G.4 Ruler Context Menu (Unit Toggle)

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/RulerContextMenu.tsx` (new)

Right-click on either ruler opens a MUI `Menu` with the six unit options (`px`, `mm`, `cm`, `in`, `pica`, `pt`). Selecting a unit calls `guideGridStore.setRulerUnit(unit)`. The menu anchors at the cursor position.

Uses `useAppTranslation(Translation.Dashboard.CertificatesCanvas)` for labels — keys under `ruler.units.*`.

### R5-G.5 Guide Creation — Drag from Ruler

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/interactions/guideDragInteraction.ts` (new)

When a pointer-down occurs on a ruler (not on the origin marker), the canvas enters a "guide-creation drag" interaction state:

```typescript
type InteractionState =
  | ...
  | { type: 'creating-guide'; orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical'; startPosition: number; currentPosition: number };
```

**Flow**:
1. `pointerdown` on ruler → set interaction state `{ type: 'creating-guide', orientation, startPosition, currentPosition }`.
2. `pointermove` → update `currentPosition` (in template-px, converted from screen coords via `screenToTemplate`). A temporary guide line renders at the current position (see §R5-G.6).
3. While dragging, the position snaps to smart-guide candidates (center, thirds, quarters, element edges) when within `snapThreshold` (default 6px). See §R5-G.9.
4. `pointerup` → if the release position is inside the canvas bounds, call `guideGridStore.addGuide({ orientation, position: snappedPosition })`. If released back over the ruler area (outside canvas), the guide is discarded (not created).

**Horizontal guide** (dragged from top ruler): the guide's `position` = template-Y. **Vertical guide** (dragged from left ruler): `position` = template-X.

### R5-G.6 Guide Rendering & Overlay

**Files**:
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/GuideOverlay.tsx` (new)
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/GuideDragPreview.tsx` (new — temporary guide during creation drag)

**GuideOverlay** renders all guides from `guideGridStore.guides` as SVG lines *on top of elements* (or below, per `gridConfig.aboveElements` — guides follow the same toggle). Guide color comes from `guideGridStore.guideColor` which maps to a theme palette token (default: `theme.palette.info.main` — cyan/blue per spec).

```tsx
// GuideOverlay.tsx
interface GuideOverlayProps {
  guides: readonly GuideLine[];
  zoom: number;
  panOffset: { x: number; y: number };
  canvasSize: { width: number; height: number };
  guideColor: string;      // resolved from theme token
  guidesLocked: boolean;
  guidesVisible: boolean;
  selectedGuideId: string | null;
  onGuideClick: (guideId: string) => void;
  onGuideDrag: (guideId: string, newPosition: number) => void;
  onGuideDelete: (guideId: string) => void;
}
// Each guide renders as <line stroke={guideColor} strokeWidth={1} />
// Selected guide renders with a thicker stroke (2px) and a small handle dot at the intersection.
// Locked guides render with a lock icon at the end.
```

**GuideDragPreview**: During a guide-creation drag (§R5-G.5) or guide-move drag, a dashed line renders at the current pointer position. When snapping is active, a label appears near the line showing the position in the current ruler unit (e.g., `"X: 500px"`).

### R5-G.7 Guide Interactions — Move, Delete, Lock

**Move guide**: `pointerdown` on an existing guide line (when `guidesLocked === false` and the Move tool is active) enters a `{ type: 'moving-guide'; guideId }` interaction state. Pointer-move updates the guide position (with snap). Pointer-up commits.

**Delete guide**: Two methods:
1. Drag the guide back into the ruler area (outside canvas bounds) → `guideGridStore.removeGuide(guideId)`.
2. Select the guide (click) then press `Delete` key → `removeGuide`.

**Lock guides**: `guideGridStore.lockGuides` toggles a boolean. When `true`, all guides render with the lock styling and `pointerdown` on a guide is a no-op (no move/delete).

**Clear guides**: `guideGridStore.clearGuides()` removes all guides. Triggered from View menu (Plan 10 §R5).

**Show/hide guides**: `guideGridStore.guidesVisible` boolean. Keyboard: `Ctrl+;` toggles. When hidden, `GuideOverlay` renders nothing.

### R5-G.8 New Guide Dialog

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/NewGuideDialog.tsx` (new)

Triggered from View menu (Plan 10 §R5) → "New Guide…". A MUI `Dialog` with:
- `ToggleButtonGroup` for orientation: Horizontal / Vertical
- `TextField` for position (numeric), with the current ruler unit shown as a suffix (`InputProps.endAdornment`)
- Submit calls `guideGridStore.addGuide({ orientation, position: unitToPx(value, unit) })`

```tsx
interface NewGuideDialogProps {
  open: boolean;
  onClose: () => void;
  unit: RulerUnit;
  canvasSize: { width: number; height: number };
  onAddGuide: (orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical', positionPx: number) => void;
}
```

### R5-G.9 Smart Guide Placement (Drag Snap)

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/smartGuideSnap.ts` (new)

During guide-creation and guide-move drags, the position snaps to candidate positions when within `snapThreshold` (default 6px template-px). Candidates are computed from the canvas dimensions and existing elements:

```typescript
export interface SnapCandidate {
  position: number;       // template-px
  label: string;          // e.g., "Center", "33%", "Element: Text 1 - Left Edge"
  priority: number;       // lower = higher priority
}

export const computeSnapCandidates = (
  orientation: 'horizontal' | 'vertical',
  canvasSize: number,      // dimension perpendicular to the guide
  elements: readonly CanvasElement[],
  currentPos: number,
  threshold: number,
): SnapCandidate[] => {
  const candidates: SnapCandidate[] = [];
  // Canvas-relative: center, thirds, quarters
  const relative = [
    { p: canvasSize / 2, label: 'Center' },
    { p: canvasSize / 3, label: '33%' },
    { p: (2 * canvasSize) / 3, label: '67%' },
    { p: canvasSize / 4, label: '25%' },
    { p: canvasSize / 2, label: '50%' }, // duplicate of center, deduped
    { p: (3 * canvasSize) / 4, label: '75%' },
  ];
  for (const r of relative) {
    if (Math.abs(r.p - currentPos) < threshold) {
      candidates.push({ position: r.p, label: r.label, priority: 0 });
    }
  }
  // Element edges and centers
  for (const el of elements) {
    const edges = orientation === 'horizontal'
      ? [el.y, el.y + el.height, el.y + el.height / 2]
      : [el.x, el.x + el.width, el.x + el.width / 2];
    const labels = ['Top/Left Edge', 'Bottom/Right Edge', 'Center'];
    edges.forEach((pos, i) => {
      if (Math.abs(pos - currentPos) < threshold) {
        candidates.push({ position: pos, label: `${el.type} ${labels[i]}`, priority: 1 });
      }
    });
  }
  // Sort by distance, then priority; return all within threshold
  candidates.sort((a, b) => Math.abs(a.position - currentPos) - Math.abs(b.position - currentPos));
  return candidates;
};
```

When a candidate is within threshold, the guide snaps to `candidate.position` and a small label appears showing the snap reason (e.g., `"50% — Center"`).

### R5-G.10 Grid Overlay Rendering

**Files**:
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/GridOverlay.tsx` (new — extends the existing `SnapGuideOverlay`'s grid dots from §1.2)
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/gridRenderer.ts` (new — pure function that produces SVG line/dot data)

The existing `SnapGuideOverlay` (§1.2, §8.3) renders basic grid dots. Round 5 replaces that with a configurable `GridOverlay` supporting five grid types.

```tsx
interface GridOverlayProps {
  config: GridConfig;
  zoom: number;
  panOffset: { x: number; y: number };
  canvasSize: { width: number; height: number };
  visible: boolean;
  color: string;           // resolved from colorToken via theme
}
```

**Grid type rendering** (`gridRenderer.ts`):

```typescript
export interface GridLine { x1: number; y1: number; x2: number; y2: number; isMajor: boolean; }
export interface GridDot { x: number; y: number; isMajor: boolean; }

export const renderGrid = (config: GridConfig, canvasSize: { width: number; height: number }): { lines: GridLine[]; dots: GridDot[] } => {
  switch (config.type) {
    case 'square': return renderSquareGrid(config, canvasSize);
    case 'baseline': return renderBaselineGrid(config, canvasSize);
    case 'modular': return renderModularGrid(config, canvasSize);
    case 'isometric': return renderIsometricGrid(config, canvasSize);
    case 'dot': return renderDotGrid(config, canvasSize);
  }
};
```

**Square grid**: Vertical and horizontal lines at every `config.size` px. Major lines at `size` intervals, minor lines at `size / subdivisions`. Lines span the full canvas.

**Baseline grid**: Horizontal lines only at `config.baselineSpacing` intervals, starting from `config.baselineStart`. No vertical lines. Used for text baseline alignment.

**Modular grid**: `config.columns` vertical column dividers and `config.rows` horizontal row dividers, with `config.gutterWidth` gutters between columns. The column width is computed as `(canvasWidth - (columns - 1) * gutterWidth) / columns`. Alignment shifts the grid vertically (top/center/bottom).

**Isometric grid**: Two sets of diagonal lines at `config.isoAngle` and `-config.isoAngle` from horizontal, spaced at `config.size` intervals. Produces the classic 30°/60° isometric diamond pattern used in technical/AXO drawing.

**Dot grid**: Dots (1.5px radius for major, 0.75px for minor) at every grid intersection instead of lines. Lighter visual weight — preferred for layout work where line grids are visually noisy.

All colors via `sx` theme callback — never hardcoded. Grid opacity applied via SVG `opacity` attribute from `config.opacity / 100`.

### R5-G.11 Smart Guides — Element Alignment During Drag

**Files**:
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/components/SmartGuideOverlay.tsx` (new)
- `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/smartGuideEngine.ts` (new)

Smart guides appear **temporarily** during element drag/resize operations. They show alignment matches with other elements and the canvas. Colors: magenta/pink — `theme.palette.error.main` is used as the magenta token (configurable via `guideGridStore.smartGuideColorToken`).

```typescript
// smartGuideEngine.ts
export interface SmartGuideMatch {
  type: 'center-h' | 'center-v' | 'edge-top' | 'edge-bottom' | 'edge-left' | 'edge-right'
      | 'equal-spacing-h' | 'equal-spacing-v' | 'equal-size-w' | 'equal-size-h';
  guideLine: { x1: number; y1: number; x2: number; y2: number }; // spans full canvas
  snappedValue: number;  // the position the dragged element snaps to
  // For distance labels:
  distanceLabel?: string; // e.g., "24 px"
  distanceLine?: { x1: number; y1: number; x2: number; y2: number };
}

export const computeSmartGuides = (
  draggedElement: CanvasElement,
  draggedBounds: CanvasBounds, // current (potentially unsnapped) bounds
  otherElements: readonly CanvasElement[],
  canvasSize: { width: number; height: number },
  threshold: number,  // snapThreshold from SmartGuideConfig
  config: SmartGuideConfig,
): { matches: SmartGuideMatch[]; snappedDelta: { dx: number; dy: number } => {
  // 1. Compute alignment candidates (center-h, center-v, all 4 edges) for the dragged element
  // 2. Compare against each other element's same reference points + canvas center
  // 3. For each match within threshold, produce a SmartGuideMatch
  // 4. If equal spacing detected (three elements with equal gaps), add spacing match + label
  // 5. If equal size detected (same w/h within threshold), add size match + label
  // 6. Compute the snapped delta (closest match per axis wins)
};
```

**SmartGuideOverlay** renders all matches as SVG lines (magenta, 1px) and distance labels (small text badges with background). The overlay is cleared (`useCanvasStore.setActiveSmartGuides([])`) when the drag ends.

**Toggle**: `guideGridStore.smartGuides.enabled` (default `true`). Keyboard shortcut `Ctrl+;` toggles guides (Plan 10 §R5 maps this — the same shortcut toggles both user guides and smart guides, per Photoshop convention).

**Sensitivity**: `guideGridStore.smartGuides.snapThreshold` (default 6px) controls the snap distance. Configurable in the Grid/Guide Settings panel (Plan 12 §R5).

### R5-G.12 Snap Engine Integration

**File**: `frontend/common/views/dashboard/cert-canvas/utils/snapEngine.ts` (new — supersedes the simple `snapUtils.ts` from §8.1)

The existing snap logic (§8.1 grid snap, §8.2 element alignment) is extended into a unified snap engine that respects the `SnapConfig` from `guideGridTypes.ts`:

```typescript
export const applySnap = (
  position: { x: number; y: number },
  config: SnapConfig,
  context: {
    gridSize: number;
    guides: readonly GuideLine[];
    elements: readonly CanvasElement[];
    canvasSize: { width: number; height: number };
  },
): { x: number; y: number; snapSource: { x: SnapSource | null; y: SnapSource | null } } => {
  // For each axis, try snap targets in config.priority order:
  // 1. grid: Math.round(pos / gridSize) * gridSize (if snapToGrid)
  // 2. guides: nearest guide line within threshold (if snapToGuides)
  // 3. elements: nearest element edge/center within threshold (if snapToElements)
  // 4. pixels: Math.round(pos) (if snapToPixels)
  // 5. artboard: clamp to [0, canvasSize] (if snapToArtboard)
  // First match per axis wins (priority order).
};

export type SnapSource = 'grid' | 'guides' | 'elements' | 'pixels' | 'artboard' | null;
```

This replaces the inline `Math.round(value / gridSize) * gridSize` from §8.1, which only handled grid snap. The existing `snapToGrid` and `snapToElements` booleans in `useCanvasStore` (§3.3) are migrated to the richer `SnapConfig` in `guideGridStore`. Backward-compatible: `useCanvasStore.snapToGrid` reads from `guideGridStore.snapConfig.snapToGrid`.

