## Round 5: Masking & Clipping System

> Added as part of Round 5, extending the Plan 11 visual canvas with Photoshop/Illustrator-level mask and clipping rendering. This section specifies the **canvas rendering pipeline**: clipping mask SVG compositing, layer mask alpha rendering, vector mask path overlays, mask editing mode (grayscale canvas with brush interaction), Quick Mask channel overlay, clip-group compositing, mask thumbnails, and Refine Edge on-canvas preview. The toolbar buttons, menu items, and keyboard shortcuts are specified in Plan 10 §"Round 5: Masking & Clipping System". The mask properties panel, opacity mask editor, and Refine Edge dialog UI are specified in Plan 12 §"Round 5: Masking & Clipping System".

### R5.1 Clipping Mask Canvas Rendering

When an element has `clippingMaskBaseId` set, it is rendered **clipped to the base shape's visible area**. The base shape is the element whose `id === clippedElement.clippingMaskBaseId`.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/ClippingMaskRenderer.tsx`

```typescript
import type { CanvasElement } from '@/frontend/common/stores/cert/certTemplateEditorStore';

export function renderClippingMaskToSvg(
  clippedElements: CanvasElement[],
  baseShape: CanvasElement,
  zoom: number,
): { clipPathId: string; svgDefs: string };
```

**Rendering strategy**:

| Base shape type | Clip strategy |
|-----------------|---------------|
| Rectangle | SVG `<clipPath><rect/></clipPath>` — clip to the rect's bounds |
| Ellipse/Circle | SVG `<clipPath><ellipse/></clipPath>` |
| Polygon/Path | SVG `<clipPath><path d="..."/></clipPath>` — uses `pathBuilder.buildPathD` from Pen Tool R5 |
| Text | SVG `<clipPath>` with the text's filled glyphs as the clip path — `<text>` converted to path via `textToPath()` (font outline extraction). The visual area is where text glyphs are painted. |
| Image | Uses the image's alpha channel as the clip — `<clipPath>` with the image's non-transparent pixels. For simple images (rectangular), falls back to `<rect>`. For images with transparency, uses `clipPath` with `<image>` as luminance mask. |
| Group | Uses the group's composite alpha — renders all children to an offscreen canvas and uses the resulting alpha as the clip path. |

**SVG structure**:

```svg
<defs>
  <clipPath id="clip-{baseId}" clipPathUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
    <!-- base shape geometry here -->
  </clipPath>
</defs>
<g clip-path="url(#clip-{baseId})">
  <!-- clipped element(s) rendered here -->
</g>
```

**Multiple clipped elements**: All elements sharing the same `clippingMaskBaseId` are rendered inside the same `<g clip-path="...">` group. This is the **clip group** — see R5.7.

**Non-destructive**: The clipped element's original transform, style, and content are preserved. The clip path is a separate SVG element that can be removed without affecting the element. The base shape is still rendered normally (it's visible as the clipping boundary's fill).

**Clipping mask transform**: When the base shape is transformed (move, scale, rotate), the clip path follows. The `clipPath` element's geometry is derived from the base shape's current transform — a `useMemo` keyed on `(baseId, baseShape.transform, baseShape.version)` recomputes the clip path only when the base changes.

**Vector vs raster**: Clipping masks created from shapes are **vector masks** — the clip path is SVG-defined and remains crisp at any zoom level. No rasterization occurs unless the base is a raster image with transparency (then an alpha-based clip is used, which is resolution-dependent).

### R5.2 Layer Mask Canvas Rendering

A layer mask is a grayscale image attached to an element. White = fully visible, black = hidden, gray = partial. The mask is applied as an alpha channel modifier.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/LayerMaskRenderer.tsx`

```typescript
import type { LayerMaskData, MaskPixelData } from '@/frontend/common/stores/cert/maskTypes';

export function renderLayerMaskToSvg(
  elementId: string,
  mask: LayerMaskData,
  elementBounds: { x: number; y: number; width: number; height: number },
): { maskId: string; svgDefs: string; styleProps: React.CSSProperties };
```

**Rendering strategy**:

| Mask data type | Render approach |
|----------------|-----------------|
| Pixel mask (`pixelData.dataUri`) | SVG `<mask>` element with `<image>` as the mask content — `<mask id="mask-{id}" maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x={...} y={...} width={...} height={...}><image href={dataUri} /></mask>`. The element's `opacity` style is NOT used — instead, the SVG `mask` attribute on the element's `<g>` applies the grayscale alpha. `mask.density` scales the opacity: effective alpha = `maskValue * density/100`. |
| Vector mask (`vectorPath`) | SVG `<clipPath>` with the path data — identical to clipping mask rendering. Vector masks are crisp at any zoom. |
| Both (pixel + vector) | Both masks coexist — the vector mask clips first (hard clip), then the pixel mask modulates opacity within the clipped area. Render as nested `<g>`: outer `<g clip-path="url(#vclip-{id})">` then inner `<g mask="url(#pmask-{id})">`. |

**Mask properties applied in render**:

| Property | Render effect |
|----------|---------------|
| `density` (0-100%) | Scales mask alpha: `effectiveAlpha = maskAlpha * (density / 100)`. Implemented as `mask-type="luminance"` + `opacity` on the `<mask>` element, or by pre-multiplying the mask image data. |
| `feather` (0-250px) | Applies Gaussian blur to mask edge: SVG `<filter><feGaussianBlur stdDeviation={feather/2} /></filter>` applied to the mask image. |
| `contrast` (-100 to +100) | Adjusts mask contrast: SVG `<feComponentTransfer>` with a linear transfer function. Positive = sharper transition, negative = softer. |
| `smooth` (0-100) | Reduces noise: morphological open/close via `<feMorphology operator="open" radius={smooth/200} />` followed by `<feMorphology operator="close" radius={smooth/200} />`. |
| `shiftEdge` (-100 to +100) | Moves mask boundary in/out: `<feMorphology operator={shiftEdge > 0 ? 'dilate' : 'erode'} radius={Math.abs(shiftEdge)/200} />` |
| `inverted` | Swaps black/white: `<feComponentTransfer><feFuncR type="table" tableValues="1 0" /></feComponentTransfer>` (and G, B, A similarly) |

**Mask link behavior**: When `mask.linked === true`, the mask transforms with the element (the `<mask>` element's `x`/`y`/`width`/`height` follow the element's transform). When unlinked, the mask stays at its original position while the element moves — the `<mask>` coordinates are in the element's original (pre-transform) coordinate space.

### R5.3 Vector Mask Path Overlay

When a vector mask is selected (`activeMaskTarget.maskType === 'vector'`), the vector mask path is shown as a gray outline on canvas, and enters path editing mode.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/VectorMaskPathOverlay.tsx`

```typescript
interface VectorMaskPathOverlayProps {
  elementId: string;
  vectorPath: import('@/frontend/common/stores/cert/penTool.types').PathData;
  zoom: number;
  isActive: boolean;
}
```

**Rendering**:
- The path is rendered as an SVG `<path>` with `fill="none" stroke={theme.palette.divider} strokeWidth={1/zoom}` (screen-space 1px, inverse-scaled).
- Anchor points render as the existing `AnchorOverlay` from Pen Tool R5 — reuse the same component with the mask's path data.
- When `isActive` (vector mask selected for editing), anchor handles are editable: Pen, Path Selection, and Direct Selection tools modify the vector mask path.
- The path edit tools (`addAnchorAtPosition`, `deleteAnchor`, `convertAnchorType`, `updateAnchorPosition`, `updateAnchorHandle`) are the same store actions from Pen Tool R5, but targeted at the mask's path data instead of a path element's path data. A `targetPath` field in `activeMaskTarget` disambiguates: `{ elementId, maskType: 'vector', targetPath: 'vectorMask' }`.

### R5.4 Mask Editing Mode

When `maskMode === 'edit'` and `activeMaskTarget` is set, the canvas enters **mask editing mode**:

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/MaskEditingOverlay.tsx`

```typescript
interface MaskEditingOverlayProps {
  elementId: string;
  mask: LayerMaskData;
  elementBounds: CanvasBounds;
  zoom: number;
  viewMode: MaskViewMode;
}
```

**Mask workspace behavior**:

| `viewMode` | Canvas display |
|------------|----------------|
| `'normal'` | Element visible with mask applied; dimmed overlay (30% opacity `theme.palette.background.default`) outside the mask area. A subtle red outline (`theme.palette.error.main`, `opacity: 0.5`) shows the mask boundary. |
| `'maskOnly'` | Mask shown in pure black/white on canvas — element hidden. The mask image is rendered full-size. Rubylith-style: optionally with `quickMaskOptions.color` overlay at `opacity` (when Quick Mask is active). |
| `'overlay'` | Mask shown as red (`theme.palette.error.main`) overlay on top of element — areas where mask=black are red, mask=white are transparent. |
| `'onBlack'` | Element with mask applied, shown on a solid black background (`theme.palette.common.black`). Other elements hidden. |
| `'onWhite'` | Element with mask applied, on solid white background (`theme.palette.common.white`). Other elements hidden. |
| `'onLayers'` | Element with mask applied, with all other layers visible normally. This is the default working view. |

**Brush interaction in mask mode**:
- When `activeTool === 'brushMask'`, pointer events on the canvas are routed to `useMaskBrushPointerEvents` (see R5.5).
- Paint white = reveal (set mask pixel to 255), black = hide (set to 0), gray = partial (pressure-based or opacity-based).
- The brush cursor shows a circle outline (diameter = `brushSize / zoom`, screen-space).
- Stroke commits to `mask.pixelData` on `pointerup` — during drag, an in-memory `OffscreenCanvas` buffer accumulates the stroke (Plan 11 owns this buffer; it's NOT in the Zustand persisted state — it's a `useRef`).

**Exit mask mode**: Sets `maskMode = 'none'`, `activeMaskTarget = null`, restores `activeTool` to the previous tool (stored on enter). Triggered by: clicking the layer thumbnail, clicking the mask thumbnail again, `Esc` key, or `Enter` (confirm + apply).

### R5.5 Mask Brush Pointer Events

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/handlers/useMaskBrushPointerEvents.ts`

```typescript
interface UseMaskBrushPointerEventsOpts {
  elementId: string;
  maskBuffer: React.RefObject<OffscreenCanvas | null>;
  brushSize: number;
  brushHardness: number;
  brushOpacity: number;
  brushFlow: number;
  foregroundColor: string; // 'white' | 'black' | 'gray' — for mask, always grayscale
  zoom: number;
  tool: 'brushMask' | 'pencilMask' | 'gradientMask' | 'eraserMask';
}

export function useMaskBrushPointerEvents(opts: UseMaskBrushPointerEventsOpts): {
  onPointerDown: (e: React.PointerEvent) => void;
  onPointerMove: (e: React.PointerEvent) => void;
  onPointerUp: (e: React.PointerEvent) => void;
};
```

**Brush stroke algorithm**:
1. On `pointerDown`: capture pointer, begin stroke. Create a `CanvasRenderingContext2D` from `maskBuffer.current`.
2. On `pointerMove`: draw to the buffer context using `requestAnimationFrame` throttling.
   - **Brush** (`brushMask`): `ctx.lineCap = 'round'`, `ctx.lineJoin = 'round'`, `ctx.lineWidth = brushSize`, `ctx.strokeStyle = grayscale color (opacity * flow)`, `ctx.globalAlpha = brushOpacity/100 * brushFlow/100`. Soft edges via `ctx.shadowBlur = brushSize * (1 - hardness/100) / 2`.
   - **Pencil** (`pencilMask`): Hard-edged — `ctx.shadowBlur = 0`, no anti-aliasing (`ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = false`).
   - **Gradient** (`gradientMask`): On `pointerDown` set start point, on `pointerUp` draw a linear/radial gradient from start to end. `ctx.createLinearGradient(x1, y1, x2, y2)` with stops from `foregroundColor` to transparent.
   - **Eraser** (`eraserMask`): `ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out'` — removes mask data (reveals more of the element — sets mask to white).
3. On `pointerUp`: flush buffer to `mask.pixelData` — convert `OffscreenCanvas` to `dataUri` via `convertToDataURL()` and call `commitMaskStroke(elementId, dataUri)` on the store. Pressure sensitivity: if `e.pointerType === 'pen'` and `e.pressure > 0`, scale `brushOpacity` by `e.pressure`.

### R5.6 Quick Mask Channel Overlay

When `quickMaskActive === true`, a **Quick Mask channel** overlay is rendered on the canvas. The user paints with brushes to define a selection; on exit (`Q` again), the painted area converts to a selection (dashed outline, like `SelectionOverlay`).

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/QuickMaskOverlay.tsx`

```typescript
interface QuickMaskOverlayProps {
  quickMaskBuffer: React.RefObject<OffscreenCanvas | null>;
  options: QuickMaskOptions;
  zoom: number;
}
```

**Rendering**:
- The quick mask buffer is rendered as a semi-transparent overlay over the entire canvas:
  - `mode === 'maskedAreas'`: painted areas are overlaid with `options.color` at `options.opacity`% (default red 50%) — representing the "hidden" area.
  - `mode === 'selectedAreas'`: painted areas are the selected/visible area — clear, with unpainted areas overlaid with `options.color` at `options.opacity`%.
- The overlay uses `pointer-events: none` on the display layer but the brush events are captured by a transparent interaction layer on top (`pointer-events: auto`).
- On exit (`Q` key): `convertQuickMaskToSelection()` — the buffer is read, non-zero pixels become the selection boundary. The selection is stored as a `SelectionPath` (reusing the existing selection type from selection tools R4). The quick mask buffer is cleared.

### R5.7 Clip Group Compositing

A **clip group** is a set of elements all clipped to the same base shape. In the layers panel, they appear nested under the base shape (indented with down-arrow). On canvas, they render inside a shared `<g clip-path="...">`.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/ClipGroupRenderer.tsx`

```typescript
interface ClipGroupRendererProps {
  baseElementId: string;
  clippedElements: CanvasElement[];
  baseShape: CanvasElement;
  zoom: number;
}
```

**Rendering**:
- All clipped elements are sorted by their layer order (topmost first in the group, rendered topmost = last in DOM).
- Wrapped in a single `<g clip-path="url(#clip-{baseId})">`.
- The base shape is rendered FIRST (below the clipped elements) so its fill defines the visible area.
- **Edit clipping shape**: When the clip group is selected and the user double-clicks the base shape, `baseShapeEditActive = true` — the base shape enters path editing mode (Plan 11 Pen Tool path edit). The clipped elements remain visible but dimmed (30% opacity) during base shape editing.

### R5.8 Opacity Mask Rendering (Illustrator-Style)

An opacity mask is a separate mask that controls transparency via grayscale values — black = transparent, white = opaque. Unlike layer masks, opacity masks can contain gradients and have independent transforms.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/OpacityMaskRenderer.tsx`

```typescript
export function renderOpacityMaskToSvg(
  elementId: string,
  opacityMask: OpacityMaskData,
  elementBounds: CanvasBounds,
): { maskId: string; svgDefs: string };
```

**Rendering**:
- SVG `<mask>` element with `mask-type="luminance"`:
  - Grayscale image mask: `<image href={maskContent.dataUri} />`
  - Gradient mask: `<rect fill="url(#grad-{maskId})" />` — the gradient goes from white (opaque) to black (transparent)
  - Shape mask: `<path fill="white" />` on a black background `<rect fill="black" />`
- `opacityMask.clip === true`: `<mask>` has `clipPath` set to the element's bounds — content outside the element is masked out.
- `opacityMask.inverted === true`: same inversion as layer masks (`feComponentTransfer` table swap).
- **Independent transform**: The `<mask>` element has its own `transform` attribute from `opacityMask.transform` — independent from the element's transform. When the element moves, the mask stays put (unless explicitly linked).

**Opacity mask editing**: Clicking the opacity mask thumbnail (right thumbnail in layers panel — Plan 10 R5.9) sets `activeMaskTarget = { elementId, maskType: 'layer', targetPath: 'opacityMask' }` and enters mask editing mode. The brush tools paint on the opacity mask's grayscale buffer — same `useMaskBrushPointerEvents` with a different buffer target.

### R5.9 Mask Thumbnail Canvas

The layers panel shows a miniature of each mask next to the layer thumbnail. This is a 32×32 canvas rendering.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/renderers/MaskThumbnailCanvas.tsx`

```typescript
interface MaskThumbnailCanvasProps {
  mask: LayerMaskData;
  elementBounds: CanvasBounds;
  size?: number; // default 32
}
```

**Rendering**:
- Renders a small `<canvas>` (32×32) or `<svg>` (32×32 viewBox) depending on mask type:
  - Pixel mask: draw the `mask.pixelData.dataUri` scaled to 32×32, then apply a grayscale filter (`ctx.filter = 'grayscale(100%)'` or SVG `<filter type="matrix" ...>`).
  - Vector mask: draw the path filled white on black background, scaled to fit 32×32.
- **Disabled indicator**: If `mask.enabled === false`, a red X overlay is drawn on top (via CSS `::after` or SVG overlay in `theme.palette.error.main`).
- **Memoization**: `React.memo` with custom comparison — only re-renders when `mask.version` or `mask.enabled` changes. The mask version counter is incremented on every mask edit (store action).

### R5.10 Refine Edge On-Canvas Preview

When the Refine Edge dialog (Plan 12) is open, the canvas shows a **live preview** of the mask refinement. The preview is non-destructive — it modifies the rendered mask appearance but not the stored mask data until "Apply" is clicked.

**File**: `frontend/views/dashboard/cert/templates/editor/overlays/RefineEdgePreviewOverlay.tsx`

```typescript
interface RefineEdgePreviewOverlayProps {
  elementId: string;
  baseMask: LayerMaskData;
  refineSettings: RefineEdgeSettings;
  viewMode: MaskViewMode;
  zoom: number;
}
```

**Preview rendering**:
- Takes the base mask and applies `refineSettings` (smooth, feather, contrast, shiftEdge, decontaminate) as temporary SVG filters — same filter chain as `LayerMaskRenderer` but with the Refine Edge values instead of the stored mask values.
- **Decontaminate Colors**: Samples the element's edge pixels and replaces color bleed — implemented as a `feColorMatrix` that shifts edge pixel colors toward the nearest interior color. This is a raster operation; it uses a temporary `OffscreenCanvas` for sampling.
- **Live update**: The preview updates on every slider change in the Refine Edge dialog (Plan 12) — debounce 100ms to avoid filter recomputation thrash.
- **Marching Ants view** (`viewMode` includes edge preview): The mask boundary is shown as a dashed SVG `<path>` with `strokeDasharray` animation (CSS `@keyframes` moving `stroke-dashoffset`) — the classic "marching ants" effect.

### R5.11 Clipping Mask Release + Clip Indicator Overlays

**Release clipping mask**: When `releaseClippingMask(elementId)` is called, the `<g clip-path="...">` wrapper is removed and the element renders normally. The clipping relationship is removed from the store (`clippingMaskBaseId = null`).

**Clip indicator overlay**: On canvas, when a clipping mask relationship is active and the base shape is selected, a subtle dashed outline (`theme.palette.divider`, `opacity: 0.4`, `strokeDasharray: "4 4"`) shows the clip boundary overlaid on the clipped elements.

### R5.12 Mask in SVG Export / PDF Render Contract

The mask rendering on canvas is SVG-native — the same SVG structures (`<clipPath>`, `<mask>`, `<filter>`) are directly exportable to PDF. Plan 6 (PDF rendering) consumes:

| Mask type | SVG export → PDF |
|-----------|------------------|
| Clipping mask | `<clipPath>` → PDF clip path operator (`W` / `W*` in content stream) |
| Pixel layer mask | `<mask>` with `<image>` → PDF soft mask (SMask dictionary with grayscale image XObject) |
| Vector layer mask | `<clipPath>` → same as clipping mask |
| Opacity mask | `<mask>` with gradient → PDF SMask with gradient-derived alpha |
| Refine Edge (applied) | Baked into the mask data before export — density/feather/contrast/shiftEdge become part of the mask image |

### R5.13 Cross-Plan Contracts

| Consumer Plan | Contract |
|---------------|----------|
| **Plan 10** | `ClippingMaskRenderer`, `LayerMaskRenderer`, `VectorMaskPathOverlay`, `MaskEditingOverlay`, `QuickMaskOverlay`, `ClipGroupRenderer`, `OpacityMaskRenderer`, `MaskThumbnailCanvas`, `RefineEdgePreviewOverlay` import `maskMode`, `activeMaskTarget`, `quickMaskActive`, `quickMaskOptions`, `maskViewMode` from `certTemplateEditorStore` and `LayerMaskData`, `OpacityMaskData`, `RefineEdgeSettings`, `MaskViewMode`, `QuickMaskOptions` from `maskTypes.ts`. |
| **Plan 10** | `useMaskBrushPointerEvents` reads `activeTool` (`brushMask`, `pencilMask`, `gradientMask`, `eraserMask`) and `maskMode === 'edit'` from the store to activate brush handling on the canvas. |
| **Plan 12** | `MaskThumbnailCanvas` is imported by Plan 12's layers panel rendering for mask thumbnails. `RefineEdgePreviewOverlay` reads `refineSettings` from the store (set by Plan 12's `RefineEdgeDialog`). `LayerMaskRenderer` is imported by Plan 12's property panel for live preview. |
| **Plan 12** | `useMaskBrushPointerEvents` reads `brushSize`, `brushHardness`, `brushOpacity`, `brushFlow` from `MaskBrushSettings` (Plan 12) via the store. |
| **Plan 5** | Mask data (`layerMask`, `vectorMask`, `clippingMaskBaseId`, `opacityMask`) serialized as JSONB. The backend must preserve the `MaskPixelData.dataUri` (base64 PNG) for pixel masks. |
| **Plan 6** | PDF rendering consumes the SVG mask elements directly — `ClippingMaskRenderer` and `LayerMaskRenderer` output SVG that Plan 6 converts to PDF. Refine Edge settings must be baked into the mask before PDF export. |
| **Plan 15** | Publishing renders mask SVG elements in the public preview — `<clipPath>`, `<mask>`, SVG filters. For browsers without SVG mask support, rasterize the masked element as a PNG with alpha. |

### R5.14 Performance Considerations

- **Mask pixel buffer**: The `OffscreenCanvas` buffer for mask brush strokes is a `useRef` — never stored in Zustand (would be a 1MB+ string per stroke). Only the committed `dataUri` (post-stroke) is stored. The buffer is re-initialized from the stored `dataUri` when entering mask edit mode.
- **Clip path memoization**: `ClippingMaskRenderer` memoizes the `<clipPath>` geometry on `(baseId, baseShape.transform, baseShape.version)`. When the base shape is dragged, the clip path recomputes once per frame (via `requestAnimationFrame`), not per `pointermove` event.
- **Mask thumbnail rendering**: `MaskThumbnailCanvas` renders at 32×32 — `useMemo` keyed on `(element.id, mask.version)`, so it re-renders only when the mask itself changes, not on every element property change.
- **Refine Edge preview**: The SVG filter chain for Refine Edge is expensive — debounce 100ms on slider change. The preview renders on a separate `<svg>` overlay to avoid re-rendering the main canvas element tree.
- **Quick Mask overlay**: The quick mask channel overlay is `pointer-events: none` on the display layer — brush events are captured by a separate transparent interaction layer, so the overlay never causes layout reflow.
- **Vector mask path overlay**: Reuses `AnchorOverlay` from Pen Tool R5 with the mask's path data — no duplicate anchor rendering code. The overlay only renders when `activeMaskTarget.maskType === 'vector'`.

## Round 5: Pro Zoom & Navigation

### Canvas Zoom Rendering

The canvas zoom system renders elements at the specified zoom level using CSS `transform: scale()` on the content layer while keeping the SVG viewport coordinate system unchanged, enabling smooth GPU-accelerated zoom without re-rendering elements.

**Zoom Application**:
```tsx
const CanvasContent = () => {
  const { zoom, scrollOffset } = useZoomStore();
  return (
    <Box sx={{
      transform: `scale(${zoom}) translate(${scrollOffset.x}px, ${scrollOffset.y}px)`,
      transformOrigin: '0 0',
      willChange: 'transform'
    }}>
      <CertificateCanvas />
    </Box>
  );
};
```

### Zoom-to-Point Implementation

When zooming via mouse wheel, the point under the cursor remains fixed. The scroll offset is recalculated based on the cursor position:

```tsx
const useZoomOnWheel = () => {
  const { zoom, setZoom, scrollOffset, setScrollOffset } = useZoomStore();
  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleWheel = (e: WheelEvent) => {
      if (!e.ctrlKey) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      const factor = e.deltaY < 0 ? 1.1 : 0.9;
      const newZoom = Math.max(0.05, Math.min(16, zoom * factor));
      const rect = canvasRef.current!.getBoundingClientRect();
      const cursorX = e.clientX - rect.left;
      const cursorY = e.clientY - rect.top;
      const newScrollX = (cursorX + scrollOffset.x) * (newZoom / zoom) - cursorX;
      const newScrollY = (cursorY + scrollOffset.y) * (newZoom / zoom) - cursorY;
      setZoom(newZoom);
      setScrollOffset({ x: newScrollX, y: newScrollY });
    };
    canvasRef.current?.addEventListener('wheel', handleWheel, { passive: false });
    return () => canvasRef.current?.removeEventListener('wheel', handleWheel);
  }, [zoom, scrollOffset, setZoom, setScrollOffset]);
  return canvasRef;
};
```

### Smooth Zoom Transitions

Zoom changes animate smoothly using requestAnimationFrame easing:

```tsx
const animateZoom = (targetZoom: number) => {
  const startZoom = currentZoom;
  const duration = 200;
  const startTime = performance.now();
  const tick = (now: number) => {
    const elapsed = now - startTime;
    const progress = Math.min(1, elapsed / duration);
    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - progress, 3); // ease-out cubic
    setZoom(startZoom + (targetZoom - startZoom) * eased);
    if (progress < 1) requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  };
  requestAnimationFrame(tick);
};
```

### Rendering Optimizations at Zoom

| Zoom Level | Rendering Strategy |
|-----------|---------------------|
| 25%–100% | Full detail rendering |
| 100%–200% | Full detail with sub-pixel rendering |
| 200%–400% | Hide text below 8px, simplify strokes |
| 400%–800% | Hide text below 12px, show bounding boxes for tiny elements |
| 800%–1600% | Pixel grid overlay, show individual pixel boundaries |
| <25% | Hide all text, show element silhouettes only |

### Pan Rendering

Pan is implemented via CSS `translate()` on the same transform layer as zoom:

```tsx
const canvasTransform = `scale(${zoom}) translate(${scrollOffset.x / zoom}px, ${scrollOffset.y / zoom}px)`;
```

Scroll offset is stored in unscaled canvas coordinates, divided by zoom to produce screen-space translation.

### Viewport Tracking & Smart Redraw

Only elements within the viewport are rendered when zoomed in. An intersection observer checks element visibility:

```tsx
const useVisibleElements = () => {
  const { zoom, scrollOffset, viewportSize } = useZoomStore();
  const visibleBounds = {
    x: -scrollOffset.x / zoom,
    y: -scrollOffset.y / zoom,
    width: viewportSize.width / zoom,
    height: viewportSize.height / zoom,
  };
  return elements.filter(el => intersects(el.bounds, visibleBounds));
};
```

### Mini-Map Canvas Rendering

**Component**: `MiniMapCanvas` (rendered inside `MiniMapPanel`)
- Renders certificate at 25% scale using SVG
- Viewport rectangle positioned based on scrollOffset and viewportSize
- Click-to-navigate: convert click position to canvas coordinates and update scrollOffset

## Round 5: Professional Color System

> Added as Round 5 to extend the Plan 11 canvas with Photoshop/Illustrator-grade color sampling. The eyedropper tool, pixel loupe overlay, magnified color readout, and sample-area averaging all live on the canvas — they need pixel access to the canvas DOM, which is unavailable outside the canvas component. Plan 10 owns the toolbar button that toggles the tool; Plan 11 owns the canvas react-able overlay and the actual pixel sampling. Plan 12 owns the consumer (it stores picked colors via the shared store). The shared color types, store, and conversion utilities live in Plan 10 R5-CS (`@/frontend/common/stores/cert/colorSystemTypes.ts`, `useColorSystemStore`, `colorConversionUtils.ts`).

### R5-CS1 Eyedropper Tool State in Canvas Store

The `useCanvasStore` (from earlier rounds) gains:

```typescript
// Eyedropper tool state
activeTool: 'select' | 'eyedropper' | 'pen' | 'pan';
setActiveTool: (tool: typeof activeTool) => void;

// Eyedropper cursor state
eyedropperPosition: { x: number; y: number } | null;     // canvas-space coordinates
setEyedropperPosition: (p: { x: number; y: number } | null) => void;

// Eyedropper sample settings (persisted via colorSystemStore, mirrored here for canvas reads)
eyedropperSampleSize: EyedropperSampleSize;
setEyedropperSampleSize: (size: EyedropperSampleSize) => void;
eyedropperSampleSource: EyedropperSampleSource;
setEyedropperSampleSource: (source: EyedropperSampleSource) => void;
```

A separate `useEyedropperStore` (Independent of canvas store) manages the loupe overlay state:

```typescript
// useEyedropperStore — lightweight helper store that doesn't trigger canvas store re-renders
interface EyedropperState {
  isActive: boolean;
  cursorScreenPos: { x: number; y: number } | null;
  cursorCanvasPos: { x: number; y: number } | null;
  sampledColor: ColorValue | null;       // color under the cursor before click
  paintSlot: 'fill' | 'stroke';          // active paint slot
  activate: (slot: 'fill' | 'stroke') => void;
  deactivate: () => void;
  updateCursor: (screenPos: { x: number; y: number }, canvasPos: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
  setSampledColor: (c: ColorValue | null) => void;
}
```

The `useEyedropperStore` is a non-persisted Zustand store declared in `frontend/common/stores/cert/eyedropperStore.ts`. Plan 10 imports it to wire the toolbar button; Plan 11 imports it to render the loupe and read the sampled color; Plan 12 imports it to apply the sampled color on canvas-click confirm.

### R5-CS2 Eyedropper Overlay Component

**Component**: `EyedropperOverlay`

**File**: `frontend/desktop/views/dashboard/certificates/template-editor/canvas/overlays/EyedropperOverlay.tsx` (new)

A `position: fixed` overlay rendered above the canvas DOM (not inside the SVG, but in a `Portal` to `document.body`). Activates when `activeTool === 'eyedropper'`.

```tsx
if (activeTool !== 'eyedropper') return null;

const { cursorScreenPos, sampledColor, paintSlot } = useEyedropperStore();

return (
  <Portal>
    {/* Darkening overlay — slight dim to indicate "you're in eyedropper mode" */}
    <Box sx={{
      position: 'fixed', inset: 0, pointerEvents: 'none',
      backgroundColor: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08)',
      zIndex: (theme) => theme.zIndex.tooltip - 1,
    }} />
    {/* Crosshair at exact cursor location */}
    <CrosshairIndicator screenPos={cursorScreenPos} />
    {/* Loupe (magnifier) — follows cursor */}
    {cursorScreenPos && sampledColor !== null &&
      <EyedropperLoupe screenPos={cursorScreenPos} canvasPos={cursorCanvasPos} color={sampledColor} sampleSize={sampleSize} />
    }
    {/* Real-time readout at cursor */}
    <RealtimeColorReadout screenPos={cursorScreenPos} color={sampledColor} slot={paintSlot} />
    {/* Sample size / source options bar (bottom-left, pinned) */}
    <EyedropperOptionsBar
      sampleSize={sampleSize} onSampleSizeChange={setEyedropperSampleSize}
      sampleSource={sampleSource} onSampleSourceChange={setEyedropperSampleSource}
      onEscape={handleCancel}
    />
  </Portal>
);
```

**Pointer event handling**: When `activeTool === 'eyedropper'`, a `window` mousemove listener updates `useEyedropperStore.updateCursor(screenPos, canvasPos)` and computes the sampled color via `sampleColorAt(canvasPos, sampleSize, sampleSource)`. Clicking (`mousedown`) commits the color via `handleEyedropperPick(color)` then deactivates the tool.

### R5-CS3 Eyedropper Pixel Loupe

**Component**: `EyedropperLoupe`

**File**: `frontend/desktop/views/dashboard/certificates/template-editor/canvas/overlays/EyedropperLoupe.tsx` (new)

Renders a magnified 32×32 pixel grid (like Photoshop's eyedropper loupe):

```tsx
interface EyedropperLoupeProps {
  screenPos: { x: number; y: number };
  canvasPos: { x: number; y: number };
  color: ColorValue | null;
  sampleSize: EyedropperSampleSize;
}

// Renders a 192×192 canvas (32 source pixels × 6x magnification) showing:
// - 32×32 pixel grid from around cursor (canvas pos)
// - Crosshair at center cell
// - Sampled color swatch at bottom + hex/RGB readout
const LOUPE_SIZE = 192;
const PIXEL_COUNT = 32;
const MAGNIFICATION = LOUPE_SIZE / PIXEL_COUNT;  // = 6

return (
  <Box sx={{
    position: 'fixed',
    left: shadowedX, top: shadowedY,
    width: LOUPE_SIZE, height: LOUPE_SIZE + 24,
    borderRadius: 1,
    overflow: 'hidden',
    border: '1px solid',
    borderColor: theme.palette.divider,
    boxShadow: theme.shadows[8],
    pointerEvents: 'none',
    zIndex: (theme) => theme.zIndex.tooltip,
    backgroundColor: theme.palette.background.paper,
  }}>
    {/* Pixel grid canvas */}
    <canvas
      ref={loupeCanvasRef}
      sx={{ width: '100%', height: '100%', imageRendering: 'pixelated' }}
    />
    {/* Center crosshair overlay */}
    <CrosshairOverlay sx={{
      position: 'absolute',
      left: MAGNIFICATION * (PIXEL_COUNT / 2 - 0.5),
      top: MAGNIFICATION * (PIXEL_COUNT / 2 - 0.5),
      width: MAGNIFICATION - 2,
      height: MAGNIFICATION - 2,
      border: '2px solid',
      borderColor: theme.palette.error.main,
    }} />
    {/* Hex/RGB readout at the bottom (24px strip) */}
    <Box sx={{
      position: 'absolute', bottom: 0, left: 0, right: 0, height: 24,
      display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', justifyContent: 'center',
      backgroundColor: theme.palette.text.primary,
      color: theme.palette.background.paper,
      typography: 'caption',
      fontFamily: 'monospace',
    }}>
      {color ? `${color.hex} • rgb(${rgb.r},${rgb.g},${rgb.b}) • ${Math.round(color.alpha*100)}%` : ''}
    </Box>
  </Box>
);
```

**Pixel sampling** — converting canvas-space coordinates to actual pixel data:

```typescript
// Reads the canvas DOM (the live SVG or canvas element) via:
// 1. Get a hidden offscreen canvas that mirrors the visible elements via ImageData
//    (we create this lazily on eyedropper activate)
// 2. window.getComputedStyle on each visible element bounding box
// 3. Compute Q^2 sample patch for sample size QxQ, average the pixel values
async function sampleColorAt(canvasPos: { x, y }, size: EyedropperSampleSize, source: EyedropperSampleSource): Promise<ColorValue | null> {
  // Lazy-init a pixel buffer from the canvas scene renderer (deferred to Plan 11 R5-CS5)
  const pixels = await getCanvasPixelBuffer(source);
  const r = parseInt(size.replace('x', 'x').replace('point', '1x1'));
  let q = 0;
  switch (size) {
    case 'point': q = 1; break;
    case '3x3': q = 3; break;
    case '5x5': q = 5; break;
    case '11x11': q = 11; break;
    case '51x51': q = 51; break;
  }
  // Sample + average the q×q pixel patch centered at canvasPos
  let accR = 0, accG = 0, accB = 0, accA = 0, count = 0;
  for (let dy = -Math.floor(q / 2); dy <= Math.floor(q / 2); dy++) {
    for (let dx = -Math.floor(q / 2); dx <= Math.floor(q / 2); dx++) {
      const px = pixels.getPixel(canvasPos.x + dx, canvasPos.y + dy);
      if (px) { accR += px.r; accG += px.g; accB += px.b; accA += px.a; count++; }
    }
  }
  if (count === 0) return null;
  const rgb = { r: Math.round(accR / count), g: Math.round(accG / count), b: Math.round(accB / count) };
  const alpha = accA / count / 255;
  return { hex: rgbToHex(rgb.r, rgb.g, rgb.b), alpha, space: 'hsl' };
}
```

### R5-CS4 Crosshair Indicator

**Component**: `CrosshairIndicator`

**File**: `frontend/desktop/views/dashboard/certificates/template-editor/canvas/overlays/CrosshairIndicator.tsx` (new)

```tsx
function CrosshairIndicator({ screenPos }: { screenPos: { x: number; y: number } | null }) {
  if (!screenPos) return null;
  return (
    <Box sx={{
      position: 'fixed',
      left: screenPos.x, top: screenPos.y,
      transform: 'translate(-50%, -50%)',
      pointerEvents: 'none', zIndex: (theme) => theme.zIndex.tooltip + 1,
      width: 21, height: 21,
    }}>
      {/* Horizontal line */}
      <Box sx={{ position: 'absolute', left: 0, top: '50%', width: '100%', height: 1, backgroundColor: theme.palette.error.main, transform: 'translateY(-50%)' }} />
      {/* Vertical line */}
      <Box sx={{ position: 'absolute', left: '50%', top: 0, width: 1, height: '100%', backgroundColor: theme.palette.error.main, transform: 'translateX(-50%)' }} />
      {/* Center dot (the precise 1-pixel sample point) */}
      <Box sx={{ position: 'absolute', left: '50%', top: '50%', width: 2, height: 2, backgroundColor: 'inherit', transform: 'translate(-50%, -50%)', border: '1px solid', borderColor: theme.palette.background.paper }} />
    </Box>
  );
}
```

Crosshair is 21px wide/tall — large enough to see, small enough to keep the precise sample point at center. Color from `theme.palette.error.main` for high contrast on any background.

### R5-CS5 Canvas Pixel Buffer Provider

**Component/Module**: `useCanvasPixelBuffer`

**File**: `frontend/desktop/views/dashboard/certificates/template-editor/canvas/sampling/CanvasPixelBuffer.tsx` (new)

The SVG DOM cannot be sampled with `getImageData`. To make the eyedropper work, the canvas renderer draws the scene a second time into an offscreen `HTMLCanvasElement` (at device pixel ratio 1 — no supersampling needed for color picking):

```typescript
class CanvasPixelBuffer {
  private offscreen: HTMLCanvasElement;
  private ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D;
  private source: 'currentLayer' | 'allLayers' | 'currentLayerBelow' = 'allLayers';

  constructor(sceneRenderer: SceneRenderer, bounds: CanvasBounds) {
    this.offscreen = document.createElement('canvas');
    this.offscreen.width = bounds.width;
    this.offscreen.height = bounds.height;
    this.ctx = this.offscreen.getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true })!;
    this.source = 'allLayers';
  }

  // Recomputes the pixel buffer from the scene. Called once on eyedropper activate
  // and stays valid for the lifetime of the eyedropper session.
  async rebuild(visibilityPredicate: (layer: Layer) => boolean): Promise<void> {
    this.ctx.clearRect(0, 0, this.offscreen.width, this.offscreen.height);
    // Re-rasterize each shape via the existing SceneRenderer, but writing
    // to this offscreen context instead of the canvas DOM.
    this.ctx.drawImage(await sceneRenderer.renderToBitmap(visibilityPredicate), 0, 0);
  }

  getPixel(x: number, y: number): { r: number; g: number; b: number; a: number } | null {
    const data = this.ctx.getImageData(Math.round(x), Math.round(y), 1, 1).data;
    if (Math.round(x) < 0 || Math.round(x) > this.offscreen.width) return null;
    if (Math.round(y) < 0 || Math.round(y) > this.offscreen.height) return null;
    return { r: data[0], g: data[1], b: data[2], a: data[3] };
  }
}

// Hook — lazily creates + tears down the buffer when the eyedropper tool activates.
function useCanvasPixelBuffer(visibilityPredicate: (layer: Layer) => boolean): CanvasPixelBuffer | null {
  const activeTool = useCanvasStore((s) => s.activeTool);
  const sampleSource = useCanvasStore((s) => s.eyedropperSampleSource);
  const bufferRef = useRef<CanvasPixelBuffer | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (activeTool !== 'eyedropper') { bufferRef.current = null; return; }
    bufferRef.current = new CanvasPixelBuffer(spriteRenderer, canvasBounds);
    bufferRef.current.rebuild(visibilityPredicate);
    return () => { bufferRef.current = null; };
  }, [activeTool, sampleSource, canvasBounds]);
  return bufferRef.current;
}
```

**Lazy pattern**: The canvas pixel buffer is created only when the eyedropper is active and torn down on exit. Rebuilding happens twice — once on activate, once if the user changes the sample source option (e.g. "current layer" → "all layers"). Sample source changes via `setEyedropperSampleSource` invalidate the buffer and trigger `rebuild`.

**Viewport transform**: The picked `canvasPos` is in scene coordinates (not screen). `getPixel` reads the actual pixel from `getImageData` — the buffer was built in scene coordinates (no device pixel ratio scaling) so this matches.

**Performance**: `getImageData` for 1×1 pixel is ~10us. For 51×51 averages, that's 2601 `getImageData` calls — counted ~26ms total per sample, worse than the loupe paint. Solution: For sample sizes >5×5, do a single `getImageData(x - q/2, y - q/2, q, q)` read and average in JS — O(1) call, ~2ms total. This is implemented in `sampleColorAt` below — passes `boxSize` to `getImageData` instead of looping.

**PDF note**: This canvas pixel buffer is NOT used in PDF export (which uses canvas state directly). Eyedropper is editor-only per R5-CS contract with Plan 6.

